1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CASTILE.htm#Felipedied1274 Medlands]
2 CONT
2 CONT Felipe de Castilla
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Felipe_de_Castilla De Wikipedia, la enciclopedia libre]
2 CONT
2 CONT No confundir con Felipe I de Castilla.
2 CONT
2 CONT Felipe de Castilla (1231 - 28 de noviembre de 1274). Infante de Castilla, fue hijo de Fernando III de Castilla y de su primera esposa, la reina Beatriz de Suabia. Señor de Valdecorneja, ciertas fuentes aseguran que ingresó como ca
2 CONC ballero en la Orden del Temple,1​en uno de cuyos templos, la Iglesia de Santa María la Blanca de Villalcázar de Sirga, recibió sepultura el infante, en un sepulcro adornado con emblemas de la Orden del Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arzobispo electo de Sevilla, fue además abad de la Colegiata de Valladolid y de la Colegiata de San Cosme y San Damián de Covarrubias hasta el año 1258, en que abandonó la carrera eclesiástica con el consentimiento de su hermano, 
2 CONC Alfonso X el Sabio, a pesar de la oposición de este, y contrajo matrimonio con la princesa Cristina de Noruega, hija del rey Haakon IV de Noruega.2​ Felipe de Castilla (1231-1274). Infante de Castilla, fue el séptimo hijo de Ferna
2 CONC ndo III el Santo y Beatriz de Suabia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Su nombre procede de Alemania, pues le fue impuesto en atención a su abuelo materno, el emperador Felipe de Suabia, y fue el primer miembro de la realeza castellana en portar este nombre de etimología griega.
2 CONT
2 CONT Por voluntad paterna, nació encaminado al estado eclesiástico. Fue canónigo de las catedrales de Burgos y Toledo, abad de la Colegiata de Valladolid, abad de la Colegiata de San Cosme y San Damián de Covarrubias hasta los 21 años,
2 CONC  siendo nombrado como primer arzobispo de Sevilla en 1251 por el Papa Inocencio IV, tras la reconquista de la ciudad por su padre Fernando III. Sin embargo, en 1257 renunció a la carrera eclesiástica debido a su compromiso matrimo
2 CONC nial con la princesa Cristina de Noruega. Su vacante en el arzobispado de Sevilla fue cubierta en mayo de 1259 por don Raimundo de Losana.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aunque el matrimonio se celebró el 31 de marzo de 1258 en la Colegiata de Valladolid, la pareja se estableció en Sevilla, hasta la muerte de la princesa Cristina (1262).
2 CONT
2 CONT __________________________
2 CONT *'''Philip of Castile (Spanish: Felipe de Castilla y Suabia; 1231 – 28 November 1274) was an Infante of Castile and son''' of Ferdinand III, King of Castile and León, and his first queen, Beatrice of Swabia. '''He was Lord of Vald
2 CONC ecorneja, and, according to some sources, Knight of the Order of the Temple,[1] in one of those churches, the Church of Santa María la Blanca in Villalcázar de Sirga, he was buried in a coffin adorned with emblems of the Templars.
2 CONT *'''An archbishop-elect of Seville, he was also abbot of the Collegiate church of Santa María la Mayor in Valladolid and of the Collegiate Church of Saints Cosme and Damian in Covarrubias until 1258, when he left his ecclesiastica
2 CONC l career with the consent of his brother''', King Alfonso X, despite the latter's opposition, '''and married Christina of Norway''', daughter of King Haakon IV of Norway.[2]
2 CONT *'''Infante Philip of Castile was born in 1231 as the son''' of Ferdinand III and Beatrice of Swabia. '''He was named after his maternal grandfather''', Philip of Swabia, King of Germany and Duke of Swabia. '''From childhood he wa
2 CONC s groomed by his father''', King Ferdinand III, '''for an ecclesiastical career, along with his brother''' Infante Sancho, who would later become Archbishop of Toledo.[3] '''During his youth, after his upbringing was entrusted by 
2 CONC his grandmother''', Queen Berengaria, to Rodrigo Jiménez de Rada, who would also become Archbishop of Toledo, '''he was sent to Paris for his studies, receiving lessons from Albertus Magnus, the teacher of Thomas Aquinas.[4] In 12
2 CONC 49 Infante Philip was named Procurator of the Archdiocese of Seville by Pope Innocent IV.[5] Two years later, in 1251, he was named archbishop-elect of Seville by the same pope. The archdiocese continued to be administered by Raim
2 CONC undo de Losana, Bishop of Segovia, as the infante had not yet been consecrated a bishop.[5]
2 CONT *'''In 1258, shortly after his brother the king, in spite of his initial opposition, authorized him to leave his ecclesiastical career, Infante Philip married Christina of Norway''', daughter of King Haakon IV. '''A legend held in
2 CONC  the past that Christina came to Spain after Alfonso X asked Haakon to send one of his daughters to marry, due to the infertility of his wife, Queen Violant, daughter of King James I of Aragon. Nonetheless, the falsehood of the le
2 CONC gend is demonstrated by the fact that Queen Violant had already given birth to several children by 1258.[3] In fact, Haakon sent Christina to Castile to marry one of the king's brothers. On her way to Castile, the princess passed 
2 CONC through France and Aragon, where she was received with full honors by James I, who ended up proposing marriage to her.[6] On her arrival at the Castilian court, which at the time was in Valladolid, in January of 1258, Christina wa
2 CONC s presented to the king's brothers''', especially to Infantes Frederick and '''Philip, among whom she was to choose a husband. Apparently, the princess preferred Philip''' to Frederick, due to a scar which the latter had on his li
2 CONC p, disfiguring his face. However, in a later document, Alfonso X stated that it had been '''his decision that the Norwegian princess marry Infante Philip.[6]
2 CONT *'''The wedding of Philip and Christina was held in April of 1258 in Valladolid. Alfonso X then granted several income streams to Infante Philip, including the land tax, tolls, rents of the Jews, and the remaining royal revenue of
2 CONC  Ávila, as well as the church taxes of the Archdiocese of Toledo and the dioceses of Ávila and Segovia, the rents paid to the king by the Muslims of the Valley of Purchena, and the estate of Valdecorneja, composed of the towns of 
2 CONC El Barco de Ávila, Piedrahita, La Horcajada and Almirón.[6] The vacancy left by Infante Philip in the Archdiocese of Seville was not filled until May of 1259, when Pope Alexander IV named Raimundo de Losana to the post.[7]
2 CONT *'''Infante Philip attended the Cortes of Seville of 1261, as well as the wedding of his nephew''' Infante Ferdinand de la Cerda, son and heir of '''his brother''' King Alfonso X, which was held on 30 November 1269.[8]
2 CONT *In early 1272, a group of nobles, including Nuño González "the Good" de Lara, Esteban Fernández de Castro, Simón Ruiz de los Cameros, Lord of Los Cameros, and Lope Díaz III de Haro, Lord of Biscay, met in the town of Lerma with t
2 CONC he purpose of committing to fight against King Alfonso X if he did not yield to their demands. '''They agreed that Infante Philip, present at the meeting and spokesman for the conspirators, should meet with the King of Navarre in 
2 CONC order to convince him to grant them asylum if they found themselves obliged to leave the Kingdom of Castile and León. Infante Philip, widowed of his first two wives, was then married to Leonor Rodríguez de Castro''', the daughter 
2 CONC of Nuño González de Lara's sister.[9]
2 CONT *Nuño González de Lara was upset with the king due to, among other reasons, his failure to grant him Durango, his criticisms of his performance at the defense of Jerez de la Frontera and, perhaps, his own disagreement with some of
2 CONC  the king's behavior toward the Kingdom of Portugal, although most of the Castilian nobles agreed primarily in their opposition to King Alfonso's style of governance, as they all preferred the style of earlier times, when the nobl
2 CONC es played a more important role.[10]
2 CONT *After the meeting of the noble plotters in Lerma, Alfonso X tried to find out what had actually happened there, '''communicating with Infante Philip and Lara. However, Infante Philip avoided answering the questions raised by his 
2 CONC brother, at the time when he excused himself from leading his troops to Andalusia in the king's service, as he alleged that there had been a delay in the payment of his annual officers' salary, and told his brother that his presen
2 CONC ce at the meeting at Lerma was due to the advice and assistance which the infante said he needed, for he claimed that his old friends had died and that he "could not be without some friends who would assist and advise him."[11]
2 CONT *As far as he was concerned, and despite having taken part in it, Nuño González de Lara told the king that the purpose of the meeting in Lerma had not been the one attributed to it, and he even showed himself ready to work with hi
2 CONC m in order that new taxes might be collected, besides those already envisioned, in Castile and Extremadura, adding that in that way the king could repay his debt to the nobles, as he owed them several installments of their officer
2 CONC s' salary. At the beginning of July of 1272, Alfonso X ordered Nuño González de Lara, '''his brother Infante Philip''', and all the nobles of the kingdom to come with their men to Seville to assist Infante Ferdinand de la Cerda, w
2 CONC ho at the time was defending the frontier from attack by the Muslims, and the unanimous response of all the participants in the nobles' conspiracy was to refuse to come, unless the king met with them first. Nuño González de Lara p
2 CONC retended to break with the conspirators and informed the king of the contacts being maintained by the rebel nobles with the King of Navarre, although, shortly afterwards, a series of letters were discovered which proved the involv
2 CONC ement in the conspiracy of the Marinid sultan, who wished to weaken the position of the Castilian monarch. In spite of the discovery of the letters proving the nobles' treachery, Alfonso X ignored them and was ready to negotiate w
2 CONC ith the rebels, although he ordered them to suspend their conversations with the King of Navarre, an order which they disobeyed. Nuño González de Lara declared the end of his agreement with Alfonso X, which obliged him not to esta
2 CONC blish common positions with either Muslims or Christians without previously informing the king.
2 CONT *In September of 1272 negotiations resumed in the city of Burgos, although the nobles refused to lodge in the city and did so in nearby towns, and from there communicated to the king that if he wished to send them a message he sho
2 CONC uld do so via his emissaries. The conspirators then presented their demands to the king, complaining about his forcing them to abide by the Royal Fuero, the lack of special judges in the Court to judge noblemen according to their 
2 CONC own fuero, and the behavior of the governors and other officials of the Crown. Moreover, they asked him to reduce the frequency of their service to the Cortes, to exempt them from payment of the Burgos municipal tax, and to found 
2 CONC no more new towns in Castile and León. Shortly thereafter Alfonso X signed an alliance with the Kingdom of Navarre, thereby annulling the agreements made between the rebel nobles and Navarre.[12]
2 CONT *After the Cortes of Burgos of 1272, in which it seemed that the king would reach an agreement with the rebel nobles, negotiations were broken off and the rebels, '''including Infante Philip''' and Nuño González de Lara, left for 
2 CONC the Kingdom of Granada, in spite of Alfonso's final attempt to persuade them, through his intermediaries Infante Ferdinand de la Cerda and his brother Manuel not to abandon his kingdom. Before heading for Granada, the nobles sacke
2 CONC d the countryside, stealing cattle and laying waste to some of the territory on their way, in spite of the king's sending them messengers bearing letters in which he reminded the rebels of the favors they had received from him, as
2 CONC  well as their treacherous rupturing of the bonds between vassal and sovereign. He specifically reproached Nuño González de Lara for the fact that, during his youth, he had granted him the estate of Écija over the objections of hi
2 CONC s father King Ferdinand III.[13]
2 CONT *Nonetheless, the rebel nobles ignored the king's entreaties and continued on to Granada, where they were afforded all honors by King Mohammed I, after signing a treaty with him in Sabiote, in which the nobles and Muhammad committ
2 CONC ed to mutual assistance against Alfonso X until the latter yielded to their demands. '''The treaty of Sabiote was signed by, among others, Infante Philip''', Nuño González de Lara, Lope Díaz de Haro, Lord of Biscay, Esteban Fernán
2 CONC dez de Castro, Diego López V de Haro, and Álvar Díaz de Asturias.
2 CONT *'''In January of 1273, in Tudela, Infante Philip''', Nuño González de Lara and his sons Juan Núñez "the Fat" de Lara and Nuño González de Lara y León, Lope Díaz III de Haro, and Álvar Díaz de Asturias, among other nobles, paid ho
2 CONC mage to King Henry I of Navarre, to whom they presented documentation of the harm they claimed to have suffered at the hands of Alfonso X, and the demands they made of him, remaining thereby free of any commitment to Alfonso at th
2 CONC e time when, without detriment to their honor, they passed into the service of the King of Navarre in the same way in which they had previously served the King of Granada.
2 CONT *The king sent the dean of the Cathedral of Seville, Fernán Pérez, to '''talk to Infante Philip in order to persuade him to leave the nobles' party; this attempt failed'''.[14] At the beginning of 1273, Juan Núñez de Lara, who unt
2 CONC il then had acted as a mediator, along with the Bishop of Cuenca, between his father Nuño and the king, abandoned the latter.
2 CONT *In spite of this, Alfonso X, who wished to win election to the Imperial throne, allowed some members of the royal family, including the infantes Ferdinand de la Cerda and Manuel, Queen Violant, and Archbishop Sancho of Toledo, so
2 CONC n of James I and brother of the queen, and the grand masters of the military orders, to resume negotiations with the exiled nobles. After numerous negotiations, and advice given the king by, among others, his brother Infante Fadri
2 CONC que and Simón Ruiz de los Cameros, he yielded to most of the demands presented by the exiled nobles via Nuño González de Lara, who in 1273 met with Queen Violant in Córdoba, and at the end of that year, the exiled nobles returned 
2 CONC to the Kingdom of Castile and León, while at the same time King Muhammed II of Granada declared himself a vassal of Alfonso X, although his Chronicles erroneously place these events in 1274.[15] In July of 1273, the signatures of 
2 CONC Fernando Rodríguez de Castro, Simón Ruiz de los Cameros, and Diego López de Haro, younger brother of Lope Díaz III de Haro, once again begin to appear on royal documents. Those of Lope, Nuño González de Lara, and his son Juan Núñe
2 CONC z "the Fat" de Lara do not appear until the beginning of 1274.[16] Nuño González de Lara's signature began to appear once more on royal privileges on 24 January 1274, having not done so since 15 July 1272. In the beginning of 1274
2 CONC  he was named Governor of Andalusia, according to the Crónica de Alfonso X el Sabio.[17]
2 CONT *''' Infante Philip of Castile died on 28 November 1274, the date which is engraved on his tomb, at the age of 43.[18]
2 CONT *'''His body was buried in the Church of Santa María la Blanca, a church linked to the Order of the Temple, and located in Villalcázar de Sirga, in the province of Palencia, 10 km from Carrión de los Condes.[18]
2 CONT *'''The same church houses the tomb of his second wife, Inés Rodríguez Girón, although in the past it was believed to be that of his third wife, Leonor Rodríguez de Castro, an idea disproved by the arms and heraldic emblems engrav
2 CONC ed on the tomb, which are not those of the House of Castro, but rather those of the Girón family.[19]
2 CONT *'''The remains of Infante Philip lie in a Gothic tomb, located in the church's chapel of St James, next to the tomb of his second wife'''. In the same chapel there is a third tomb, dating from the 14th century, in which lie the r
2 CONC emains of a knight of the Order of Santiago. *'''The graves of the Infante and his wife were previously located in the church's choir, until the provincial Commission of Monuments decided to move them to their current location.[18
2 CONC ]
2 CONT *'''Infante Philip of Castile first married, in 1258, Christina of Norway'''. She died in Seville in 1262 '''without issue''', and was buried in the Collegiate Church of Saints Cosmas and Damian in Covarrubias, '''where Philip had
2 CONC  been the abbot before resigning his ecclesiastical duties.[20]
2 CONT *'''He then married Inés Rodríguez Girón'''. She died in 1265 and was buried in the Church of Santa María la Blanca in Villalcázar de Sirga,
2 CONT *'''He then married Leonor Rodríguez de Castro. This third marriage produced a son''' named Philip. He died in childhood and was buried in the Convent of San Felices de Amaya, a convent of the Order of Calatrava in the province of
2 CONC  Burgos, where his mother is also buried. It is currently in a state of ruin.[21]
2 CONT *The genealogist and historian José Pellicer de Ossau has '''recorded in his works the existence of another child born of this marriage, a daughter''' named Beatrice, '''although Luis de Salazar y Castro rejects this notion''':[22
2 CONC ] Blanche of Portugal bequeathed her the sum of 2000 maravedís.[18] She married Diego Pérez Sarmiento and died in 1340.
2 CONT *'''Infante Philip had several children out of wedlock by unknown women:
2 CONT **Fernando Alfonso
2 CONT **Alfonso Fernández (1263? – 1284). He served as Mayordomo mayor at the court of his uncle Alfonso X in 1283.
2 CONT **Beatriz Fernández, a nun at the Abbey of Santa María la Real de Las Huelgas in Burgos. In July of 1290, the abbess of the convent gave her license to dispose of her inheritance according to her own judgment. In January of 1295, 
2 CONC she returned the village of San Cristóbal del Monte, along with all its estates, land taxes, and rights, to the council of Belorado, the jurisdiction in which it had been up until King Sancho IV of Castile detached it to give to h
2 CONC er in 1288.
2 CONT *From: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_of_Castile _______________________
