2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Albrecht II "den fete" av Braunschweig-Göttingen, Albrecht "der Fette", född omkring 1268, död 1318, hertig av Braunschweig-Göttingen 1279-1318. Son till hertig Albrecht den store av Braunschweig (1236-1279) och Alessina av Montfe
2 CONC rrat (död 1285).
2 CONT
2 CONT Albrecht "den fete" gifte sig 10 januari 1284 med Rikissa av Werle (levde 1312), dotter till furst Henrik I av Werle (mördad 1291) och Rikissa Birgersdotter (Bjälboätten). Paret fick följande barn:
2 CONT
2 CONT    1. Magnus I av Braunschweig-Wolfenbüttel (död 1369), hertig av Braunschweig-Wolfenbüttel
2 CONT
2 CONT (Wikipedia)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Albrecht II Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen1
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #5342, b. circa 1268, d. 22 September 1318
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=16 Jul 2005
2 CONT
2 CONT      Albrecht II Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen was born circa 1268. He was the son of Albrecht I Herzog von Braunschweig and Adelheid de Montferrat.1 He married Rixa von Werle, daughter of Heinrich I Prinz von Werle and Rixa (
2 CONC ?), on 10 January 1284. He died on 22 September 1318.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Albrecht II Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen also went by the nick-name of Albrecht 'the Fat'.1 He was a member of the House of Guelph.1 He succeeded to the title of Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen in 1286.1 He succeeded t
2 CONC o the title of Herzog von Braunschweig in 1292.1 He gained the title of Herzog von Braunschweig-Wolfenbüttel in 1292.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Albrecht II Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen and Rixa von Werle
2 CONT
2 CONT Bruno von Braunschweig-Göttingen d. 31 Aug 1303
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelheid von Braunschweig d. c 1311
2 CONT
2 CONT Maud von Braunschweig-Göttingen d. a 1 Jun 1356
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto Herzog von Braunschweig+1 b. b 24 Jun 1292, d. 30 Aug 1344
2 CONT
2 CONT Albrecht von Braunschweig b. c 1294, d. 13 Oct 1359
2 CONT
2 CONT Wilhelm von Braunschweig b. c 1295, d. b 1318
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich von Braunschweig-Göttingen b. c 1296, d. 6 Feb 1363
2 CONT
2 CONT Richenza von Braunschweig b. c 1298, d. b 26 Apr 1317
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus I Herzog von Braunschweig+1 b. c 1304, d. c Jul 1369
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernst Herzog von Braunschweig-Göttingen+1 b. c 1305, d. 24 Apr 1367
2 CONT
2 CONT Luther von Braunschweig-Göttingen b. 1307, d. 1319
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT
2 CONT [S38] John Morby, Dynasties of the World: a chronological and genealogical handbook (Oxford, Oxfordshire, U.K.: Oxford University Press, 1989), page 132. Hereinafter cited as Dynasties of the World.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Albert II, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg; c. 1268 – 22 September 1318), called the Fat (pinguis), was duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second son of Albert the Tall, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Albert was a boy when his father died in 1279. He was first under guardianship of his uncle, Conrad, Bishop of Verden, and then of his elder brother, Henry I, Duke of 
2 CONC Brunswick-Lüneburg. In 1286 the three brothers divided their father's territory; Albert received the areas around Göttingen, Minden, Northeim, Calenberg, and Hanover. He made Göttingen his residence. In 1292, the third brother, Wi
2 CONC lliam, died childless, and Albert and Henry quarrelled about William's share, the areas around Brunswick and Wolfenbüttel; Albert finally prevailed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert married Rixa, daughter of Henry I, Prince of Werle, in 1284. They had the following children who reached adulthood:
2 CONT
2 CONT Adelaide, married John, Landgrave of Lower Hesse 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richenza, Abbess of Gandersheim 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mechtild 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jutta 
2 CONT
2 CONT Luder, joined the Teutonic Order 
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert II, Bishop of Halberstadt (died 1358) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry, Bishop of Hildesheim (died 1362) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto (died 1344) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Magnus (died 1369) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ernest (died 1367) 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT "Kövér"Albert herceg
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince of Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel
2 CONT
2 CONT joint reign with Henry I and William I
2 CONT
2 CONT 1279–1291
2 CONT
2 CONT Albert (Latin ''Albertus''; c. 1268 – 22 September 1318), called the Fat (''pinguis''), was duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second son of Albert the Tall, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, Albert was a boy when his father died in 1279. He was first under guardianship of his uncle, Conrad, Prince-Bishop of Verden, and then of his elder brother, Henry I, D
2 CONC uke of Brunswick-Lüneburg. In 1286 the three brothers divided their father's Principality of Wolfenbüttel; Albert received the areas around Göttingen, Minden, Northeim, Calenberg, and Hanover. He made Göttingen his residence, thus
2 CONC  Principality of Göttingen. In 1292, the third brother, William, died childless, and Albert and Henry, who had received the Principality of Grubenhagen, quarrelled about William's share, the remaining belittled areas around Brunsw
2 CONC ick and Wolfenbüttel; Albert finally prevailed.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albert_II,_Duke_of_Brunswick-L%C3%BCneburg
