2 CONT Eldest son of Duke Wartislaw IV and Elisabeth of Silesia, Bogislaw had two brothers, Barnim IV and Wartislaw V. The brothers were joint rulers from their father's death in 1326. They allied with King Casimir III of Poland, whose d
2 CONC aughter Elisabeth married Bogislaw, against the Teutonic Order.
2 CONT
2 CONT The death of Barnim led to a quarrel between Bogislaw and Wartislaw, which was settled by a treaty partitioning Pomerania between Bogislaw, Wartislaw, and Barnim's sons, also Bogislaw and Wartislaw. Bogislaw received part of Weste
2 CONC rn Pomerania, Wartislaw received Neustettin (now Szczecinek), and the sons of Barnim received Rügen and Usedom.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bogislaw's daughter Elisabeth married the Holy Roman Emperor Charles IV, King of Bohemia in 1363 and he concluded an alliance with his son-in-law in 1370.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 28 February 1343, Bogislaw married his first wife Elisabeth of Poland. She was a daughter of Casimir III of Poland and his first wife Aldona of Lithuania. They had two children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth of Pomerania (1347 - 15 April 1393). She married Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Casimir IV of Pomerania (c. 1351 - 2 January 1377). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth died in 1361. In 1362, Bogislaw V married his second wife Adelheid of Brunswick-Grubenhagen. She was a daughter of Ernest I, Duke Brunswick-Grubenhagen and Adelheid of Everstein. They had four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Wartislaw VII of Pomerania (d. 24 February 1395). Father of Eric of Pomerania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bogislaw VIII of Pomerania (c. 1363 - 11 February 1418). 
