2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/Sbl/Mobil/Artikel/14142#/Sbl/Mobil/Artikel/18240 History of Birger Persson (Finsta), by The Swedish Biographical Dictionary]
2 CONT
2 CONT Birger Petersson, Lagman,Knight, National Council, By Yngve Brilioth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birger Petersson, d. 1327, has been reported to be the son of a certain Peter Andersson to Mohammar, but this assumption has proved to be as unsustainable as the union of his family tree with Braheättens. Probably his father was k
2 CONC night Peter Israelsson, who is mentioned in records from the years 1269-74, and his mother was a daughter of Birger Skänkare. It is in a seal belonging to another daughter of this, which we first find the weapon with two wings, wh
2 CONC ich was then taken up by B. and became his family, the so-called Finstadättens, shield mark. On the fatherhood, B. was liberated with several of the chief men of the church: Archbishop Jakob Israelsson should have been his uncle, 
2 CONC Bishop Israel Erlandsson (angel) in Västerås and domprosten Andreas (And)in Uppsala his cousins. His brother Israel Petersson was likewise a court prosecutor in Uppsala. B. was married twice, first with Kristina, who was the daugh
2 CONC ter of the sword knight Johan Elovsson and did his will on July 6, 1293, and the second time with Ingeborg (di sept. 1314), daughter of Bengt Magnusson , the lawman in Östergötland. B's son Israel (d. 1351) became a knight and a c
2 CONC ouncilor, and the lawyer in Uppland; even two daughters survived the father, namely Katarina, GM Magnus Gudmarsson, and Birgitta , known as "the holy one" (see this).
2 CONT
2 CONT In preserved records, B. was first named in 1280. The gentlemen's title, the sign of attained knights' worthiness, was added to him in 1285. Ten years later he was named the lawyer, certainly over Tiundaland. However, he has proba
2 CONC bly already reached this dignity and has begun the work that would perhaps be the greatest of his life: the co-ordination and codification of the legal heritage of the three uplands into a common book of law, as already 2 January.
2 CONC  In 1296, the letter was issued, which in the name of the inexperienced king Birger made the assumption of the new law book. From this letter we find that it was as the leader of a twelve-man committee, B. performed the work. This
2 CONC  committee was composed of representatives from the three peoples, the foremost second B. was his cousin, the prince Andreas And. Upplandslagen holds a rank among our landscape laws: in formal terms designated as masterful, it als
2 CONC o actually meant progress in comparison with the previously codified Götalagar. This, in conjunction with the role it has come to play in our legal history, guarantees to the man who exercised the greatest influence on its editori
2 CONC al office, a prominent place in the Swedish legacy of hell's claim. After the uprising of the Uppland team, the three laws were merged into one, and his first law was B, the title "legifer Uplandiarum" then he carried to his death
2 CONC , with an interruption for the years 1316-18, when for unknown reasons he did not hold the office.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1297, B. was explicitly named as a member of the Council. This dignity as well as his attribute of the lawyer and perhaps already one of the nation's largest earth queens must give him a prominent position during the struggle b
2 CONC etween King Birger and his brothers, the dukes Erik and Valdemar, who are the first decades of the 14th century. From the beginning he seems to have been on a good footing with the king: in 1301 he received from this a gift of fiv
2 CONC e herb country in Finstad "for his faithful service", and in 1303 he was commissioned, together with the Archbishop and Andreas And, to be an arbitrator between the king and Duke Erik in a dispute about some uplifting goods upheav
2 CONC al to the following bread fight. During his first period (next to Torgils Knutsson's execution) we cannot decide if he has definitely taken part, nor do we know if he has taken part in those events, which culminated in the Håta Wi
2 CONC ki. The agreements of the year 1308, which returned the King's freedom and led to the division of the kingdom, made B. the Dukes of the Dukes, and soon we find the Uppland Team as their most trusted followers. Then the 1309 inhabi
2 CONC tants of Tiundal commanded that a man of every home would be prepared day and night to ride on their or B's commandments, and in 1310 he was their representative in demanding the so-called land debt. We do not feel, if it was prim
2 CONC arily personal or other, perhaps financially, considerations that conditioned his position. Together with Erik and Valdemar's other councilors, however, he seems to have been allowed to go to the castle, when the dukes borrowed mo
2 CONC ney from the lyre bears, and when we 1313 found him again as their tax collector, this may only mean, that he had to distract his gentlemen a round sum to then pay off the tax. For an expedition to Visby, he also seems to have had
2 CONC  to make expenses, and in connection with this he lists some other claims on the dukes. Of course, he was also among the gentlemen, who in Lödöse in 1315 settled a distribution between Erik and Valdemar of the landscapes that accr
2 CONC ued to them, and when these in prison at Nyköpingshus (1318) settled their will, they also included B. among the executives (together with Matts Kettilmundsson et al.). Surely, B., along with the newly named, also took the lead in
2 CONC  the uprising against Birger. When the Council on July 8, 1319 issued an assurance in the name of the newly elected King Magnus, B's name was set next to Mr Matts'. However, he also understood how to pay for what he sacrificed for
2 CONC  the new king's cause: Dec. 13
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1321, B. was prepared to go on a pilgrimage to "St. James (in Compostela) and other Saints' resorts," imitating their ancestors, who in several stages have been piled up to Jerusalem. On July 22, 1321, he issued a letter of con
2 CONC firmation to Lödöse on the farms he was ordained for a permanent substitute in Uppsala Cathedral. He says in this letter he is ready to undertake the "dangerous journey to the apostles' thresholds". Oct 1 In the same year, we also
2 CONC  found him in the estrangement, Avignon, where, on his behalf, a large decree was issued by Patriarch Egidius of Alexandria, two archbishops and eleven other bishops. These promises were for forty days each devoted to each one who
2 CONC  reads certain prayers for B's welfare during life or for his soul after death, as well as for the dukes of Erik and Valdemar, B's two wives, his father's. The souls of the mother, brothers and sisters, as well as for every one wh
2 CONC o gives alms to their pious or even received only by the alms, B. on Saturdays used to give in to the glory of the holy virgin, or in his presence with devotion heard the sermon. It is usually said that B. visited except St. Jacob
2 CONC  in Compostela also Rome and Jerusalem. The pilgrimage to the exhilarated Spanish pilgrimage site is explicitly rated and should not have to be questioned. The fact that he has also come to Rome and Jerusalem, however, seems unlik
2 CONC ely, since such a long journey can hardly have been cohabited during the time between July 22, 1321 and June 4, 1322, when he is home again, and the task of this should depend on a misunderstanding of the The tasks of the oldest s
2 CONC ources: "The thresholds of the apostles" need not mean anything but the assertion, in this case Avignon. likewise for every one who gives alms to their pious or also received only by the alms, B. on Saturdays used to give to the g
