2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT -https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MEISSEN.htm#Agnesdied1248
2 CONT
2 CONT c)         '''AGNES''' (-1 Jan 1248, bur Kloster Wienhausen).  The Genealogica Wettinensis names "Machtildem…et Agnetem" as the two daughters of "Conradus marchio filius Dedonis" & his wife, specifying that Agnes married "Heinricu
2 CONC s palatinus Reni frater Othonis imperatoris"[243].  She founded Kloster Wienhausen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (1211) as his second wife, HEINRICH Pfalzgraf bei Rhein, son of HEINRICH "der Löwe" ex-Duke of Saxony and Bavaria [Welf] & his second wife Matilda of England ([1173]-Braunschweig 28 Apr 1227, bur Braunschweig cathedral).  
2 CONC Herzog von Braunschweig-Lüneburg 1213.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnes_von_Landsberg 
2 CONT *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agnes_of_Landsberg 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Agnes of Landsberg''' [1] (1192 or 1193 – 1266 in Wienhausen) was a German noblewoman. She was the third child of Conrad II (1159–1210), Margrave of Lusatia, and his wife, Elisabeth (c. 1153 – 1209), the daughter of Mieszko III
2 CONC  the Old (1126–1202), Duke of Poland. She was a daughter-in-law of Henry the Lion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes was the youngest daughter of the count family from Landsberg near Halle, after Konrad (before 1207–1210) and Mathilde (around 1190–1225), whom Duke Albrecht II (Brandenburg) married. She was founded in 1211 with Henry I ("th
2 CONC e Long") of the Palatinate, Count Palatine near the Rhine (* 1173; † 28. April 1227 in Braunschweig), son of Henry the Lion, married in church (second marriage of Henry I.).[ 2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ersch and Gruber (1828) says about Agnes and her husband:[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT >When Otto died without heirs in 1218 after a restless and changing government in Harzburg, Henry took possession of his countries, handed over the imperial insignia to Frederick II and retired to Braunschweig, where he decided to
2 CONC  spend the rest of his days in peace, also declared his brother Wilhelm's only son, Otto the child, the sole heir of all Welfic allodiallanders in Saxony in 1223 and died in 1227. From his wife Agnes he had 2 daughters and 1 son: 
2 CONC last, the second Henry, the father had already ceded the Palatinate, but he died before him in 1214, it also seems that at this time Frederick II had taken the Palatine count from his father and transferred it to Duke Ludwig, beca
2 CONC use there are no further documents where he signs himself as Count Palatine, or no act where he had continued to accept the Palatinate after 1214; he married his two daughters Agnes to Duke Otto the Erlyte of Bavaria, Irmgard to M
2 CONC argrave Hermann IV of Baden; it is also in doubt whether not who Gertrude of Brunswick, who was married to Duke Frederick of Ostreich, had been a daughter of our Henry, after his beloved Agnes and the son produced with her had die
2 CONC d, Heinrich married Agnes von Landsberg for the second time, but no children were born from this marriage.
2 CONT Duchess Agnes died in 1266 and was buried in Wienhausen. 4]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heinrich_(V.)_der_Ältere_von_Braunschweig Henry (V.) the Elder of Brunswick]
2 CONT
2 CONT Heinrich (V.) the Elder of Braunschweig (* around 1173/74; † 28. April 1227 in Braunschweig) from the family of the Welfs was from 1195 to 1212 Palatine Count near the Rhine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT The eldest son of Duke Henry the Lion and Matilda of England was married since January or February 1194[1] to Agnes von Staufen, the hereditary daughter of the Rhenish Palatine Count Konrad von Staufen. From this marriage, Heinric
2 CONC h the Younger, Irmengard bei Rhein and Agnes, had three children. In a second marriage he was married to Agnes von Landsberg, daughter of Margrave Konrad II of Lower Lusatia († 1266), since 1211. This marriage remained childless.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Konrad_II._(Lausitz)
2 CONT
2 CONT Margrave Konrad II., also Margrave Konrad II. von Landsberg (* after the 13th century). September 1159; † 6. May 1210), was from 1190 Margrave of the East Mark (marchio Orientalis) / Lusatia and Lord of Rochlitz. After the death o
2 CONC f his older brother Dietrich in 1207, he inherited the Grafschaft of Groitzsch. As the son of Margrave Dedo III and Countess Mechthild von Heinsberg, who came from the Lower Rhine, he was called a Dedonid and belonged to the relat
2 CONC ives of the '''Wettiners''' in a broader sense.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages and descendants
2 CONT
2 CONT He probably married in the early 1180s, but not before 1181, Elisabeth of Great Poland (* around 1152; † 2. April 1209), a daughter of the Polish Duke Mieszko III and widow of Duke Soběslav II of Bohemia († 1180). He had three chi
2 CONC ldren with her.
2 CONT
2 CONT # a son whose name is not handed down (1207 testified; † before 6. May 1210)
2 CONT # Mechthild († 1255 in Salzwedel), buried in the monastery of Lehnin; ⚭ August 1205 Albrecht II. Margrave of Brandenburg, † 25. February 1220, buried in the monastery of Lehnin (Askani)
2 CONT # Agnes († 1266), founder of the monastery of Wienhausen, also buried there; ⚭ 1211 Heinrich I. der Lange, * probably 1173/74; † 28. April 1227 in Braunschweig, 1195–1212Pfalzgraf bei Rhein, buried in Braunschweig Cathedral
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------
