1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henneberg_%28Adelsgeschlecht%29#Von_Heinrich_I._an_.281262_bis_1583.29
2 CONT
2 CONT Henneberg (noble family)
2 CONT
2 CONT This article explains the count's family and their servants, the Truchseß von Henneberg family .
2 CONT
2 CONT Family coat of arms according to the Scheibler's coat of arms book
2 CONT The Counts of Henneberg were a Franconian noble family that rose to the status of princes directly subordinate to the Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Towards the end of the 11th century, in the wake of the collapse of the Franconian county constitution and the disappearance of the Franconian imperial property in the area between the Thuringian Forest and the Main, they expanded
2 CONC  their territory and founded the County of Henneberg . The counts, who presumably descended from the Popponen (Babenberger) family, were first mentioned in chronicles in 1078 and in documents in 1096. The male line of the house di
2 CONC ed out in 1583.
2 CONT
2 CONT The original coat of arms of the Hennebergers shows a black hen on three green hills in a golden field. From 1393 onwards, the coat of arms of the Henneberg-Schleusingen line shows the hen on the three hills in two fields and the 
2 CONC divided coat of arms of the Burgraviate of Würzburg (with double eagle and chess field) in the other two fields. [1]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Table of contents
2 CONT Development of the house
2 CONT
2 CONT Coat of arms of the von Hennenberg family in the Zurich coat of arms roll (ca. 1340)
2 CONT
2 CONT Veßra Monastery : Reconstructed entrance coat of arms
2 CONT
2 CONT The princely county of Henneberg 1312–1353
2 CONT origin
2 CONT The ancestral castle of the Henneberg counts was the Henneburg on the Henneberg near the village of the same name in what is now the Thuringian district of Schmalkalden-Meiningen . The family of the "von Henneberg" is first record
2 CONC ed in 1096 with the Würzburg burgrave Godebold II, whose descent is traced back to the Popponen (Babenbergers from the Babenburg ). The Hennebergers were probably burgraves in Würzburg as early as 1057 and thus military commanders
2 CONC  of the castle and city of Würzburg as well as chairmen of the high court; [2] these positions were then lost in the power struggle with the Würzburg bishops by 1354.
2 CONT
2 CONT The lordship's land holdings were built on the possessions of the Babenbergs, from whom Poppo I, Count of Henneberg (d. 1078) descended. Their imperial fiefdoms lay in the Thuringian Forest from the Schleuse to the Hasel , as well
2 CONC  as at Lichtenberg Castle and the surrounding area. Under Godebold II (d. 1144) the foundations for the importance of the Henneberg count's house were laid. He shifted the focus of his rule to the east. In this context the house m
2 CONC onastery of Veßra was founded in 1131. Godebold II strove for a closed land holding between Schleusingen and Henneberg. This meant that the ancestral castle of Henneberg was pushed to the fringes of the lordship. From the second h
2 CONC alf of the 12th century the Hennebergs gained influence to the northeast through the inheritance of the lordship of Nordeck ( Stadtsteinach ). The holdings remained constant until the middle of the 13th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Divisions into lineages
2 CONT In 1190, the main line split into the Henneberg, Botenlauben and Strauf lines . In the 12th and 13th centuries, several secondary lines split off, but these either remained insignificant or quickly died out again (Popponian lines,
2 CONC  Burgraves of Würzburg, Counts of Botenlauben, Henneberg-Coburg).
2 CONT
2 CONT Poppo II (died 1118) had already founded the branch line of the Lords or Counts of Frankenstein, which was based at Frankenstein Castle and Krayenburg and in the Lengsfeld lordship until around 1354, while Gotebold III (* 1107; d.
2 CONC  after 1164) founded a branch line in Wasungen . Branch lines that did not bear the title of count and were only named after their respective seats emerged in Lower Franconia in 1131 with the Lords of Irmelshausen , in 1156 those 
2 CONC of Lichtenberg and in 1199 those of Sternberg (Popponian lines).
2 CONT
2 CONT With the main division of Henneberg in 1274, the three lines of Hartenberg (until 1378), Aschach - Römhild (until 1549) and Schleusingen (until 1583) were created. The Schleusingen and Aschach-Römhild lines were made princes in 13
2 CONC 10 and 1474 respectively. The most powerful line was Henneberg-Schleusingen, based at Bertholdsburg Castle , which also lasted the longest. In 1310, Berthold VII of Henneberg-Schleusingen, who had received Henneburg in 1274, was e
2 CONC levated to the status of "princely count" and thus ruling imperial prince . At times he was plenipotentiary of the Electorate of Brandenburg and Saxony, administrator of Bohemia and guardian of the Wittelsbach emperor's son Ludwig
2 CONC  of Bavaria.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Dukes of Andechs-Merania died out in 1248, their possessions around Coburg fell to the Counts of Henneberg-Strauf. There were family ties between the two houses due to the marriage of Poppo VI, the father of the famous m
2 CONC instrel Otto von Botenlauben , to Sophie of Istria. After the Ludowingers died out in 1249 , Count Hermann I of Henneberg -Coburg (1224–1290) received an area around Schmalkalden from his stepbrother, Margrave Henry III of Meissen
2 CONC , and formed the "New Rulership" from the Strauf and Botenlauben inheritance, the Coburg area, and other gains. This was lost to the Electorate of Brandenburg with the death of his son Poppo VIII in 1291 as the inheritance of Herm
2 CONC ann's daughter Jutta through her marriage to the Ascanian co-regent Margrave Otto the Tall of Brandenburg at Salzwedel under the name of Pflege Coburg . Through the marriage of Jutta's granddaughter Jutta of Brandenburg, the later
2 CONC  regent Jutta of Henneberg , with the son of Count Berthold VII of Henneberg-Schleusingen, Henry VIII, the care of Coburg was returned to the now princely county of Henneberg in 1312 as a dowry, which thus reached its greatest ter
2 CONC ritorial extent.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most important towns in the county were now Schmalkalden , Coburg and Suhl . In 1542 Meiningen became part of Henneberg and became another centre of the county. Suhl was the economic centre with extensive mining and weapons ma
2 CONC nufacturing, while Meiningen had a strong textile and metalworking industry and trade, while the other towns became residences and centres of culture and art. The spiritual centre of the county was the Premonstratensian monastery 
2 CONC of Veßra (now Hennebergisches Museum Kloster Veßra ), founded by the Hennebergers in 1131 , which served as a burial place for almost all generations. One of the most important family members was Count Berthold VII, the Wise (1272
2 CONC –1340) of Henneberg-Schleusingen, who was recognized as an Imperial Prince in 1310 and who, through the marriage of his son to Jutta of Brandenburg, reacquired the Coburg estate for the House of Henneberg in 1312, which practicall
2 CONC y doubled his area of ​​rule. In 1313 he was the administrator of the Kingdom of Bohemia. The Archbishop of Mainz and Elector Berthold of Henneberg-Aschach (d. 1504) also played an important role at the imperial level. He implemen
2 CONC ted an imperial reform with the introduction of the Imperial Regiment , albeit one that was short-lived .
