1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Elżbieta pomorska''' -  córka Elżbiety Kazimierzówny i Bogusława V księcia wołogosko-słupskiego. Wnuczka Kazimierza III Wielkiego, króla Polski. 21 maja 1363 r. w Krakowie poślubiła króla Czech i cesarza rzymski
2 CONC ego Karola IV Luksemburskiego. 18 czerwca 1363 r. została koronowana w Pradze na '''królową Czech''', a 1 listopada 1368 r. w Rzymie papież Urban V koronował ją na '''cesarzową'''. http://pl.wikipedia.org/wiki/El%C5%BCbieta_pomors
2 CONC ka
2 CONT
2 CONT the Peerage.com
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Elizabeth of Pomerania (1347 – 15 April 1393) was the fourth and final wife of Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor and king of Bohemia. Her parents were Bogislaw V, Duke of Pomerania and Elisabeth of Silesia, (died 1361). Her maternal 
2 CONC grandparents were Casimir III, King of Poland and Aldona of Lithuania.
2 CONT
2 CONT The marriage of Charles and Elizabeth was held on 21 May 1363 in Krakow, only one year after the death of Charles' third wife Anne of Schweidnitz. The bride was only 16 years old while the groom was already 47. Charles married Eli
2 CONC zabeth mainly for diplomatic reason, as the marriage helped to break the anti-Czech coalition led by Duke Rudolf IV, Duke of Austria, with Polish and Hungarian kings as participants. On 18 June 1363 in the Bohemian capital Prague 
2 CONC Elizabeth was crowned Queen of Bohemia and 5 years later on 1 November 1368 she was also crowned empress in Rome by Pope Urban V.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth is reputed to have been a very vigorous, self-confident and physically strong woman. She bore six children to Charles:
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of Bohemia (1366–1394), who was married to Richard II of England  Sigismund (1368–1437), the Holy Roman Emperor and King of Bohemia and Hungary.  John of Görlitz (1370–1396).  Charles (13 March 1372–24 July 1373).  Margaret (
2 CONC 1373–1410), who was married to John III, Burgrave of Nuremberg.  Henry (1377–1378).  After her husband's death at 29 November 1378 in Prague, Elizabeth's stepson Wenceslaus IV, son of Charles's previous wife, ascended the throne. 
2 CONC Elizabeth then cared for her own two sons, mainly the older Sigismund, whom she supported in his efforts to become the king of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth outlived Charles IV by 15 years. She died on 14 February 1393 in Hradec Kralove and was buried next to her husband in St. Vitus Cathedral.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Elizabeth of Pomerania From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth of Pomerania (1347 – 15 April, 1393) was the fourth and final wife of Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor and king of Bohemia. Her parents were Bogislaw V, Duke of Pomerania and Elizabeth of Poland, (died 1361). Her maternal 
2 CONC grandparents were Casimir III, King of Poland and Aldona of Lithuania. The marriage of Charles and Elizabeth was held on 21 May 1363 in Kraków, only one year after the death of Charles' third wife Anne of Schweidnitz. The bride wa
2 CONC s only 16 years old while the groom was already 47. Charles married Elizabeth mainly for diplomatic reason, as the marriage helped to break the anti-Czech coalition led by Duke Rudolf IV, Duke of Austria, with Polish and Hungarian
2 CONC  kings as participants. On 18 June 1363 in the Bohemian capital Prague Elizabeth was crowned Queen of Bohemia and 5 years later on 1 November 1368 she was also crowned empress of the Holy Roman Empire in Rome by Pope Urban V. Eliz
2 CONC abeth is reputed to have been a very vigorous, self-confident and physically strong woman. She bore six children to Charles: Anne of Bohemia (1366–1394), who was married to Richard II of England Sigismund (1368–1437), the Holy Rom
2 CONC an Emperor and King of Bohemia and Hungary. John of Görlitz (1370–1396). Charles (13 March 1372–24 July 1373). Margaret of Bohemia (1373–1410), who was married to John III, Burgrave of Nuremberg. Henry (1377–1378). After her husba
2 CONC nd's death at 29 November 1378 in Prague, Elizabeth's stepson Wenceslaus IV, son of Charles's previous wife, ascended the throne. Elizabeth then cared for her own two sons, mainly the older Sigismund, whom she supported in his eff
2 CONC orts to become the king of Hungary. Elizabeth outlived Charles IV by 15 years. She died on 14 February 1393 in Königgrätz Hradec Králové and was buried next to her husband in St. Vitus Cathedral.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Elizabeth of Pomerania 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth of Pomerania  Queen consort of Germany  Tenure 1363–1376  Queen consort of Bohemia  Tenure 1363–1376  Holy Roman Empress  Tenure 1363–1376 
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor  Issue  Anna, Queen of England Sigismund, Holy Roman Emperor Margaret  House Luxembourg dynasty (by marriage) House of Pomerania (by birth)  Father Bogislaw V, Duke of Pomerania  Mother Elizab
2 CONC eth of Poland  Born 1347  Died 15 April 1393 (aged 45-46) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth of Pomerania (1347 – 15 April 1393) was the fourth and final wife of Charles IV, Holy Roman Emperor and king of Bohemia. Her parents were Bogislaw V, Duke of Pomerania and Elizabeth of Poland, (died 1361). Her maternal g
2 CONC randparents were Casimir III, King of Poland and Aldona of Lithuania.
2 CONT
2 CONT MarriageThe marriage of Charles and Elizabeth was held on 21 May 1363 in Kraków, only one year after the death of Charles' third wife Anne of Schweidnitz. The bride was 16 years old while the groom was 47. Charles married Elizabet
2 CONC h mainly for diplomatic reason, as the marriage helped to break the anti-Czech coalition led by Duke Rudolf IV, Duke of Austria, with Polish and Hungarian kings as participants. On 18 June 1363 in the Bohemian capital Prague Eliza
2 CONC beth was crowned Queen of Bohemia and 5 years later on 1 November 1368 she was also crowned empress of the Holy Roman Empire in Rome by Pope Urban V.
2 CONT
2 CONT She bore six children :
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne of Bohemia (1366–1394), who was married to Richard II of England  Sigismund (1368–1437), the Holy Roman Emperor and King of Bohemia and Hungary.  John of Görlitz (1370–1396).  Charles (13 March 1372–24 July 1373).  Margaret o
2 CONC f Bohemia (1373–1410), who was married to John III, Burgrave of Nuremberg.  Henry (1377–1378). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth is reputed to have been a very vigorous, self-confident and physically strong person.The relationship between Elizabeth and Charles is described to have been good and harmonius. During the serious illness of Charles in 1
2 CONC 371, Elizabeth made a miniature pilgrimage by walking on foot to the cathedral and offering gifts in a prayer to his health. Their good relationship has been portrayed in art, such as in Noc na Karlštejně (A Night at Karlstein). S
2 CONC he does not seem to have vieled any influence, however: she was tormented by the fact that Charles preferred his children form his former marriage, but was unable to change his mind.
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husband's death at 29 November 1378 in Prague, Elizabeth's stepson Wenceslaus IV, son of Charles's previous wife, ascended the throne. Elizabeth then cared for her own two sons, mainly the older Sigismund, whom she suppo
2 CONC rted in his efforts to become the king of Hungary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth outlived Charles IV by 15 years. She died on 14 February 1393 in Königgrätz Hradec Králové and was buried next to her husband in St. Vitus Cathedral.
2 CONT
