1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT Sophie was the third of seven children and the second daughter of Duke Magnus II of Mecklenburg and his wife Sophie of Pomerania.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 1 March 1500, she married Electoral Prince John of Saxony. John and Sophie had a son together:
2 CONT
2 CONT John Frederick I (1503–1554), succeed his father as Elector of Saxony, married in 1527 to Sibylle of Cleves (1512–1554)
2 CONT
2 CONT Torgau Altarpiece
2 CONT Sophie died shortly after the birth of her son before her husband became Elector. She was buried in the city church of St. Mary in Torgau. Her bronze grave plate was crafted by Peter Vischer the Elder in his workshop in Nuremberg,
2 CONC  after a design by Jacopo de' Barbari.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her widower and his brother Frederick the Wise founded an altar in her memory. It was dedicated to Saint Anne and the Fourteen Holy Helpers, and was inaugurated on 18 July 1505. The brother commissioned an altar piece from Lucas C
2 CONC ranach the Elder. A triptych which is now on display at the Städel Museum in Frankfurt and is known as the Torgau Altarpiece, is generally believed to be this altarpiece.[1] Another painting by Cranach, a compact representation of
2 CONC  the Fourteen Holy Helpers, remained in Torgau, and is now placed behind her grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sophie von Mecklenburg-Schwerin (1481-1503), Tochter des Herzogs Magnus II. von Mecklenburg-Schwerin; Heirat am 1. März 1500
2 CONT
