2 CONT The peace treaty held for the rest of Inge’s reign. However, Philippus did not respect its provisions and continued to use the title of king, and kept his royal seal. The relationship between Inge and his brother Haakon seems to h
2 CONC ave been at times tense. When it became clear that Philippus was continuing to call himself king, Haakon made attempts to have himself declared king as well, but Inge refused to accept this. Instead, an agreement was drawn up by w
2 CONC hich the brother that survived the other would inherit the other’s lands, while a legitimate son of either would inherit them both. Haakon had a legitimate son, while Inge only had an illegitimate son, Guttorm (b. 1206) by a concu
2 CONC bine called Gyrid. In 1214, Inge suppressed a rising by the farmers of Trøndelag – earl Haakon was suspected of having had a hand in the rising. Open conflict between the two brothers never broke out, however, and Haakon died of n
2 CONC atural causes in Bergen just after Christmas of 1214. Inge took over his part of the kingdom.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1217, Inge fell ill in Nidaros. During his illness, he appointed his younger half-brother, Skule Bårdsson, earl and leader of the army. On 23 April 1217, Inge died. He was buried in Nidaros Cathedral. He was succeeded as king b
2 CONC y the 13-year-old Haakon Haakonsson, an illegitimate grandson of king Sverre, who had been raised at the courts of king Inge and earl Haakon since they became aware of his existence in 1206. Skule continued as earl and de facto ru
2 CONC ler for the next few years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Appraisal of Inge
2 CONT
2 CONT Many historians have seen Inge as a rather weak king. He never achieved control over all of Norway, and agreed to a power-sharing with the bagler, which he stuck to even though Philippus broke the agreement by continuing to style 
2 CONC himself as "king". As a reaction to such views, others have claimed that Inge was a strong ruler, in that he was able to withstand the pressure of the more war-like among the birkebeiner and put a halt to the destructive civil war
2 CONC s for a time.
2 CONT
2 CONT The bagler sagas - a contemporary source - describes Inge as a quiet and calm man, who shied away from feasting and preferred to spend his time in his own quarters with close friends - a character trait which was held against him 
2 CONC by some of his men. He was weak of health after his near brush with death during the attack on Nidaros in 1206.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Our main sources to Inge's reign are the bagler sagas, which were written during and shortly after his reign. Inge is also mentioned less extensively in the start of Håkon Håkonsson's saga. The first Norwegian royal letter to surv
2 CONC ive dates from the days of Inge's reign, however, it was written by Inge's rival Philippus.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Konge av Norge 1204-1217
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Inge II Bårdsson (Spouse 1) Inge Bårdsson, 1185-1217, sønn av Ceccilia, kong Sverre Sigurdssons søster, og lendemann Bård Guttormsson til Rein i Trøndelag. Valgt til konge av birkebeinerne i 1204, kjempet mot baglerne ti
2 CONC l 1208, da de stridende møttes til forlik på Kvitsøy. Bondeopprør i Trøndelag mot kong Inge i 1213. Kilde “Braathens Safes kongerekke”
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inge Bårdsson, 1185-1217, sønn av Ceccilia, kong Sverre Sigurdssons søster, og lendemann Bård Guttormsson til Rein i Trøndelag. Valgt til konge av birkebeinerne i 1204, kjempet mot baglerne til 1208, da de stridende møttes til for
2 CONC lik på Kvitsøy. Bondeopprør i Trøndelag mot kong Inge i 1213. Kilde “Braathens Safes kongerekke”
2 CONT
2 CONT http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inge_B%C3%A5rdsson
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inge 2. Bårdsson (1185 – 23. april 1217) var norsk konge fra 1204–1217. Han var søn af Sverre Sigurdssons søster Cecilia og højadelsmanden Bård Guttormsson af Reinsætten. Inge blev valgt til konge af birkebeinerne i 1204. Han havd
2 CONC e sæde i Trondheim og havde også herredømmet over store dele af vestkysten.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han kæmpede mod baglerne frem til 1208, da han indgik forlig med baglerkongen Filippus Simonsson, som fik styret over Viken. Forliget fandt sted på Kvitsøy i Rogaland og blev kendt som «Kvitsøforliget». Konflikterne mellem baglere
2 CONC  og birkebeinerne sluttede, da både Inge og Filippus døde i 1217. Efterfølgeren, Håkon 4. Håkonsson, blev anerkendt af både baglere og birkebeinere. Inge er gravlagt i Nidarosdomen i Trondheim.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://no.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inge_B%C3%A5rdsson
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inge_II_of_Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inge 2 Bårdsson, konge 1204–17, sønn av Cecilia, kong Sverres søster, og lendmannen Bård Guttormsson til Rein i Trøndelag. Valgt til konge av birkebeinerne 1204, kjempet mot baglerne til 1208, da baglerkongen Filippus etter et for
2 CONC liksmøte fikk Viken å styre under Inges overhøyhet. Inges halvbrødre Håkon Galen og senere Skule Bårdsson hadde som ledere for hirden stor innflytelse på hans styre.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inge II Bårdsson (født 1185, død 1217) var en norsk konge. Han var sønn av Sverre Sigurdssons søster Cecilia og høyadelsmannen Bård Guttormsson av Reinsætten. Hans halvbror var Skule Bårdsson som ble Norges første hertug. Inge ble
2 CONC  valgt til konge av birkebeinerne i 1204.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han hadde kongesete i Trondheim, og hadde også herredømme over store deler av vestkysten. Borgerkrigsperioden i norsk historie varte fra 1130 til 1240.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han kjempet mot baglerne frem til 1208, da det kom til forlik mellom ham og baglerkongen Filippus Simonsson, som fikk styre over Viken. Forliket fant sted på Kvitsøy i Rogaland og ble kjent som «Kvitsøyforliket». Konfliktene mello
2 CONC m baglere og birkebeinere tok slutt da både Inge og Filippus døde i 1217. Etterfølgeren, Håkon IV Håkonsson, ble anerkjent av både baglere og birkebeinere. Inge er gravlagt i Nidarosdomen i Trondheim.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Inge Bårdsson was the son of Bård Guttormsson and Cecilia, daughter of King Sigurd Munn. He became king with the support of the Birkebeiners, and by overthrowing Håkon Galen. Inge ruled only over a part of Norway, and in 1206 Håko
2 CONC n IV Håkonsson also became king over the rest of the country, terestingly, there were no quarrels because of this. Inge himself is described as a rather weak ruler, as he had to share his power with his half-brothers Skule and Håk
2 CONC on Galen, who were very powerful men in the background, with Håkon leading the army of Inge. Under Inge's reign the local chieftains, Earls and Jarls got stronger influence. This rather happened due to the fact that the Earls and 
2 CONC Jarls joined forces to make demands and have greater influence over the King. Inge reign was a rather peaceful period, and this was achieved by marriages and other tricks. Håkon Galen became a Jarl, which gave him stronger influen
2 CONC ce, and the kings daughter Kristin was married to the King Fillipus. After Håkon's death in 1214, Inge took over the rule of Western Norway, which Håkon had ruled before. Even though, there were revolts and the great power of Håko
2 CONC n and Skule let them over throne King Inge from time to time, which undermined Inge's position as King. Inge became sick and died in 1217.
2 CONT
2 CONT More About King Inge Ii Bårdsson of Norway: Occupation: King of Norway from 1204 to 1217.
2 CONT
2 CONT More About King Inge Ii Bårdsson of Norway and Torbjørg Åsulvsdatter: Marriage: Abt. 1205
2 CONT
