2 CONC e changed into royally appointed agentscharged with enforcing the law and collecting taxes in the counties.Personally interested in government and law, Henry strengthened royaljustice, making use of juries and re-introduced the se
2 CONC nding of justices(judges) on regular tours of the country to try cases for the
2 CONT Crown. His legal reforms have led him to be seen as the founder ofEnglish Common Law. Henry's disagreements with his Archbishop ofCanterbury,
