1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_IX_of_Poitou
2 CONT
2 CONT William (17 August 1153 – April 1156) was the first child of Henry Plantagenet (later Henry II of England) and Eleanor of Aquitaine. He was born in Normandy  on the same day that his father's rival, Eustace IV of Boulogne, died.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was a younger maternal half-brother of Marie de Champagne and Alix of France. He was an older brother of Henry the Young King, Matilda, Duchess of Saxony, Richard I of England, Geoffrey II, Duke of Brittany, Leonora of Aqu
2 CONC itaine, Joan Plantagenet and John of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in April 1156, aged two years due to a seizure at Wallingford Castle and was buried in Reading Abbey at the feet of his great-grandfather Henry I.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of his death, he had already been given the title of Count of Poitiers. For centuries, the dukes of Aquitaine had held this as one of their minor titles, so it had passed to Eleanor from her father; giving it to her so
2 CONC n was effectively a revival of the title, separating it from the dukedom. Some authorities say he also held the title of "Archbishop of York", but this is probably an error. His half-brother Geoffrey Plantagenet (died 1212), who w
2 CONC as born within months of William, did later hold that office, causing the confusion.
2 CONT
2 CONT William
2 CONT
2 CONT Count of Poitiers
2 CONT
2 CONT Reign 	17 August 1153 – April 1156
2 CONT
2 CONT Predecessor 	Henry II Curtmantle, King of the English
2 CONT
2 CONT Successor 	Vacant (later Otto IV, Holy Roman Emperor)
2 CONT
2 CONT House 	House of Plantagenet
2 CONT
2 CONT Father 	Henry II Curtmantle, King of the English
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother 	Eleanor of Aquitaine
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 	17 August 1153(1153-08-17)
2 CONT
2 CONT Normandy, France
2 CONT
2 CONT Died 	April 1156 (aged 2)
2 CONT
2 CONT Wallingford Castle, Berkshire
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial 	Reading Abbey, Berkshire
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
