2 CONT >>b)         PHILIPPE (-after 1201).  His father gave him the castle of Cognac, installing him as Seigneur de Cognac.  Courlieu records "Philippes sieur de Cognac et Merpin, qui espousa Amélie nièce de Nobilie dame de Iarnac" livi
2 CONC ng "en Engoumois" at the end of the 12th century but does not cite the corresponding primary sources[476].  Roger of Hoveden records in 1199 that "Philippus filius Ricardi regis Angliæ nothus", to whom the king had granted "castel
2 CONC lum et honorem de Cuinac", killed "vicecomitem de Limoges" in revenge for his father’s death[477].  He sold his English lordship to King John in 1201[478].  King John granted 50 marks to “Philippo fil R. Ric” in 1201[479].  m AMEL
2 CONC IE [de Jarnac], daughter of ---.  The primary source which confirms her marriage has not been identified.  Courlieu records "Philippes sieur de Cognac et Merpin, qui espousa Amélie nièce de Nobilie dame de Iarnac" living "en Engou
2 CONC mois" at the end of the 12th century but does not cite the corresponding primary sources[480].  
