1 NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> [QUEEN OF CASTIL
2 CONT Ancestral File Number:<AFN> 8XJ3-Z8
1 NOTE Daughter of Henry II of England and Eleanor of Aquitaine.  In 1176 she married Alfonso VIII, king of Castile.  He was promised Gascony as her dowry and seized it by force in 1204 after Eleanor of Aquitaine's death.  His brother-in
2 CONC -law, King John of England, recaptured it in 1206.   The Cistercian royal nunnery of Las Huelgas in Castile was founded in 1187 at Eleanor's request.  It is built in a similar style to buildings in Angers, her birthplace, suggesti
2 CONC ng that Eleanor may have employed an Angevin architect.  She and Alfonso were buried there.
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-20792
1 NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-887
1 NOTE Eleanor (Eleanora) Plantaganet de Castile, born in 1162, died in 1214,promised initially by her father to marry French royalty, but eventuallymarried Afonso VIII. de Castile., King de Castile (1158-1214). He was theson of Sancho I
2 CONC II., the Desired, King de Castile (1157-1158). See thislineage in the Kings of Spain Line in Volume I. Eleanor and Afonso VIII.had the following children (Note: Some differences are seen between thislist and the one in the Kings o
2 CONC f Spain Line):
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Sancho de Castile.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Bergenuela (Berengaria) de Castile, married Afonso IX, King of Leon.They had a son, Ferdinand III, who married Joanna Dammartin. See thecontinuation of this lineage in the Spanish Kings Line of Volume I.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. Uracca de Castile, had been promised to Louis VIII., the heir ofFrance, but eventually married Afonso II. the Fat, King of Portugal,1211-1223. She was rejected by the French because of her unusual name.Her younger sister, Blanc
2 CONC he was deemed to be more suitable for the Frenchtastes.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. Blanche de Castile, born on March 4, 1188, in the palace of Placentia,in Castile, where she spent most of her childhood years, married on May23, 1200, Louis VIII. of France., prince and heir of France andeventually King of Fran
2 CONC ce. Blanche received as her dowry, the town ofEvreux with its surrounding land, always a bone of contention betweenFrance and England, but nevertheless given to her by her father-in-law,King Philip. Her uncle, King John of England
2 CONC , gave her the fiefs ofIssoudun and Grapay in Berry. See the continuation of this lineageelsewhere in Volume I. See Pernoud, "Blanche de Castile," for details onher life and times. Their son was King Louis IX of France.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. Constancia de Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Matilda de Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT 7. Sancha de Castile
2 CONT
2 CONT 8. Enrique de Castile
