1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/89856123/william_i-of_holland
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I,_Count_of_Holland
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00013570&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/HOLLAND.htm#WillemIdied1222B Medlands]
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_I,_Count_of_Holland Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT William I (c. 1167, The Hague – 4 February 1222), Count of Holland from 1203 to 1222. He was the younger son of Floris III and Ada of Scotland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Life
2 CONT
2 CONT William was raised in Scotland. He started a revolt against his brother, Dirk VII and became count in Friesland after a reconciliation. Friesland was considered as a part of Holland by the Counts of Holland. His niece, Ada, eventu
2 CONC ally inherited Holland in 1203, but William couldn't accept this. After a civil war, which lasted for several years, William won the war. Louis and Ada were supported by the bishop of Liège and bishop of Utrecht, and the count of 
2 CONC Flanders. William was supported by the duke of Brabant and by the majority of the Hollanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor Otto IV acknowledged him as count of Holland in 1203, because he was a supporter of the Welfs. He and many others changed allegiance to emperor Frederick II after the battle of Bouvines in 1214. He took part in a French ex
2 CONC pedition against king John of England. The pope excommunicated him for this.
2 CONT
2 CONT Possibly because of this, William then became a fervent crusader. He campaigned in Prussia and joined in the conquest of Lisbon. In Europe, he came to be called William the Crazy for his chivalric and reckless behaviour in battle.
2 CONC  William conquered the city of Damietta during the Fifth Crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Evolution of the county
2 CONT
2 CONT There were great changes in the landscape of Holland in the end of the 12th and during the 13th century. Many colonists bought land to turn the swamps into polders. Most of the swamps had been sold, and irrigation had started duri
2 CONC ng the reign of William. Huge infrastructural works were done; the island called Grote Waard was enclosed with dikes all around and a dam was built at Spaarndam. New governmental bodies were created, the so-called waterschappen an
2 CONC d hoogheemraadschappen, which were charged with the task of protecting the polders against ever-present threat of flooding. Count William granted city rights to Geertruidenberg in 1213, to Dordrecht in 1217, to Middelburg in 1220 
2 CONC and perhaps also to Leiden. In this way he gave an impulse to trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family
2 CONT
2 CONT Count William was married twice. First, he was married in 1197 at Stavoren to Adelheid of Guelders, daughter of Otto I, Count of Guelders and Richarde of Bavaria. Adelheid died on 12 February 1218 while William was away on crusade
2 CONC . On his return he married secondly, in July 1220, Marie of Brabant, daughter of Henry I, Duke of Brabant and Maud of Boulogne and Alsace. She was the widow of Emperor Otto IV. William and his first wife Adelaide had the following
2 CONC  children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Floris IV, Count of Holland (24 June 1210 The Hague–19 July 1234, Corbie, France). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Otto (d. 1249), Regent of Holland in 1238-1239, Bishop of Utrecht. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem (d. 1238), Regent of Holland in 1234-1238. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Richardis (d. 1262). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ada (d. 1258), Abbess at Rijnsburg 1239. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT William I (c. 1167, The Hague – 4 February 1222), Count of Holland from 1203 to 1222. He was the younger son of Floris III and Ada of Scotland.
