0 @N6000000002187788010@ NOTE Dietrich, Count of Oldenburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Derrick or Dietrich of Oldenburg, latin-based anglicization also Theoderic of Oldenburg [1] (c. 1398–February 14, 1440), nicknamed Theoderic the Lucky or the Fortunate (Teudericus Fortunatus), was a feudal lord in northern Germany
1 CONC , holding the counties of Delmenhorst and Oldenburg. He was called "Fortunatus" as he was able to secure Delmenhorst for his branch of the Oldenburgs.
1 CONT
1 CONT Dietrich is the father of Christian I of Denmark, a male-line ancestor to the present-day Danish throne under Margaret II of Denmark.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Lineage
1 CONT     * 2 Marriages and children
1 CONT     * 3 Male Line of Descendants
1 CONT     * 4 Notes
1 CONT     * 5 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Lineage
1 CONT
1 CONT He was the son of Count Christian V of Oldenburg (who became count in about 1398 and died in 1423) and his wife Countess Agnes of Honstein. His grandfather, Count Conrad I of Oldenburg (d. approx. 1368) had left his lands divided 
1 CONC between his father and his uncle Conrad II.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father Christian V managed to gain the upper hand when Conrad II's son Maurice IV died in 1420. After this, most of Oldenburg family patrimony was under the power of Theodoric's branch. However, the house had several minor bra
1 CONC nches who had estates and claims, as was usual in any medieval fief.
1 CONT
1 CONT Theoderic of Oldenburg was the grandson of Ingeborg of Itzehoe, a Holstein princess who had married count Conrad I of Oldenburg. After the death of her only brother, Count Gerhard V of Holstein-Itzehoe-Plön, in 1350, Ingeborg and 
1 CONC her issue were the heirs of Ingeborg's grandmother Ingeborg of Sweden (d. about 1290, first wife of Gerhard II of Plön-Itzehoe), the eldest daughter of King Valdemar of Sweden and Queen Sophia, who herself was the eldest daughter 
1 CONC of the sonless King Eric IV of Denmark and his wife Jutta of Saxony. Since other legitimate heirs of king Valdemar apparently were extinct at this time, Theoderic was regarded to have been the Heir-General of kings Valdemar I of S
1 CONC weden and Eric IV of Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT Theodoric succeeded his father as head of the house in 1423.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Marriages and children
1 CONT
1 CONT He had firstly, as a child, married (for reasons of succession and uniting the hereditary fiefs) a distant cousin, Countess Adelheid of Oldenburg-Delmenhorst (who is said to have died already in 1404), daughter of Oldenburg Count 
1 CONC Otto IV of Delmenhorst, and in 1423 he married for a second time, Helvig of Schauenburg (born in about 1398-1400, died 1436), widow of Prince Balthasar of Mecklenburg and daughter of the murdered Duke Gerhard VI of Schleswig-Holst
1 CONC ein and his wife Elisabeth of Brunswick, thus sister of the reigning Duke Adolf VIII. All his legitimate children were born of the second wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT His second marriage strengthened this interest in Scandinavian monarchies, since Helvig was a descendant of King Eric V of Denmark, King Haakon V of Norway and King Magnus I of Sweden.
1 CONT
1 CONT At this time, all Scandinavia lived under the Kalmar Union erected by Queen Margaret I of Denmark. In 1387 she had lost her own heir Olav IV of Norway, the new heirs now being Eric of Pomerania, and his sister Catherine who was ma
1 CONC rried with a prince of the Palatinate and Bavaria.
1 CONT
1 CONT Count Theodoric of Oldenburg is said to have been a rival claimant to the crowns of Sweden and Denmark during the reign of Eric VII/ Eric XIII, whose succession was through Christopher I of Denmark, the younger brother of the murd
1 CONC ered Eric IV, and through Magnus I of Sweden, younger brother of the deposed King Valdemar.
1 CONT
1 CONT Count Theodoric had three surviving sons and one daughter:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Christian (1426-1481), who succeeded him as Count of Oldenburg and Delmenhorst, and became later King Christian I of Denmark, Norway and Sweden, as well as Duke of Schleswig and Holstein.
1 CONT     * Maurice (1428-1464); when his elder brother became king, he was given the County of Delmenhorst.
1 CONT     * Gerhard (1430-1500); when his eldest brother had become king, he was given the county of Oldenburg, and from his other brother's heirs he also inherited Delmenhorst in about 1483. The third son got his name from usages of th
1 CONC e mother's Holstein clan.
1 CONT     * Adelheid (1425-1475), first married count Ernest III of Hohnstein (d. 1454) and then in 1474 Count Gerhard VI of Mansfeld (d. 1492).
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Male Line of Descendants
1 CONT
1 CONT Dietrich of Oldenburg is a direct ancestor to the British royal family and, ultimately, Elizabeth II, Queen of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland. In fact, if one is to follow the paternal ancestry, one would
1 CONC  find that following only Prince William's male ancestry, Dietrich is the direct patrilineal ancestor to William (see below). Dietrich is also the patrilineal ancestor to the last Russian czars of Romanov-Holstein-Gottorp as well.
1 CONC  Dietrich is also the patrilineal ancestor to the Norwegian royal family and, ultimately, Harald V of Norway. The Norwegian line splits off the British line at Christian IX of Denmark, who was the father of both George I of Greece
1 CONC  and Frederick VIII of Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Conrad I of Oldenburg
1 CONT     * Christian V of Oldenburg
1 CONT     * Dietrich of Oldenburg
1 CONT     * Christian I, King of Denmark
1 CONT     * Frederick I, King of Denmark
1 CONT     * Christian III, King of Denmark
1 CONT     * Johann II, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Plon
1 CONT     * Alexander, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg
1 CONT     * August Philipp, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Beck
1 CONT     * Frederick Louis of Holstein-Sonderburg-Beck
1 CONT     * Peter August, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Beck
1 CONT     * Karl Anton August, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Beck
1 CONT     * Friedrich Karl Ludwig, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Beck
1 CONT     * Friedrich Wilhelm, Duke of Schleswig-Holstein-Sonderburg-Glücksburg
1 CONT     * Christian IX, King of Denmark
1 CONT     * George I, King of the Hellenes
1 CONT     * Prince Andrew of Greece
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Duke of Edinburgh, Prince Philip
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Prince Charles, Prince of Wales, Duke of Cornwall, Duke of Rothesay
1 CONT     * His Royal Highness The Prince William, Prince of the United Kingdom of Great Britain and Northern Ireland
1 CONT
1 CONT The Norwegian line splits off of Christian IX here:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Christian IX, King of Denmark
1 CONT     * Frederick VIII, King of Denmark
1 CONT     * Haakon VII, King of Norway
1 CONT     * Olav V, King of Norway
1 CONT     * Harald V, King of Norway
1 CONT     * Haakon, Crown Prince of Norway
1 CONT     * Ingrid, Princess of Norway
1 CONT
1 CONT Dietrich therefore is a great(x15)-grandfather of William, who is currently second in line for the British throne. He is also a great(x16)-grandfather of Ingrid, who is currently second in line for the Norwegian throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eventually, he would become the ancestor of every royal family in Europe through Christian I, Frederick I, Christian IX, and Alexandra of Denmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Notes
1 CONT
1 CONT     * ^ Alternate names include:
1 CONT           o Medieval Latin: Teudericus de Oldenburg
1 CONT           o Medieval Scandinavian: Didrik af Oldenborg
1 CONT           o German: Dietrich von Oldenburg
1 CONT           o Medieval French: Thierry d'Oldenbourg
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Photos from Oldenburg town museum
