0 @N6000000002322315796@ NOTE He ably ruled his Bohemian kingdom and spread his influence not only into
1 CONT Poland, but into Hungary, as well. Succeeding the throne at age seven,
1 CONT Wenceslas lived at the court of his cousin Otto IV of Brandenburg who served
1 CONT as regent for Wenceslas until 1283. Upon returning to Prague, he found that
1 CONT his country was dominated by the ambitious Zavis of Falkenstein, his mother's
1 CONT lover and later her husband, whom he arrested in 1289, destroyed the
1 CONT dissident faction and executed his rival in 1290. King of Poland in 1300.
0 @N6000000002187834927@ NOTE Wenceslaus II of Bohemia
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Waclaw II
1 CONT Wenceslaus II Premyslid (Czech: Václav II; Polish: Waclaw II Czeski; September 17, 1271 – June 21, 1305) was King of Bohemia (1278 - 1305), Duke of Kraków (1291 - 1305), King of Poland (1300 - 1305).
1 CONT
1 CONT Václav II was the son of Ottokar II "the Great," King of Bohemia, and Kunegunda, daughter of Rostislav, Grand Duke of Kyiv. Václav's father died in battle August 26, 1278, shortly before Václav's seventh birthday.
1 CONT
1 CONT Václav's early reign was tainted with abuses of power by his regent, Otto, Margrave of Brandenburg, and later by his mother's secret husband, Zavis of Falckenstein.
1 CONT
1 CONT On January 24, 1285, Václav married Judith (Jutta) von Habsburg, daughter of Holy Roman Emperor Rudolf I. In 1290 Václav had Zavis beheaded and began ruling independently.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1291 Václav was invited by a group of Polish lords to take over the Duchy of Kraków, and in 1300 he was crowned King of Poland. He was planning to invade Austria when he died in 1305. He was succeeded by his son, Václav III, la
1 CONC st of the Premyslid kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1298, silver was discovered at Kutná Hora, in central Bohemia. Václav took control of the mine by making silver production a royal monopoly. Kutná Hora was one of the richest European silver strikes ever: between 1300 and 1340,
1 CONC  the mine may have produced as much as 20 tons of silver a year.
1 CONT
1 CONT Václav's daughter by Judith, Elizabeth (Eliska), married John "the Blind" of Luxembourg. Judith died in 1297.
1 CONT
1 CONT Václav's second wife was Elisabeth Richeza, daughter of Przemysl II, King of Poland 1295 - 1296. After Václav's death, she married Rudolph of Habsburg.
