1 CONT HIST HENRY II, WAS BORN IN 1133 TO GEOFFREY V PLANTAGENT AND MATILDA THEEMPRESS, DAUGHTER OF KING HENRY I OF ENGLAND.  HE GREW UP IN ANJOU,FRANCE, BUT VISITED ENGLAND AS EARLY AS 1142 TO DEFEND HIS
1 CONT  MOTHER'S CLAIM TO THE THRONE OF STEPHEN. EDUCATED
1 CONT HIST HENRY REVITALIZED THE BRITISH EXCHEQUER, ISSUING RECEIPTS FOR TAXPAYMENTS AND LOGGING ACCOUNTS ON ROLLED PARCHMENT.  HE REPLACEDINCOMPETENT SHERRIFFS, EXPANDING THE AUTHORITY OF ROYAL COURTS,
1 CONT  WHICH BROUGHT MORE MONEY TO HIM.  A BODY OF COMMON
1 CONT HIST THIS PROCESS OF STRENGTHENING ROYAL COURTS LED TO CONTROVERSY WITHCHURCH COURTS, WHERE MANY FUGITIVES FOUND A SAFE HAVEN AS AS MANY AS 1 IN50 ENGLISH SUBJECTS QUALIFIED AS CLERICS.  HENRY
1 CONT  WISHED TO TRANSFER SUCH CASES TO THE ROYAL COURTS, AS
1 CONT HIST THE SONS OF HENRY, HENRY THE YOUNG, RICHARD THE LION- HEART,GEOFFREY, AND JOHN LACK;AND, WERE NEVER SATISFIED AT THEIR FATHERS PLANSIN DIVIDING THE KINGDOM UPON HIS DEATH.  AT THE
1 CONT  ENCOURAGEMENT OF THEIR MOTHER, THE SON'S REBELLED SEVERAL
1 CONT DATE 24 MAY 2000
0 @N6000000005599114553@ NOTE The Plantagenet dynasty which ruled England from 1154 until 1485, wast he longest period for any Royal house since the Norman Conquest.  The instant Henrys' father, Geoffrey V, Count of Anjou and Maine had the P lantagenet sobriqu
1 CONC et applied to him.  It has been variously spelled, b ut its exact derivation is not known for certain.  Henry was a man of action, learning and is generally considered to have been a great king .  He had a freckeled 'lion like' fa
1 CONC ce, with a strong stocky frame, re d hair, grey eyes, a bull like neck, spectacular temper and unbounded energy.  It is said that he had a tendency towards corpulence.  His ni ckname of 'Curtmantle' is derived from his habit of we
1 CONC aring his robes only to his knee - the style of Anjou.  The larger part of his educati on was completed at Angers and in Normandie under the guidance of Will iam of Conches.  Henry inherited a control of the duchy of Normandie, a 
1 CONC claim to the throne of England and control of the counties of Anjou and Maine.  The instant Henry, sometimes styled as 'Henry Fitz Empres s ( as a result of his mother) was Count of Anjou, Duke of Normandie a nd by marriage to Ele
1 CONC anor d'Aquitaine (cast off of King Louis VII of F rance) Duke of Aquitaine when at the age of 21 he ascended to the thro ne of England.  His realm, the 'Angevin Empire' extended from Ireland to the Pyrenees.  He maintained his rul
1 CONC e in England through his justic iar and on the continent through his sensecal.  When Henry II moved, t he entire paraphernalia of his court moved with him, his treasury, hi s chancellor, his clerks and business documents, his cham
1 CONC berlain and t ailor, his ewerer (bath preparer) and the keeper of his hounds.  The b iggest disaster of his reign, the murder of Thomas Beckett stemmed par tly from his ungovernable temper.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was born on the 5th of March 1133 at Le Mans. He was the son of Matilda (daughter of Henry I), and of Geoffrey, the Count of Anjou.  T he instant Henry, also bore the titles of Duke of Normandie and Aquita ine and was styled
1 CONC  as Henry Fitz Geoffrey or Henry Fitz Empress.  The counts of Anjou were known as the Angevins and Henry was to become th e first Angevin king.  Henry was sent from Normandie to England by hi s father Geoffrey in 1142, but within 
1 CONC the year was sent back.  By the time that he was 14, Henry was fully convinced that both Normandie an d England were his as the rightful heir.  He was knighted at Carlisle by his great uncle, David, King of Scotland in 1149.  Henr
1 CONC y first beca me the Duke of Normandie in 1151 and when his father died in the follo wing year, he inherited the Angevin empire.  At the time that Henry (t hen 17) became Duc of Normandie, he is styled as a stocky reheaded you th e
1 CONC ndowed with energy and ambition, but his reaction to frustration w as often termed as violent.  When his father died, Henry broke his pro mise to let his younger brother Geoffrey rule the families ancesteral fiefs and took over in
1 CONC  Anjou, Maine & Touraine.  In 1153 Henry marrie d Eleanor of Aquitaineand through this marriage added the territories of Aquitaine to his already large empire.
1 CONT
1 CONT Matilda, Henry's mother, was heir to the English throne as she was th e eldest daughter of Henry I, King of England, but Matilda's cousin St ephen had claimed the throne with some backing from the English Baron s who were opposed 
1 CONC to a female ruler. Matilda's attempts to reclaim th e throne lead to a damaging civil war in England between forces loyal to her and those loyal to King Stephen.  In December of 1152, Henry ha d visisted his mother Matilda who the
1 CONC n lent him the monies necessary t o finance his expedition to England.  In January of 1153, Henry saile d from Barfleur for England with twenty-six ships and an army of 3,00 0 foot and 140 horse.  Having landed, he took the castle
1 CONC s of Malmesbur y and Warwick.  He marched for Wallingford, but found Stephen held th e opposite bank of the Thames.  Both of the banks of the river were pi led high with snow.  William de Aubigny convinced Stephen to negotiat e wi
1 CONC th Henry to prevent further strife within the country.  By the Tre aty of Winchester (Treay of Wallingford)  in November 1153, Henry was recognized as King Stephen's heir.  The treaty was ratified by Archbis hop of Canterbury Theo
1 CONC bald at Winchester in December 1153.  The ratifi cation was witnessed by 14 bishops and 11 earls.   On Stephen's death in 1154 Henry became King of England as Henry II. Henry's empire now s tretched from the Cheviot Hills of Scotl
1 CONC and all the way down through E ngland, across the English Channel to Normandie, Anjou and into Aquita ine, the heart of which was at Anjou, not England.  Henry viewed Engla nd as a souce of income that he could exploit for his con
1 CONC tinental ambi tions.  Henry, as king, carried on policies started by his grandfather .  Henry, slowly, persistently, and tirelessly brought new order into the life of the realm.  Henry II and Louis VII were reconciled in Augu st 1
1 CONC 154.  The empire was held together by diplomacy and force of arms .  During his reign, he relied heavily upon three men; Theobald - Arch bishop of Canterbury, Robert - Earl of Leicester and Thomas Becket.  H enry and Eleanor arriv
1 CONC ed in England at a harbour south of New Forest a nd headed to Winchester and then on to London.  At the Christmas Cour t of 1154, Archbishop of Canterbury Theobald presented Thomas Becket t o King Henry.  His friendship with the K
1 CONC ing became very friendly and h e was made Chancellor by Henry II.  Thomas and Henry often played ches s together, dined together and hunted.
1 CONT
1 CONT As Kink, Henry was not content with the surveilance of local developme nts by the officers of his government.  He was constantly on the road .  He was on the watch, inquiring, inspecting and prodding his royal s ervants to greater
1 CONC  altertness.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry's domain included the direct rule over England and southern Wale s and a claim to the overlordship of northern Wales.  In Henry's Engla nd, the King was supreme and answerable only to God.  There was no par liament and gover
1 CONC nment was carried out by the King and his Council of Lords, both spirtual and temporal.  England was divided into shires, e ach under the control of a sheriff (shire-reeve).  The Church administ ered its own courts and dominated m
1 CONC ost education.  One of Henry's firs t acts as King was to recall the ministers of his grandfather who had been dismissed by Stephen.  Henry's early years as king found him cont rolling the rebellious Barons who had used the chaos 
1 CONC of the civil war to fortify their homes and illegally control their territories.  The c astles they built are now known as the 'adulterine castles'.   In Scot land and Wales Henry stamped his authority and began the process of su 
1 CONC bduing Ireland.  He sought papal permission for his plans to invade Ir eland.  But soon, other matters on the continent diverted his attentio n.  Once at Caen, his famous temper was unleased while addressing the issue of the King 
1 CONC of Scotland.  He broke out in abusive language again st Richard du Hommet for speaking in the King of Scotlands favor (ref : W.C. Warren, 'Henry II', 1973).  He encouraged the development of ju ries of local men in the investigati
1 CONC on of crimes, and trial of those a ccused by royal justices.  His writs to sheriffs improved the disposit ion of claims over possession of property and benefices, thereby disco uraging local self-help of violent ejection and usurp
1 CONC ation.  Henry too k measures to ensure that coins bearing his image, which continued to be issued throughout the reigns of his sons were accepted as sound cur rency throughout Christendom.  He was passionate about justice in his r
1 CONC ealm.  He was the first king to attempt to break down the feudal syst em of government by bring its countless independent jurisdictions int o subjection to one uniform judicial administration.  He divided the c ountry into adminis
1 CONC trative regions and instituted legal courts wehre h is justices administered justice via assize courts.  On occassion as h e traveled the country, he would preside at a court and his judgement s are reputed to have been quite just
1 CONC .  He began the process of replac ing trial by ordeal with trial by jury.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1155, Henry felt confident enough to leave the administration of hi s realm in the hands of his chancellor and justiciars.  He marched thr ough East Anglia to York and besieged several castles in the area.  I n March of 1155, H
1 CONC enry returned from his foray into East Anglia and is sued charters to the canons of Holy Trinity and Christ Church, witness ed by Eleanor and Robert de Lucy.  At this point, it was Henry's inten t that his son William would acceed
1 CONC  to England and his son Henry woul d acceed to the duchy of Anjou.  It is likly that at this time he als o acknowledged his illigitimate son Geoffrey.  Also, in 1155, Pope Adr ian IV issued the papal 'Bull Laudabilitier' ("Ireland
1 CONC  and all the isl ands upon which Christ, the Sun of Justice, has shone, belong to the j urisdiction of the Blessed Peter and the Holy Roman Church"), which na med Henry as 'overlord' and granted him dispensation to invade Irelan d
1 CONC  and bring the Irish Church under the control of the Church of Rome .  He considered giving Ireland to his younger brother William, but th is was opposed by his mother just prior to her death.  The pope also f orwarded an Emerald 
1 CONC Ring to Henry II so as to influence him to extend the boundaries of the chruch.
1 CONT
1 CONT The plans for Ireland were soon layed on the back burner, as Henry's b rother Geoffrey of Brittany taking up arms against Henry in hopes of e nforcing his claims in Maine and Anjou.  On 5 February 1156, Henry na d Louis met on the
1 CONC  Norman border where Henry paid homage for Hormandie , Anjou and Aquitaine.  Henry then spent the next few months bringing his brother Geoffrey to submission.  After Easter of 1157, Henry bega n to plan a new campaign against Owai
1 CONC n Gwynedd - Prince of North Wale s who was threatening Chester.  The campaign went bad for Henry and h e negotiated a truce with Owain Rhuddlan.  In 1158 he vigorously reorg anized the Exchequer.  In 1159, Henry assesembled forces
1 CONC  and laid sieg e to the city of Toulouse and met Louis VII who took charge of the cit ies defenses.  Henry eventually withdrew and by September of 1159 aban doned the campaign.  Henry laid siege to Castillon-sur-Agen in 1161. In O
1 CONC ctober of 1161, Henry met again with Louis VII at Freteval and the y made peace with Louis VII ceding the Vexin to Henry.  At Christmas 1 161, Henry decided on Becket as the new Archbishop of Canterbury.  Bec ket vigoursly protest
1 CONC ed the appointment, but Henry ignored his argumen ts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry is most famous for his quarrels with his friend Thomas Becket (w hom Henry at times was fond of teasing and mocking), the Archbishop o f Canterbury (ca. May 1162), over the subject of Ecclesiastical court s (ref: W.C. Warren
1 CONC , 'Henry II', 1973).  Henry failed to anticipate th at once Becket was named Archbishop that he would become an ardent cha mpion of the ecclesiastical over the royal perogative, Beckets former domain as Chancellor.  Between 1160 a
1 CONC nd 1164, Henry began to take advi ce from his mother Matilda regarding his relationship with Becket.  I n October of 1163, Henry and Becket had their first clash at Westminst er over the jurisdiction of ecclesiastical courts.  In 
1 CONC 1164 Henry's Co nstitutions of Clarendon (written by Henry's lawyers)  tried to bring the church into line with the state and one statement required that a member of the church should be tried in a state court and not in a chu rch
1 CONC  one.  The Constitutions spelled a completed defeat for Thomas Beck et (who refused to put his seal to the constitution) and he quickly wi thdrew to Winchester and after 8 months he was ordered to stand trial for 'contempt of the 
1 CONC King's person' before a Royal Court at Northampto n Castle.  Eventually, Henry ordered that a charge of treason be levie d against Becket. The rift between Henry and Becket forced Becket (wh o ws granted asylum by Louis VII at the
1 CONC  Cistercian Abbey of Pontigny i n Burgundy) to leave England from 1164 until 1170.  During that time L ouis arranged twelve meetings between Becket and Henry of which ten ac tually occured.  None of the meetings produced any agree
1 CONC ment between H enry and Becket.
1 CONT Henry moved on the Welsh in 1165, but by October of that year was bac k in England having failed to subdue them again.  Henry was also respo nsible for bringing in new legal reforms including in 1166, the Assiz e of Clarendon whic
1 CONC h started the jury system.  In March of 1166, Henr y crossed from Southampton to Falaise.  He marched on Maine destroyin g castles of the various local barons and crushing their resistance. During this period, he quelled a rebelli
1 CONC on by William Tallefer, one o f Eleanor's uncles in Aquitaine.  In July of 1166, he turned his atten tion towards Brittany and deposed Conan IV.  At Rennes, he took posses sion of Bretagne in the name of his son Geoffrey.  At abou
1 CONC t this time , Henry presented his son Henry to the Poitevins as their future Duke .  Also, in 1166, Dermot McMurrough, King of Leinster appealed to Kin g Henry for help in opposition to a confederation of Irish Kings.  Hen ry sent
1 CONC  a force led by Richard de Clare - Earl of Pembroke, thereby be ginning the English settlement of Ireland.  Between 1166 and 1170, Hen ry II ordered that Windsor Castle be rebuilt of stone.  On 6 January 1 169, Henry and his two s
1 CONC ons Henry and Richard met with Louis VII to en shrine the peace treay of Montmirail.  The treaty of peace provided th at young Lord Henry would receive Normandie and Anjou, that Lord Richa rd would receive Aquitaine as a vassel of
1 CONC  the French crown and that Lo rd Geoffrey would recive Brittany as a vassel of his his older brothe r Henry & in right of his future wife Constance.  Finally the betrothe l of Lord Richard and Alice (Alys) of France would take pla
1 CONC ce immediat ely.  On 22 July 1170, Henry and Becket met at Fretival near 'Traitor s Meadow', to no avail.  In October of 1170, Henry II and Becket met f or the last time at Chaumont near Amboise..  When in 1170 Becket retur ned to
1 CONC  England, an outburst of anger, "will no one rid me of this turb ulent priest", on 25 December 1170  (ref. F. Barlow, 'Thomas Becket', 1986), by Henry led to four knights, Reginald FitzUrse, William de Tra cy, Hugh de Morville and
1 CONC  Richard de Brito to slip away Bures, make the ir way to England and to murder Becket at Canterbury on 29 December 11 70.  Soon Henry was reviled throughout christendom.  He learned of Bec kets death on either 31 December 1170 or 
1 CONC 1 January 1171 at Argentau. He was paralyzed with horror and remorse.  Although Henry was cleared of any direct involvement in the crime, he did penance before the Cath edral Avrances in Normandie. Henry would allow no one to comf
1 CONC ort him ( ref: W.C. Warren, 'Henry II', 1973).
1 CONT
1 CONT Also in 1170, Henry denounced the son of his kinsman Earl Robert of Gl oucester, Bishop of Worcester Roger as a 'traitor' (ref: W.C. Warren, 'Henry II', 1973).  In 1171,  Henry had already sailed for Waterford w ith a great armada
1 CONC  of some 400 vessels carrying as many as 4,000 men o f whom 500 were knights.  He reserved Dublin, Waterford and Wexford fo r himself, but allowed Richard 'Strongbow' de Clare to do homage for L einster.  Henry II then created Ric
1 CONC hard 'Strongbow' as Earl of Strigui l and Buckingham and Lord of Tudenham and Leinster, in Ireland.  Henr y was named Lord of Ireland where he remained for six months until th e spring of 1172.  In 1171, he also granted Hugh de La
1 CONC cy, the county o f Meath and appointed him the ruler of Ireland in the Kings name as h e did not entirely trust Strongbow.  By 1172, Eleanor was in possessio n of a growing grudge against her husband Henry.  During the Christma s 
1 CONC season of 1172, Henry and count Humbert III of Mauriene concluded a n agreement regarding Humberts lands and a possible marriage between H enry's son John and Humberts daughter.  Henry and his sons also quarre lled which led to co
1 CONC nflicts in England and abroad, including a rebelli on by his Barons in 1173.  By 1173, Henry believed that his own securi ty demanded the imprisonment of his wife Eleanor and he seems to have anticipated the disloyalty of his four
1 CONC  sons.  Some writers have sugges ted that Henry attributed the rebellion of his sons to divine retribut ion for his part in the murder of Becket.  John remained loyal to his father during the rebellion.  By 1174, Henry had put dow
1 CONC n the rebellio ns of his sons.  He treated them generously at an armistice at Montlou is near Tours in September of 1174.
1 CONT In 1175, Henry and his son Henry made a tour of England that included the Welsh marshes and York.   In 1173, Young Henry and Richard moved a gainst their father and his succession plans, trying to secure the lan ds they were promi
1 CONC sed. The King's changing and revising of his inherit ance nurtured jealousy in his offspring, which turned to aggression. W hile both Young Henry and, Richard were relatively strong in France, t hey still lacked the manpower and e
1 CONC xperience to trouble their father u nduly.  The Church remained loyal to Henry II during the hostilities. The King crushed this first rebellion between March and May of 1173 an d was fair in his punishment.  During the twelve mont
1 CONC hs of the rebelli on, Henry II remained cool and shifted from crisis to crisis. Richard for example, lost half of the revenue allowed to him as Count of Poito u.  In September of 1173, Henry was faced with another revolt on the p 
1 CONC art of William of Angouleme, the de Lusignan brothers Guy and Geoffre y and Geoffrey de Rancon.  Henry moved on Poitou with a large army of Brabantine mercenaries and quickly quelled the revolt there as well a s in Brittany.  The 
1 CONC Church remaine loyal to Henry II during this perio d, gaining him great advantage.  On 25 September 1173, Henry met with his three sons and Louis VII at Gisors in an effort to bring a peacefu l solutions to their problems.  Henry'
1 CONC s three sons however rejected hi s terms.  In late 1173, England was invaded from the north by William 'the Lion' Stewart, but the invasion was repelled by Henry II's force s led by Richard de Lucy and Humphrey de Bohun.  In 1174,
1 CONC  Henry organi zed and held a festival and tournament at Beaucaire in order to foste r a reconciliation between Alfonso II of Aragon and Raymond V of Toulo use, though neither attended.  As  many an 10,000 knights are said to have 
1 CONC participated in the tournament. On 12 May 1174, in the face of re bellion by his sons and wife, Henry marched into Poitiers and dismisse d Eleanor's courts.  Henry dismantled Eleanors court at Poitiers and t ook his daughters Joan
1 CONC na, Marguerite and Alys of France, Emma of Anjou , Constance of Brittany and Alice of Maurienne from the ducal palace .  In 1176, Henry divided England into six districts, each with three judges to hold courts and levy taxes.  Hen
1 CONC ry became the master of the castle at Chinon with its tall towers overlooking the Vienne valley wh ich to him became a symbol of his power.
1 CONT The struggle between Henry and his natural sons continued to grow as t he years went by and continued for some sixteen years till the time o f Henry's death.  At times they fought amongst themselves, while at ot her times they wer
1 CONC e united against him.  By 1177, the conflict betwee n his sons flew into open conflict and Henry returned to Normandie.  H is sons Richard and Geoffrey of Brittany seemed to have a fatalistic a pproach to their lives.  Henry soon 
1 CONC sent envoys to his two sons to dis cuss peace and the envoys were murdered.  In June 1180, Henry crossed from Portsmouth to France and met with Philip at Gisors and renewed th e peace of 1177.  In 1182, the Plantagenet children's 
1 CONC aggression turne d inward. Young Henry, Richard and their brother Geoffrey all began fi ghting each other for their father's possessions on the continent.  Th e young Henry joined his brother Geoffrey in alliance with Bertran de B
1 CONC orn against Richard over his harsh rule in Aquitaine. The situation w as exacerbated by French rebels and the French King, Philip Augustus. This was the most serious threat to come from within the family yet, a nd the King faced t
1 CONC he dynastic tragedy of civil war.  Henry II rode wo uth to intervene, but the brothers refused to listen.  In 1182, Henry II stopped the allowance for his son Henry, the young King.  However, on 11 June 1183, Henry the Young King 
1 CONC died. The uprising, which had be en built around the Prince, promptly collapsed and the remaining broth ers returned to their individual lands. Henry quickly occupied the reb el region of Angouleme to keep the peace.  In late 1183
1 CONC , Henry summone d Eleanor out of captivity and ordered her to come to Normandie.  The y appeared in public together.  He also sought to revise the provision s of the Treay of Montirail which prompted him to nickname his son Joh n 
1 CONC as 'Lackland'.  When Henry 'the young King' died in Jun 1183, Aquita ine was to revert to John, but Richard had no intention of allowing th at to happen.  Henry told John, that it was his if he could take it. In December 1183, Hen
1 CONC ry II met with Phillippe of France and after agre eing to pay Margaret of France (the young Kings widow) an annual pensi on and in return Phillippe allowed Henry II to retain control of the V exin and the region surrounding Gisors
1 CONC .  Henry subsequently ordered Ri chard to give up Aquitaine in return for Anjou and Normandie.  Richar d refused and Henry soon ordered his son John to invade Aquitaine.  Jo hn enlisted the support of his brother Geoffrey in the e
1 CONC nterprise.
1 CONT The final battle between Henry's Princes came in 1184. Geoffrey of Bri ttany and John of Ireland, the youngest brothers, had been promised Aq uitaine, which belonged to elder brother Richard. Geoffrey and John in vaded, but Richar
1 CONC d had been controlling an army for almost 10 years an d was an accomplished military commander. Richard expelled his fickle brothers and they would never again face each other in combat, largel y because Geoffrey died two years la
1 CONC ter, leaving only Richard and John .  Both were to become kings of England, Richard (the Lion Heart) and John.  In 1185, in the face of John's failure to develope any leadersh ip talent while in Ireland, Henry appointed John de Co
1 CONC urcy as Viceroy for Ireland.  Conflicts with Eleanor and Henry's sons, helped by Phili ppe II of France continued until Henry died in 1189 at Chinon in Franc e.  In 1187, Phillippe had marched into Berry and Richard and Henry I I 
1 CONC joined forces to resist Phillip.  In April 1187, Henry II divided hi s army into four divisions, Richard was in charge of the southern cont ingent, John was given a division which was to operate within Richard s command and the ot
1 CONC her two were commanded by William de Manville & He nry II's illegitimate son Geoffrey.  On 23 June 1187 Henry II and Phil lippe reached a truce at Chateauraux (ref 'Ralph of Diceto').  Howeve r Richard soon deserted his father aga
1 CONC in and marched to Paris and alli ed himself with Phillip.  Henry II raised an army and left England fo r the last time on 11 July 1188, where he was attacked and subsequentl y withdrew to the area of Lemans.  On 30 August 1188, He
1 CONC nry invaded Fr ance and advanced through Mantes, Ivry-la-Bataille and Danville toward s Phllippe's headquarters.  Phillippe soon proposed a peace conferenc e at Gisors, with no result.  A second conference was held at Chatillo n-s
1 CONC ur-Indre also without result.  Finally a conference was held at Bon moulins on 18 November 1188 with Richard in attendance. Phillipp II re quested that Henry II grant his son Richard with Poitou, Touraine, Mai ne and Anjou and rec
1 CONC ognize Richard as his heir.  Henry rejected the pr oposal and Richard, in fear that he would be passed over in favor of h is younger brother John, threw himself at the knees of Phillippe.  A c easefire was agreed to until 13 Janua
1 CONC ry 1189.  Richard stormed out of the meeting and Henry sent his illegitimate son Geoffrey and William M arshall after Richard, to no avail.  Henry II and his son John were to gether at LeMans on 12 Jun 1189 as they faced Richard. 
1 CONC  Henry II was f orced into a surrender at Tours of 4 July 1189, whereby he renounced t he suzeranity of the Auvergne.  At the same time he gave up the lordsh ips of Gracay and Issoudun.  On 6 July 1189, he collapsed from fever a n
1 CONC d died at Chinon.  He is said to have remarked 'Shame on a beaten Kin g, Shame on a beaten King'.  As he lay dying, he was presented with a list of the Lords that had deserted him in favor of Richard.  At the t op of the list was 
1 CONC the name of his dearest son, John.  (Johns switch t o Richard is dubious as he had been with Henry at LeMans only three we eks earlier).  When Henry died, his illigitimate son Geoffrey was wit h him and Henry is said to have remar
1 CONC ked 'Thy are my true son' to Geof frey.  His body was removed to the abbey at Fontevrault, where Willia m Marshall carried the body.  There, Richard came to view his father .  Henry at the time of his death was styled as 'a firm a
1 CONC nd wise princ e, who brought peace and prosperity to a troubled realm'.  The formida ble English tax system owes its existence to Henry II.  Henry was succ eeded by Richard, his third and oldest surviving natural son.  One sou rce
1 CONC  references Henry and Eleanor sons Henry, Richard, Geoffrey and Joh n as the 'Devils Brood'.
1 CONT
1 CONT WILL of Henry II
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry, by the grace of God, King of England, Duke of Normandie and Aqu itaine, Count of Anjou, to King Henry, to Richard, Geoffrey, and John , my sons, to Archbishops, Bishops, Abbots, Archdeacons, Deans, Earls , Barons, Justices,
1 CONC  Sheriffs, &c. and all other my faithful subjects,a s well clerks and laymen of my territories, within and beyond the seas , greeting. Know ye, that at Waltham, in the presence of R. Bishop of Winchester; J. Bishop of Norwich; G. 
1 CONC Chancellor, my son; Master Walte r de Constantiis, Archdeacon of Oxford; Godfrey de Lucy, Archdeacon o f Derby; Ralph de Glanville; Hugh de Morewic; Ralph fitz Stephen, Cham berlain; and William Rufo; I have made division of some 
1 CONC part of my mon ey in this manner: To ---, &c. To the religious houses of England MMMM M marks of silver, to be distributed by the hands of R Archbishop of C anterbury; R Bishop of Winchester; G. Bishop of Ely; and J. Bishop ofN or
1 CONC wich; and Ralph de Glanville, Justiciar of England.To the religious houses of the land of the Earl of Anjou, my father, M marks of silver ; towards the marriage of poor and free women of Normandie wanting ai d C marks of gold, to 
1 CONC be distributed by the Archbishop of Rouen, and t he Bishops of Bayeaux, Avaranches, Sagiensis. Toward the marriage of p oor and free women of the land of my father, the Earl of Anjou, C mark s of gold, to be distributed by the Bis
1 CONC hops of Main and Anjou. This di stribution I have made at the place before written, in the year of th e Incarnation 1182. And I charge you, my sons, by the fealty you owe m e, and the oath ye have sworn to me, that ye cause it to 
1 CONC be firmly an d inviolably kept; and whoever shall oppose or contravene it, may he i ncur the indignation and anger of Almighty God, and mine and God's mal ediction. And I command you, the Archbishops and Bishops, by the oathy e ha
1 CONC ve sworn to me, and the fealty ye owe to me and to God, that ye so lemnly, in your Synods, with lighted candles, excommunicate, and caus e to be excommunicated, all such as may presume to infringe my distrib ution. And know ye tha
1 CONC t our Lord the Pope has confirmed this my distri bution, under his hand and seal, on pain of anathema. ( Ref: http://ho me.earthlink.net/~kb60/plantagenet06.htm: From Testamenta Vetusta, Bei ng Illustrations from Wills, of Manners
1 CONC , Customs, &c., vol. 1, pp. 1-4 . Nicholas Harris Nicolas, Barrister at Law, Fellow of the Society ofA ntiquaries. London: Nichols & Son, 1826. )
1 CONT
1 CONT Additional information on Henry and his reign may be found includes:
1 CONT
1 CONT 01.) John Harvey, The Plantagenets.
1 CONT 02.) Timbs & Gunn, Abbeys, Castles & Ancient Halls of England & Wales.
1 CONT 03.) Edward Burman, The Templars, Knights of God.
1 CONT 04.) Mike Salter, Castles and Moated Castles of Warwickshire.
1 CONT 05.) Preston Williams, Illustrations of Masonry.
1 CONT 06.) Derek Renn, Norman Castles, 1968.
1 CONT 07.) M.T.Clanchy, England and its Rulers.
1 CONT 08.) John Fines, Who's Who in the Middle Ages.
1 CONT 09.) Stephen Howarth, The Knights Templar.
1 CONT 10.) Colin Platt, Medieval England.
1 CONT 11.) Alan & Veronica Palmer, Pimlico Chronology of Britsh History.
1 CONT 12.) John Gillingham, The Life and Times of Richard I, 1974.
1 CONT 13.) Duc de Castries, The lives of the Kings and Queens of France.
1 CONT 14.) Micheal Baigent, Richard Leigh and Henry Lincoln, The Holy Blooda nd the Holy Grail.
1 CONT 15.) H.W.C. Davis, 'Regesta Regum Anglo-Normanorum 1066-1154, 3 (1968) .
1 CONT Henry II was the first of three sons born to Matilda and
1 CONT Geoffrey of Anjou on 4 March 1133. Raised in his father's
1 CONT dominion, he did not visit English shores until 1142. At that
1 CONT time England, split in a vicious civil war, was divided into
1 CONT areas controlled by Matilda, the daughter of Henry I, and those
1 CONT controlled by Stephen, grandson of William the Conqueror. The
1 CONT nine-year old Henry returned quickly to the safety of Anjou.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1147, as a fourteen-year-old boy, Henry returned to England
1 CONT with a small band of mercenaries to take up his mother's cause
1 CONT in the civil war. The excursion was against his mother's wishes
1 CONT and better judgement. When Henry found himself out of money,
1 CONT Matilda refused to help him. So, with the brashness that would
1 CONT be Henry's trademark, he applied to his enemy, Stephen, for
1 CONT help; and with the characteristic lack of ruthlessness that
1 CONT would be Stephen's undoing, he gave Henry the money to pay off
1 CONT his mercenaries and go home. By 1151 Henry was lord of Normandy
1 CONT and Anjou. The following year he married Eleanor of Aquitaine,
1 CONT one of the most desirable women in Europe. Eleanor was recently
1 CONT divorced from Louis VII of France, after fourteen years of
1 CONT marriage and failure to bear him son.
1 CONT
1 CONT Midwinter of 1153, Henry crossed the Channel and surprised
1 CONT Stephen. The English barons were, by this time, convinced that
1 CONT the only way to end the bitter war was to have Stephen declare
1 CONT Henry as his successor. The death of Stephen's son, Eustace,
1 CONT brought the end of Stephen's resistance. The Treaty of
1 CONT Westminster left Stephen on the throne, but declared Henry his
1 CONT successor. When Stephen died, less than a year later, Henry
1 CONT ascended the throne unopposed. Now, with a kingdom that
1 CONT stretched from the Scottish border to the Pyrenees, he was the
1 CONT greatest prince in Europe. But his heart remained in Anjou, the
1 CONT land of his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Throughout the first years of Henry's reign, his attention was
1 CONT divided between England and Anjou. He first set out to destroy
1 CONT those lands and castles granted without royal license during
1 CONT Stephen's reign. He also reestablished overlordship of Scotland
1 CONT and Wales which was a relationship lost during Stephen's reign.
1 CONT His attention soon turned back to his homeland and an attempt
1 CONT to establish overlordship of Toulouse, a region included in his
1 CONT wife's inheritance. However, the most significant (and
1 CONT certainly most famous) story of Henry's reign began in 1162.
1 CONT That year Archbishop Theobald of Canterbury died. This very
1 CONT important clerical post was open for over a year, when in June,
1 CONT 1162, Henry appointed Thomas Becket Archbishop of Canterbury.
1 CONT Becket at the time was Chancellor and well respected, but a
1 CONT very good friend to the King, maybe too much so, his critics
1 CONT claimed. He was also not known for his charity. A story told by
1 CONT William FitzStephen, a friend and biographer of Becket,
1 CONT illustrates not only Becket's friendship with Henry, but his
1 CONT reputation as less that charitable:
1 CONT
1 CONT One day they were riding together through the streets of
1 CONT London. It was a hard winter and the king noticed an old man
1 CONT coming towards them, poor and clad in a thin ragged coat. 'Do
1 CONT you see that man?' said the king. 'Yes, I see him', replied
1 CONT Becket. 'How poor his is, how frail, and how scantily clad!'
1 CONT said the king. 'Would it not be an act of charity to give him a
1 CONT thick warm coat?' 'It would indeed; and right that you should
1 CONT attend to it my king.' [:ITAL]
1 CONT
1 CONT But the world underestimated Thomas Becket. Fully aware of
1 CONT public opinion, Becket decided he would be a good Archbishop,
1 CONT perhaps even a great one. Some contemporaries claim he actually
1 CONT had a conversion. Whatever the reason, Becket went out of his
1 CONT way to oppose the King. It did not take Henry long to regret
1 CONT his decision. The issue that brought Henry and Becket to the
1 CONT brink of their destinies was and old one--what to do with a
1 CONT churchman that breaks the laws of England. Like many layman,
1 CONT Henry wanted criminous clerks defrocked and tried by a lay
1 CONT court. Becket, of course, felt clerics should be tried in
1 CONT ecclesiastical courts. At Clarendon, Henry presented the
1 CONT bishops of England, led by Archbishop Becket, with a statement
1 CONT of the King's customary rights over the church. Becket argued
1 CONT for two days, but finally, with the bishops in tow, gave in. No
1 CONT sooner was the ink dry, then Becket changed his mind. In
1 CONT desperation, Henry had Becket arrested on false charges, found
1 CONT guilty, and forced to forfeit all estates. In despair, Becket
1 CONT fled across the Channel.
1 CONT
1 CONT For the next five years Becket remained in exile and Henry
1 CONT concentrated on other matters. He conquered Brittainy and
1 CONT overhauled the English legal system. (His reforms were
1 CONT revolutionary. The father of English common law, Henry made
1 CONT innovations manifest today in the form of localized and complex
1 CONT government.) But in 1170, Becket returned to England. Tales of
1 CONT his outrageous behavior and continued opposition to the King
1 CONT wasted no time in finding their way to Henry in Normandy. "Will
1 CONT no one rid me of this turbulent priest?" Henry allegedly
1 CONT shouted. True or not, Henry undoubtedly did mumble some words
1 CONT of frustration, and in response four of Henry's knights went
1 CONT looking for Becket. They found him at Canterbury Cathedral
1 CONT where Becket had gone to hear evening vespers. They first
1 CONT struck him with the flat of a sword. According to William
1 CONT FitzStephen, the warning, "Fly, you are a dead man," was
1 CONT shouted by one of the attackers, but Becket resisted and was
1 CONT brutally murdered.
1 CONT
1 CONT By all contemporary accounts, Henry appears to have been
1 CONT horrified by the actions of his knights. A friend of the king,
1 CONT Arnulf, Bishop of Lisieux wrote the following:
1 CONT
1 CONT The king burst into loud lamentations and exchanged his
1 CONT royal robes for sackcloth and ashes, behaving more like a
1 CONT friend than the sovereign of the dead man. At times he fell
1 CONT into a stupor, after which he would again utter groans and
1 CONT cries louder and more bitter than before. For three whole days
1 CONT he remained shut in his chamber and would neither take food nor
1 CONT admit anyone to comfort him, until it seemed from the excess of
1 CONT his grief that he had determined to contrive his own
1 CONT death.[:ITAL]
1 CONT
1 CONT While Henry mourned, the rest of Christiandom was outraged.
1 CONT Becket, canonized in record time, became a symbol of resistance
1 CONT against oppressive authority. Henry did penitence for his role
1 CONT in Becket's death, but he ordered the Bishop of London to
1 CONT declare in a sermon that he had not commanded Becket's death.
1 CONT After the storm died down it became apparent that despite the
1 CONT scandal, Henry was at the height of his power. The real threat
1 CONT would come from his family.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was plagued with rebellious sons. Henry the Younger, the
1 CONT oldest son, was actually crowned successor in 1169, but wanted
1 CONT more than just a title. Richard and John felt left out all
1 CONT together, and spurred on by Eleanor, Henry's wife, launched one
1 CONT plot after another. However, the Young King Henry died in 1183,
1 CONT leaving Richard the oldest surviving son, poised for the
1 CONT succession. But Henry's preference for John was obvious.
1 CONT Richard, pushed to the point of open rebellion, joined with
1 CONT Philip II of France in an attempt to destroy the Angevin empire
1 CONT and Henry. In July, 1189, with his health failing, Henry
1 CONT accepted a humiliating peace. When given a list of names of
1 CONT those who had fought against him, he was shocked to find John's
1 CONT name among them. He turned his face away and according his his
1 CONT chroniclers said, "Enough; now let things go as they may; I
1 CONT care no more for myself or for the world ... Shame, shame on a
1 CONT conquered king." A month later Henry died.
1 CONT
1 CONT Biographical information from The Lives of the Kings and Queens
1 CONT of England, ed. Antonia Frasier , The Oxford Illustrated
1 CONT History of the British Monarchy, ed. John Cannon and Ralph
1 CONT Griffiths, and The Oxford Book of Royal Anecdotes, ed.
1 CONT Elizabeth Longford.
1 CONT NOTE
1 CONT GEDCOM created by TMG...
0 @N6000000004533040243@ NOTE Henry II was born at Le Mans in 1133. He was the eldest son of theEmpress Matilda, daughter of Henry I, by her second marriage toGeoffrey the Fair of Anjou. His parents' marriage was tempestous, andboth parties were glad when poli
1 CONC tics brought a separation, withMatilda going to England to fight King Stephen, and Geoffrey ofNormandy to win a heritage for young Henry.
1 CONT
1 CONT He first came to England at the age of nine when his mother made herdramatic escape from Oxford where she was besieged by Stephen, acrossthe ice and snow, dressed all in white, to welcome him at Wallingford.His next visit, when he
1 CONC  was fourteen, showed his character: herecruited a small army of mercenaries to cross over and fight Stephenin England, but failed so miserably in the execution of his plans thathe ended up borrowing money from Stephen to get back
1 CONC  home. A thirdexpedition, two years later, was almost as great a failure. Henry wasnot a soldier, his were skills of administration and diplomacy;warfare bored and sometimes frightened him. For the meanwhile he nowconcentrated on 
1 CONC Normandy, of which his father had made him jointruler. In 1151, the year of his father's death, he went to Paris to dohomage to Louis VII for his duchy. There he met Queen Eleanor, and shefell in love with him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was by no means averse. To steal a king's wife does a great dealfor the ego of a young duke; he was as lusty as she, and late in theirlives he was still ardently wenching with 'the fair Rosamund'Clifford, and less salubrious
1 CONC  girls with names like 'Bellebelle';finally, she would bring with her the rich Duchy of Aquitaine, whichshe held in her own right. With this territory added to those he hopedto inherit and win, his boundaries would be Scotland in 
1 CONC the north, andthe Pyrenees in the south.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was, apart from his prospects, a 'catch' for any woman. He wasintelligent, had learned Latin and could read and possibly write;immensely strong and vigorous, a sportsman and hard rider who lovedtravel; emotional and passiona
1 CONC te, prone to tears and incredible rages;carelessly but richly dressed, worried enough in later life to concealhis baldness by careful arrangement of his hair, and very concernednot to grow fat.
1 CONT
1 CONT But now he was in the prime of youth, and in 1153, when he landed witha large force in Bristol, the world was ready to be won. He quicklygained control of the West Country and moved up to Wallingford for acrucial battle with Steph
1 CONC en. This was avoided, however, because inthepreparations for the battle Henry fell from his horse three times,a bad omen. Henry himself was not superstitious -- he was the reverse,a cheerful blasphemer -- but he disliked battles a
1 CONC nd when his anxiousadvisers urged him to heed the omen, he willingly agreed to parleyprivately with Stephen. The conference was a strange occasion: therewere only two of them there, at the narowest point of the Thames, withHenry o
1 CONC n one bank and Stephen on the other. None the less, they seemto have come to an agreement to take negotiations further.
1 CONT
1 CONT That summer Stephen's son died mysteriously, and Eleanor bore Henry anheir (about the same time as an English whore Hikenai produced hisfaithful bastard Geoffrey). The omens clearly showed what was soonconfirmed between the two --
1 CONC  that when Stephen died, Henry should rulein his place. A year later Stephen did die, and in December 1154,Henry and Eleanor were crowned in London.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was only 21, but he soon showed his worth, destroying unlicensedcastles, and dispersing the foreign mercenaries. He gave even-handedjustice, showing himself firm, but not unduly harsh. A country rackedby civil war sighed wit
1 CONC h relief. Only two major difficulties appeared:first Henry's failure in his two Welsh campaigns in 1157 and 1165,when guerilla tactics utterly defeated and on the first occasionnearly killed him; second was the reversal of his fri
1 CONC endship forBecket when he changed from being Chancellor to Archbishop ofCanterbury in 1162.
1 CONT
1 CONT The quarrel with Becket was linked with the King's determination tocontinue his grandfather's reform of the administration of justice inthe country. He was anxious for a uniform pattern, operated by royaljustices, to control the c
1 CONC orrupt, ill-administered and unequal localsystems operated by barons and churchmen. At Clarendon in 1166 andNorthampton in 1176, he got his council's agreeemnt to a series ofmeasures which established circuits of royal justices de
1 CONC aling with thewidest range of criminal activities. The method of operation wasnovel, too, relying on a sworn jury of inquest of twelve men. Thoughnot like a modern jury, in that they were witnesses rather thanassessors, the assize
1 CONC  juries were the ancestors of the modern Englishlegal system.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry travelled constantly, and much of the time in his Contninentalterritories, for there were constant rebellions to deal with, usuallyinspired or encouraged by Louis of France. Henry was determined tokeep the integrity of his e
1 CONC mpire, and to pass it on as a unity. To dothis was no small task, but in 1169 Henry held a conference with theKing of France which he hoped would achieve his objectives: he himselfagain did homage for Normandy, his eldest son Henr
1 CONC y did homage forAnjou, Maine and Brittany, and Richard for Aquitaine. The next year hehad young Henry crowned in his own lifetime. If anything couldpreserve the succession, surely this would, yet, in fact, it broughtall the troubl
1 CONC es in the world onto Henry's head, for he had given hissons paper domains, and had no intention that they should rule hisempire. Yet a man with a title does not rest until he has that title'spower.
1 CONT
1 CONT Late in 1171 Henry had a pleasant interlude in Ireland -- escapingfrom the world's condemnation for the murder of Becket. He spentChristmas at Dublin in a palace built for him out of wattles by theIrish.
1 CONT
1 CONT Meanwhile, Eleanor had been intriguing with her sons, urging them torevolt and demand their rights. Early in 1173 they trooped off to theFrench court, and with Louis joined in an attack on Normandy. Henryclamped Eleanor into priso
1 CONC n and went off to meet the new threat.Whilst he was busy meeting this, England was invaded from Flanders andScotland, and more barons who fancied a return of the warlord days ofStephen broke into revolt.
1 CONT
1 CONT Plainly it was St. Thomas's revenge, and there was no hope of dealingwith the situation without expiation. In July 1174 Henry returned toEngland, and went in pilgrim's dress to Canterbury. Through the townhe walked barefoot, leavi
1 CONC ng a trail of blood on the flinty stones, andwent to keep his vigil of a day and a night by the tomb, not evencoming out to relive himself. As he knelt, the assembled bishops andall the monks of Christchurch came to scourge him --
1 CONC  each giving himthree strokes, but some with bitterness in their hearts laying on withfive.
1 CONT
1 CONT It was worth it though, for the very morning his vigil ended Henry wasbrought the news that the King of Scotland had been captured. He movedquickly northwards, receving rebels' submission all the time. He metup with Geoffrey who h
1 CONC ad fought valiantly for him, and commented, 'Myother sons have proved themselves bastards, this one alone is my trueand legitimate son.'
1 CONT
1 CONT Returning to France, he quickly came to an agreement with Louis andhis three rebel sons, giving each a substantial income, though stillno share of power.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard set to work reducing the Duchy of Aquitaine to order, andquickly proved himself an able general who performed tremendous feats,such as capturing a fully manned and provisioned castle with threewalls and moats to defend it.
1 CONC  But the people were less easy to subdue-- they loved war for its own sake as their poet-leader, Bertrand deBorn, shows well in his works: '. . . I love to see amidst the meadowstents and pavilions spread; and it gives me great jo
1 CONC y to see drawn upon the field knights and horses in battle array; and it delights mewhen the scouts scatter people and herds in their path; and my heartis filled with gladness when I see strong castles besieged, and thestockades b
1 CONC roken and overwhelmed, and the warriors on the bank, girtabout by fosses, with a line of strong stakes, interlaced . . . Maces,swords, helms of different hues, shields that will be riven andshattered as soon as the fight begins; a
1 CONC nd many vassals struck downtogether; and the horses of the dead and wounded roving at random. Andwhen battle is joined, let all men of good lineage think of nought butthe breaking of heads and arms: I tell you I find no such savou
1 CONC r infood or in wine or in sleep as in hearing the shout "On! On!" fromboth sides, and the neighing of steeds that have lost their riders,and the cries of "Help! Help!"; and in seeing men great and small godown on the grass beyond 
1 CONC the fosses; in seeing at last the dead, withthe pennoned stumps of lances still in their sides.'
1 CONT
1 CONT These robust knights were actively encouraged by the young King Henry.He was handsome, charming and beloved of all, but also feckless andthoughtless -- far keener on tournaments and frivolity than theserious business of government
1 CONC . Then in the midle of his new rebellionhe caught disentery and shortly died. His devoted followers werethunderstruck -- one young lad actually pined to death -- and therebellion fizzled out.
1 CONT
1 CONT The young king was dead, but Henry, wary of previous errors, was notgoing to rush into making a new one. He called his favourite youngestson, John, to his side and ordered Richard to give his duchy into hisbrother's hands. Richard
1 CONC  -- his mother's favourite -- had madeAquitaine his home and worked hard to establish his control there; herefused to give his mother's land to anyone, unless it were back toEleanor herself.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry packed John off to Ireland (which he speedily turned againsthimself) whilst he arranged to get Eleanor out of her prison and bringher to Aquitaine to receive back the duchy. Meanwhile the new King ofFrance, Philip, was plann
1 CONC ing to renew the attack on Englishterritories, all the while the three, Henry, Richard, and Philip, weresupposed to be planning a joint crusade.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1188 Henry, already ill with the absessed anal fistula that was tocause him such an agonising death, refused pointblank to recogniseRichard as his heir. The crazy project for substituting John was atthe root of it all, though H
1 CONC enry may have deluded himself intothinking he was playing his usual canny hand.
1 CONT
1 CONT But diplomacy was giving way to the Greekest of tragedies. In June1189, Philip and Richard advanced on Henry at his birthplace in LeMans, and he was forced to withdraw with a small company of knights,showering curses on God. Inste
1 CONC ad of going to the safety of Normandy,he rode hard, his usual long distance, deep into Anjou. This worsenedhis physical condition and, in high fever, he made no effort to callup forces to his aid. Forced to meet Philip and Richard
1 CONC , he was so illhe had to be held on his horse whilst he deliriously mumbled hisabject agreement to their every condition for peace.
1 CONT
1 CONT Back in bed after his last conference he was brought the news thatJohn, for whom he had suffered all this, had joined the rebels' side.Two sons -- both rebels -- were dead, two sons -- both rebels --lived, and it was his bastard G
1 CONC eoffrey who now tended him in his lastsickness. There was not even a bishop in his suite to give him thelast rites. Over and again he cried out in agony "Shame! shame on avanquished king!"
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death the servants plundered him, leaving him in a shirt anddrawers. When the marshall came to arrange the burial he had toscratch around for garments in which to dress the body. A bit ofthreadbare gold edging from a clo
1 CONC ak was put around Henry's head torepresent his sovereignty.
1 CONT
1 CONT And yet Henry had forseen it all. According to Gerald of Wales, he hadlong before ordered a fresco for one of his rooms at Winchester: thepicture showed an eagle being pecked by three eaglets, and a fourthperched on his head, read
1 CONC y to peck out his eyes when the time shouldcome. [Source: Who's Who in the Middle Ages, John Fines, Barnes &Noble Books, New York, 1995]
0 @N6000000002244361331@ NOTE (The movie "Lion in Winter" was about Henry and Eleanor.  Don't know
1 CONT how accurate it is!)
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry II (born 1133, ruled 1154-89). The grandson of Henry I was the
1 CONT first Plantagenet king of England. His mother was Matilda, daughter of
1 CONT Henry I. His father was Geoffrey of Anjou, whom Matilda married after
1 CONT the death of her first husband, Emperor Henry V. Geoffrey was called
1 CONT Plantagenet for his habit of wearing in his cap a sprig of the broom
1 CONT plant, which in Latin is called planta genista.
1 CONT    Henry II was born in Le Mans, France, in March 1133. During his
1 CONT mother's conflict with Stephen for the English throne he was brought to
1 CONT England. Stephen eventually recognized his claim, and Henry became king
1 CONT of England in 1154 after Stephen's death.
1 CONT    Henry II held England and Normandy by his mother's right. From his
1 CONT father he inherited, as French fiefs, the important counties of Anjou,
1 CONT Maine, and Touraine. By his marriage with Eleanor of Aquitaine, whose
1 CONT marriage with the French king Louis VII had been annulled, he acquired
1 CONT Poitou, Guyenne, and Gascony, so that he held most of the British Isles
1 CONT and about half of France.
1 CONT    Henry II reestablished law and order after the anarchy of Stephen's
1 CONT reign. He improved the military service by permitting the barons to pay
1 CONT "shield money," or scutage, in place of serving in the army. With this
1 CONT he hired soldiers who would fight whenever and wherever he wished an
1 CONT important means of maintaining control over the powerful nobles of the
1 CONT land.
1 CONT    His greatest work was the reform of the law courts. He brought the
1 CONT Curia Regis (King's Court) into every part of England by sending
1 CONT learned judges on circuit through the land to administer the "king's
1 CONT justice." Thus gradually one system of law took the place of the many
1 CONT local customs that had been in use. He also established the grand jury.
1 CONT Now accusations could be brought by a body of representatives of the
1 CONT community against evildoers who were so powerful that no single
1 CONT individual dared accuse them.
1 CONT    The petit jury, also called petty or trial jury, substituted the
1 CONT weighing of evidence and testimony by sworn men for the old
1 CONT superstitious trial by combat or by ordeal. (See also Jury System.)
1 CONT Henry even attempted to bring churchmen who committed crimes under the
1 CONT jurisdiction of the king's courts, but the scandal caused by the murder
1 CONT of Archbishop Thomas Becket in the course of this quarrel forced him to
1 CONT give up this reform (see Becket).
1 CONT    Henry's last years were embittered by the rebellion of his sons,
1 CONT aided by Philip Augustus of France and by their mother, the
1 CONT unscrupulous Eleanor. The king old, sick, and discouraged had to
1 CONT consent to the terms demanded of him. When he saw the name of John, his
1 CONT favorite son, among those of his enemies, he exclaimed, "Now let all
1 CONT things go as they will; I care no more for myself, nor for the world."
1 CONT    Two days later he died, muttering, "Shame, shame on a conquered
1 CONT king." He was succeeded by his son Richard (see Richard, Kings of
1 CONT England). After Richard's death, in 1199, John came to the throne (see
1 CONT John of England).
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
1 CONT
1 CONT PLANTAGENET, HOUSE OF.  Also called the Angevin Dynasty, the House of
1 CONT Plantagenet ruled England from 1154 to 1485. The reign of the House of
1 CONT Plantagenet ended in the final battle of the Wars of the Roses, fought
1 CONT between the Lancaster and York families. The victor, Henry Tudor,
1 CONT became Henry VII and officially ended the Plantagenet reign with the
1 CONT founding of the House of Tudor.
1 CONT    The Plantagenet royal dynasty provided 14 kings descended from
1 CONT Geoffrey, 5th count of Anjou, and the empress Matilda, daughter of the
1 CONT English king Henry I. Plantagenet was not a hereditary surname and
1 CONT seems to have originated as a nickname for Count Geoffrey. The name is
1 CONT believed to refer either to the sprig of broom (Latin genista) that he
1 CONT wore in his hat or to his practice of planting broom to improve his
1 CONT hunting covers.
1 CONT    Historians differ on the naming of kings as Plantagenet, with some
1 CONT giving the name to Count Geoffrey's descendants beginning with Edward
1 CONT I. Richard Plantagenet, or Richard, 3rd duke of York, was the first to
1 CONT use the surname officially when he claimed the throne in 1460. The
1 CONT Plantagenet line became extinct when its legitimate male heir, Edward,
1 CONT Earl of Warwick, was executed in 1499.
1 CONT
1 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Excerpted from Compton's Interactive Encyclopedia
1 CONT Copyright (c) 1994, 1995 Compton's NewMedia, Inc. All Rights Reserved
