0 @N6000000001177485471@ NOTE
1 CONT Henry II (of England) (1133-89), king of England (1154-89), first monarch of the house of Anjou, or Plantagenet, an important administrative reformer, who was one of the most powerful European rulers of his time. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Born March 5, 1133, at Le Mans, France, Henry became duke of Normandy in 1151. The following year, on the death of his father, he inherited the Angevin territories in France. By his marriage in 1152 to Eleanor of Aquitaine, Henry 
1 CONC added vast territories in southwestern France to his possessions. Henry claimed the English kingship through his mother, Matilda (1102-67). She had been designated the heiress of Henry I but had been deprived of the succession by 
1 CONC her cousin, Stephen of Blois, who made himself king. In 1153 Henry defeated Stephen's armies in England and compelled the king to choose him as his successor; on Stephen's death, the following year, Henry became king. During the f
1 CONC irst few years of his reign Henry quelled the disorders that had developed during Stephen's reign, regained the northern counties of England, which had previously been ceded to Scotland, and conquered North Wales. In 1171-72 he be
1 CONC gan the Norman conquest of Ireland and in 1174 forced William the Lion, king of the Scots, to recognize him as overlord. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1164 Henry became involved in a quarrel with Thomas à Becket, whom he had appointed archbishop of Canterbury. By the Constitutions of Clarendon, the king decreed that priests accused of crimes should be tried in royal courts; B
1 CONC ecket claimed that such cases should be handled by ecclesiastical courts, and the controversy that followed ended in 1170 with Becket's murder by four of Henry's knights. Widespread indignation over the murder forced the king to r
1 CONC escind his decree and recognize Becket as a martyr. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Although he failed to subject the church to his courts, Henry's judicial reforms were of lasting significance. In England he established a centralized system of justice accessible to all freemen and administered by judges who trav
1 CONC eled around the country at regular intervals. He also began the process of replacing the old trial by ordeal with modern court procedures. 
1 CONT
1 CONT From the beginning of his reign, Henry was involved in conflict with Louis VII, king of France, and later with Louis's successor, Philip II, over the French provinces that Henry claimed. A succession of rebellions against Henry, h
1 CONC eaded by his sons and furthered by Philip II and by Eleanor of Aquitaine, began in 1173 and continued until his death at Chinon, France, on July 6, 1189. Henry was succeeded by his son Richard I, called Richard the Lion-Hearted. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "Henry II (of England)," Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c) 1993 Microsoft Corporation. Copyright (c) 1993 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation 
0 @N6000000008176865015@ NOTE [howard.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Plantagenet, surname, originally nickname, of the English royal house of Anjou or the Angevin dynasty, founded by Geoffrey IV, count of Anjou (1113-51), husband of Matilda (1102-67), daughter of King Henry I of England. The name i
1 CONC s derived from the Latin planta (“sprig”) and genista (“broom plant”), in reference to the sprig that Geoffrey always wore in his cap. Reigning from 1154 to 1485, the Plantagenet kings, in the main line of descent, were Henry II, 
1 CONC Richard I, John, Henry III, Edward I, Edward II, Edward III, and Richard II; through the house of Lancaster, Henry IV, Henry V, and Henry VI; and through the house of York, Edward IV, Edward V, and Richard III.
1 CONT "Plantagenet," Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c) 1994 Microsoft Corporation. Copyright (c) 1994 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation.[cownwall1.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Name Suffix:<NSFX> King of England
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Search The Website
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Hadrianus IV, Pope 
1 CONT Henry Plantagenet 
1 CONT Stephen I died in 1154 and Henry Plantagenet became Henry II of England. He was descended from William the Conqueror and through his mother, Matilda, from the Anglo-Saxons.
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Eleanor of Aquitaine who had been married to Louis VII of France and so Aquitaine passed from France to England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry II saw the need for central government. He wanted to strengthen his control of Scotland, Wales and Ireland. In 1157 Malcolm of Scotland paid homage to Henry and Scotland did not regain her independence until Richard I's reig
1 CONC n.
1 CONT
1 CONT Wales continued to be fractious and it was not until 1176 that the Welsh made their peace with Henry. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Ireland was next. 
1 CONT
1 CONT HENRY II (1133-1189)
1 CONT
1 CONT Aged 14 he was fighting for his mother's claim to the English throne
1 CONT In 1151 his father died and he became Count of Anjou and Duke of Normandy
1 CONT In 1152 he married Eleanor of Aquitaine
1 CONT In 1154 he became King of England
1 CONT In 1171 he invaded Ireland and was recognised as Lord by nearly all the Irish kings
1 CONT In 1173 the Count of Toulouse paid homage
1 CONT In 1173 his wife and sons turned against him and he quarrelled fatally with his one time friend Thomas Becket Archbishop of Canterbury
1 CONT Created the judicial system and the Common Law
1 CONT Died 1189, Richard I became King
