0 @N6000000005598912545@ NOTE Henry was baptised by Richard de Belmeis, Bishop of London.  As a yout h, and after his brother William's death, Henry was acknowledged as he ir to the throne of England. Young Henry is said to have been blessed with extraordinary
1 CONC  good looks and most 'blessed in courtesy'.  Thanks to William Marshall, Henry is said to have displayed 'unprecedented sk ill in arms'.  In 1158, Henry's father Henry II and Louis VII agreed t hat Henry would inherit the claim to
1 CONC  Brittany, formerly held by Henry' s uncle Geoffrey.  In 1160, he had done homage toLouis, King of France , as Duc de Normandie.  He was also styled as Count d'Anjou.  In 1161 , young Henry joined the household of Thomas Becket, w
1 CONC ho then referre d to the young prince as his adopted son.  In 1163, he received the ho mage of the Welsh princes and the King of the Scots.  On 6 January 116 9, Henry and his father and brother Richard met with Louis VII to ensh r
1 CONC ine the peace treay of Montmirail.
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry was knighted and crowned King of England by his father on 14 Jun e 1170, but died before he could claim the actual title.  He was crown ed by Archbishop Roger of York with six bishops in attendance and fro m then until his f
1 CONC athers death was to be known as 'the young King'.  A t the time, Henry is styled as tall, gook looking and full of gay irre verance.  Even though Henry II had no intention of giving up his powe r as king, young Henry  was already 
1 CONC exhibiting an alarming contempt fo r his father.  Despite his titles, he was denied any real power by hi s father.  After the coronation, he received his own household under t he guardianship of William Marshall.  Henry's 'resentm
1 CONC ent continued t o grow'.  The growing rift between Henry and his father became a 'dead ly hatred' (ref: Jordan Fantosme).  Henry became 'a weak, vain, idle, untrustworthy and irresponsible spendthrift' (ref: Robert of Torigni) .  
1 CONC Henry held his first court at Christmas 1170 at Winchester.  After his coronation, young Henry was utilized by his father primarily as a military commander, but refrained from granting him any independant po wer.  It was this deni
1 CONC al that pushed young Henry into his association with Louis VII.  It was also during this period that young Henry bega n to urge his younger brothers to join him in rebellion against their father.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1172, Henry was invited to the court of Louis VII so the French Kin g could make hearing of the 'young Kings' grievances.  During the Chri stmas season of 1172, Henry II concluded an agreement with Humbert II I de Savoy re. Hum
1 CONC berts lands and the betrothal of John Lackland to Hu mbert's daughter Alice.  Part of the agreement was that John would be in reciept of the castles of Chinon, Loudon and Mirebeau, north of Poi tiers.  This promise enraged Henry a
1 CONC nd he soon again pressed for contr ol of Anjou.  Father and son quarreled and 'the young King' left his f athers court and made for that of his father-in-law, the King of Franc e, Louis VII.  Louis VII promised his support to 'the
1 CONC  young King' in a war against Henry II.  Henry's mother had taken up her son Henry's c ause in a rejection of her husband and in hopes of removing Henry II f rom the continent in favor of her son.  She also encouraged Richard an d
1 CONC  Geoffrey to join their older brother.  She soon however was capture d while in disquise and subsequently transferred to Winchester in 1173 .  In March of 1173, Henry sought to enlist the support of his brother s Richard and Geoff
1 CONC rey against their father.  In May of 1173, William Marshall, and Louis VII recognized young Henry as the 'rightful King o f England'.  In July of 1174, Henry with Louis VII of France and Phili p de Alsace - Count of Flanders made 
1 CONC an attack on Rouen, Normandie; bu t it was doomed to failure.  By 8 August 1174, Henry had returned to t he area of Barfluer with his mercenaries.  In about 1176, 'the young k ing' Henry began to plot against his father again.  He
1 CONC  also met with v arious barons plotting the overthrow of his brother Richard as Duke o f Normandie.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1181, Henry with his brothers Richard and Geoffrey supported Philli ppe of France in a war against Philip de Alsace - Count of Flanders .   In 1182, young Henry joined with his brother Geoffrey and Bertran d de Born (Burn) agai
1 CONC nst their brother Richrds harsh rule in Aquitaine .  In February 1183, Henry - the young King, and Geoffrey laid siege t o the a fortified church at Gorre, a short distance from Limoges, wher e they were taken by suprise by their 
1 CONC brother Richard, prisoners were taken and Richard ordered some of them drowned and others to have thei r throats slit while the rest were blinded.  Later that year,  in earl y June 1183 Henry sent his father a message indicating t
1 CONC hat he had an illness, but the father Henry feared a trap and refused to come to Cha teau Martel and soon afterwards, the young Henry died.  This became on e of the great sorrows of King Henry II's life.
1 CONT
