0 @N6000000002187590204@ NOTE Richard I of England
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard I 
1 CONT King of the English, Duke of the Normans and Aquitanians, Count of the Angevins 
1 CONT
1 CONT Reign July 6, 1189 – April 6, 1199 
1 CONT Coronation September 3, 1189 
1 CONT Born September 8, 1157 
1 CONT  Beaumont Palace, Oxford 
1 CONT Died April 6, 1199 
1 CONT  Châlus, in Limousin 
1 CONT Buried Fontevraud Abbey 
1 CONT Predecessor Henry II 
1 CONT Successor John 
1 CONT Consort Berengaria of Navarre
1 CONT (c. 1165/ 1170 – 1230) 
1 CONT Issue Died without legitimate posterity 
1 CONT Royal House Plantagenet 
1 CONT Father Henry II (1133–1189) 
1 CONT Mother Eleanor of Aquitaine (1124–1204) 
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard I (September 8, 1157 – April 6, 1199) was King of England from 1189 to 1199. In his own time, the troubadour Bertran de Born called him Oc-e-Non (Yes-and-No), while some later writers referred to him as Richard the Lionhea
1 CONC rt, Cœur de Lion, as he is still known in France.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family 
1 CONT 2 Early life 
1 CONT 3 Revolt against Henry II 
1 CONT 4 Anti-Semitic violence 
1 CONT 5 Early Reign 
1 CONT 6 The struggle for Sicily 
1 CONT 7 Richard on the Third Crusade 
1 CONT 8 Richard's marriage 
1 CONT 9 Richard in Outremer 
1 CONT 10 Captivity and return 
1 CONT 11 Later years and death 
1 CONT 12 Legacy 
1 CONT 13 Folklore and fiction 
1 CONT 14 See also 
1 CONT 15 Sources 
1 CONT 16 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Family
1 CONT The third of King Henry II's legitimate sons, Richard was never expected to ascend to the throne. He is generally considered to have been the favourite son of his mother, Eleanor of Aquitaine. Richard was a younger maternal half-b
1 CONC rother of Marie de Champagne and Alix of France. He was a younger brother of William, Count of Poitiers, Henry the Young King and Matilda of England. He was also an older brother of Geoffrey II, Duke of Brittany, Leonora of Englan
1 CONC d, Joan Plantagenet and John Lackland of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT Although born at Beaumont Palace, Oxford, England, like most of the Royal Family at the time he was, for all intents and purposes, French. When his parents effectively separated, he remained in Eleanor's care, and was invested wit
1 CONC h her duchy of Aquitaine in 1168, and of Poitiers in 1172. This was his consolation prize for the fact that his eldest surviving brother, Henry the Young King, was simultaneously crowned as his father's successor. Richard and his 
1 CONC other brother, Geoffrey, Duke of Brittany, thus learned how to defend their property while still teenagers. Eleanor's favouritism of Richard was supposedly predicted by Merlin the magician who claimed, "The eagle of the broken cov
1 CONC enant shall rejoice in [Eleanor]'s third nesting.”
1 CONT
1 CONT English Royalty 
1 CONT House of Plantagenet 
1 CONT
1 CONT Henry II 
1 CONT Children 
1 CONT    William, Count of Poitiers 
1 CONT    Henry, Count of Anjou 
1 CONT    Richard I the Lionheart 
1 CONT    Geoffrey II, Duke of Brittany 
1 CONT    John 
1 CONT    Matilda, Duchess of Saxony 
1 CONT    Leonora, Queen of Castile 
1 CONT    Joan, Queen of Sicily 
1 CONT Richard I 
1 CONT As well as being an educated man, able to compose poetry in French and Occitan, he was also very attractive; his hair between red and blond, blue-eyed, his height estimated at six feet four inches (1.93 m) tall. He gloried in mili
1 CONC tary activity. From an early age he appeared to have significant political and military abilities, became noted for his chivalry and courage, and soon was able to control the unruly nobles of his territory. Like his brothers, Rich
1 CONC ard had limited respect for his father and lacked foresight and a sense of responsibility.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Revolt against Henry II
1 CONT In 1170, his elder brother Henry the Young King was crowned king of England as Henry III. Historians know him as Henry "the Young King" so as not to confuse him with the later king of this name who was his nephew.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1173, Richard joined his brothers, Henry and Geoffrey, Duke of Brittany, in a revolt against their father. They were planning to dethrone their father and leave the Young King as the only king of England. Henry II invaded Aquit
1 CONC aine twice. At the age of seventeen, Richard was the last of the brothers to hold out against Henry; though, in the end, he refused to fight him face to face and humbly begged his pardon. In 1174, after the end of the failed revol
1 CONC t, Richard gave a new oath of subservience to his father.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard had several major reasons for discontent with his father. First was Henry's refusal to allow Richard any real power or funds despite pleas by Richard for more of both. Though placated by titles such as Count of Poitou, Ric
1 CONC hard wanted more and Henry seemed unwilling to entrust any of his sons with resources that could be used against him, with very good reason. Second, Henry had appropriated Princess Alys Richard's betrothed, the daughter of Louis V
1 CONC II of France by his second wife, as his mistress. This made a marriage between Richard and Alys technically impossible – at least in the eyes of the church, but Henry, not wishing to cause a diplomatic incident, prevaricated and d
1 CONC id not confess to his misdeed. As for Richard, he was discouraged from renouncing Alys because she was the sister of King Philip II of France, a close ally.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his failure to overthrow his father, Richard concentrated on putting down internal revolts by the dissatisfied nobles of Aquitaine, especially the territory of Gascony. The increasing cruelty of his reign led to a major revo
1 CONC lt of Gascony in 1179. Richard had a terrible reputation, including reports of various rapes and murders. The rebels hoped to dethrone Richard and asked his brothers Henry and Geoffrey to help them succeed.
1 CONT
1 CONT A turning point in the rebellion against Richard occurred in the Charente Valley in spring 1179. The fortress of Taillebourg, located in the Charente Valley, was extremely well defended and completely disregarded by previous ruler
1 CONC s of Aquitaine because it was considered impregnable. The castle was surrounded by a cliff on three sides and a town on the fourth side with a three-layer wall. Richard first destroyed and looted the farms and lands surrounding th
1 CONC e fortress, leaving its defenders no reinforcements or outs. The inhabitants of the Taillebourg fortress were so afraid of Richard at this point, that they left the safety of their castle and attacked Richard outside its walls. Ri
1 CONC chard was able to subdue the army and then followed the defenders inside the open gates, where Richard easily took over the castle in two days. Richard’s victory at Taillebourg was important for two reasons. First, many barons thi
1 CONC nking of rebelling against Richard quickly abandoned those plans and declared their loyalty to Richard. Second, Richard gained a reputation as a skilled military commander, which would follow him throughout the rest of his life.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Richard subdued his rebellious barons, he again challenged his father for the throne. From 1180 to 1183 the tension between Henry and Richard grew, as King Henry commanded Richard to pay homage to Henry the Young King, the h
1 CONC eir the English crown, but Richard refused. Finally, in 1183, Henry the Young King and Geoffrey, Duke of Brittany invaded Aquitaine in an attempt to subdue Richard. Richard’s barons joined in the fray and turned against their Duke
1 CONC . However, Richard and his army were able to hold back the invading armies and cruelly executed any prisoners. The conflict took a brief pause in June of 1183 when Prince Henry died. However, Henry II soon gave John Lackland permi
1 CONC ssion to invade Aquitaine in replacement of the dead Prince Henry. With the death of Prince Henry, Richard was now the eldest son and heir to the English crown, but still the fighting between Richard and Henry II continued.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1188 Henry II planned to concede Aquitaine to his youngest son John Lackland, later King John of England. To strengthen his position, Richard had allied himself the previous year with Philip II, who was the son of Eleanor's ex-
1 CONC husband Louis VII by his third wife, Adele of Champagne. There is even a theory that Richard and Philip were lovers, largely based on Roger of Hoveden's account of their relationship:
1 CONT
1 CONT "Philip so honoured him that every day they ate at the same table, shared the same dish and at night they slept in the same bed. And the King of France loved him as his own soul; and they loved each other so much that the King of 
1 CONC England was astonished at the passionate love between them and marvelled at it." (Roger of Hoveden, vol. 2, p. 318): 
1 CONT Although sharing a bed in the Middle Ages was not considered to necessarily have sexual implications, the Latin word "vehementum," which Hoveden used in the untranslated passage implies more than mere friendship. The issue remains
1 CONC  a subject of controversy.
1 CONT
1 CONT In exchange for Philip's help against his father, Richard promised to concede his rights to both Normandy and Anjou to Philip. Richard gave an oath of subservience to Philip in November of the same year. In 1189 Richard attempted 
1 CONC to take the throne of England for himself by joining Philip's expedition against his father. On July 4, 1189 Richard and Philip II’s forces defeated the forces of Henry II at Ballans. Henry, with John's consent, agreed to name Ric
1 CONC hard his heir. On July 6, 1189 Henry II died in Chinon, and Richard I succeeded him as King of England, Duke of Normandy, and Count of Anjou. He was officially crowned duke on July 20 and king in Westminster on September 3, 1189.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Anti-Semitic violence
1 CONT When Richard was crowned King of England, he barred all Jews and women from the ceremony (this was apparently a concession to the fact that his coronation was not merely one of a king but of a crusader), but some Jewish leaders sh
1 CONC owed up anyway to present gifts for the new king. According to Ralph of Diceto, Richard's courtiers stripped and flogged the Jews, then flung them out of court. When a rumor spread that Richard had ordered all Jews to be killed, t
1 CONC he people of London joined in to persecute the Jews, and a massacre began. Many Jews were beaten to death, robbed, and burnt alive. Many Jewish homes were burned down and several Jews were forcibly baptised. Some sought sanctuary 
1 CONC in the Tower of London, and others managed to escape. Among those killed was Jacob of Orléans, widely regarded as one of the most learned of the age.
1 CONT
1 CONT However other chroniclers such as Benedict of Peterborough tell a different story; the rioting was started by the jealous and bigoted citizens of London. Richard is said to have punished the perpetrators and allowed a forcibly con
1 CONC verted Jew to return to Judaism. Archbishop of Canterbury Baldwin of Exeter reacted by remarking, "If the King is not God's man, he had better be the devil's," a reference to the supposedly infernal blood in the Angevin line.
1 CONT
1 CONT In either case, realizing that the assaults could destabilize his realm at the time of his imminent departure for the Holy Land, Richard ordered the execution of those responsible for the most egregious murders and persecutions (m
1 CONC ost of those who were hanged were the rioters who had accidentally burnt down Christian homes). Further, he distributed a royal writ demanding that the Jews be left alone. However, the edict was loosely enforced, as the following 
1 CONC March there was a renewed outbreak of violence, including a massacre at York.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Early Reign
1 CONT Richard has been criticised for doing little for England, siphoning the kingdom's resources to support his Crusade and campaigns in what is now France. He spent only six months of his reign in England, claiming it was "cold and al
1 CONC ways raining." During the period when he was raising funds for his Crusade, Richard was heard to declare, "If I could have found a buyer I would have sold London itself." However, England was a minor part of his territories, only 
1 CONC important in that it gave him a royal title with which to approach other kings as an equal. Like most of the Plantagenet kings before the 14th century, he had no need to learn the English language. Leaving the country in the hands
1 CONC  of various officials he designated (including his mother, at times), Richard was far more concerned with his French possessions.
1 CONT
1 CONT His father Henry II of England and Philip II of France had already taken the cross at Gisors on 21 January 1188, inspired by the loss of Jerusalem to the Muslims under the command of Saladin. Richard, too, had done so as Count of 
1 CONC Poitou. Having become king, he and Philip agreed to go together, since each feared that, during his absence, the other might usurp his territories.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard swore an oath to renounce his past wickedness in order to show himself worthy to take the cross. He started to raise a new English crusader army, though most of his warriors were Normans, and supplied it with weapons. He s
1 CONC pent most of his father's treasury (filled with money raised by the Saladin tithe), raised taxes, and even agreed to free King William I of Scotland from his oath of subservience to Richard in exchange for 10,000 marks. To raise e
1 CONC ven more money he sold official positions, rights, and lands to those interested in them. Even those already appointed were forced to pay exorbitant sums to retain their posts. Even William Longchamp, Bishop of Ely and the King's 
1 CONC Chancellor, made a show of bidding £3,000 to remain as Chancellor. He was apparently outbid by a man named Reginald the Italian, but his bid was refused. He finally succeeded in raising a huge army and navy.
1 CONT
1 CONT After repositioning the part of his army he left behind to guard his French possessions, Richard finally started his expedition to the Holy Land in 1190. He appointed as regents Hugh, Bishop of Durham, and William de Mandeville, 3
1 CONC rd Earl of Essex, who soon died and was replaced by Richard's chancellor William Longchamp. Richard's brother John was not satisfied by this decision and started scheming against William.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Effigy on tomb in Fontevrault Abbey[edit]
1 CONT The struggle for Sicily
1 CONT In September 1190 both Richard and Philip arrived in Sicily. In 1189 King William II of Sicily had died. His heir was his aunt Constance, later Queen Constance of Sicily, who was married to Emperor Henry VI. But immediately after 
1 CONC William's death, William's cousin, Tancred, rebelled, seized control of the island and was crowned early in 1190 as King Tancred I of Sicily. He was favoured by the people and Pope Clement III, but he had problems with the island'
1 CONC s nobles. Richard's arrival caused even more problems. Tancred had imprisoned William's widow, Queen Joan, who was Richard's sister, and did not give her the money she had inherited according to William's will. Richard demanded th
1 CONC at his sister be released and given her inheritance. Meanwhile the presence of two foreign armies caused unrest among the people. In October, the people of Messina revolted, demanding that the foreigners leave the island. Richard 
1 CONC attacked Messina and captured it on October 4, 1190. After looting and burning the city Richard established his base there. He remained there until March 1191 when Tancred finally agreed to sign a treaty on March 4, 1191. The trea
1 CONC ty was signed during the same month by Richard, Philip and Tancred. According to the treaty's main terms:
1 CONT
1 CONT Joan was to be released, receiving her inheritance along with the dowry her father had given to the deceased William. 
1 CONT Richard and Philip recognized Tancred as legal King of Sicily and vowed to keep the peace between all three of their kingdoms. 
1 CONT Richard officially proclaimed his nephew, the son of Geoffrey, Arthur of Brittany, as his heir, and Tancred promised to later marry one of his daughters to Arthur when he came of age (Arthur was only four years old at the time). 
1 CONT Richard and Tancred exchanged gifts as was customary; Richard gave Tancred a sword claimed to be Excalibur, the enchanted blade of King Arthur. 
1 CONT After signing the treaty Richard and Philip left Sicily. The treaty undermined England's relationships with the Holy Roman Empire and caused the revolt of Richard's brother John, who hoped to be proclaimed heir instead of their ne
1 CONC phew. Although his revolt failed, John continued to scheme against his brother after this point.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Richard on the Third Crusade
1 CONT In April 1191, while on route to the Third Crusade, Richard stopped on the Byzantine island of Rhodes to avoid the stormy weather. It seems that Richard had previously met his fiancée Berengaria only once, years before their weddi
1 CONC ng. He had assigned his mother to represent him and convince her father, Sancho VI of Navarre, and her other relatives to agree to the wedding, and to bring the bride to the wedding. Richard came to their rescue when they were shi
1 CONC pwrecked on the coast of Cyprus. He left Rhodes in May but a new storm drove Richard's fleet to the island.
1 CONT
1 CONT On May 6, 1191, Richard's fleet arrived in the port of Lemesos (now Limassol). Richard captured the city. When the island's despot Isaac Comnenus arrived to stop the Crusaders he discovered he was too late, and retired to Kolossi.
1 CONC  Richard called Isaac to negotiations but Isaac broke his oath of hospitality and started demanding Richard's departure. Richard ordered his cavalry to follow him in a battle against Isaac's army in Tremetusia. The few Roman Catho
1 CONC lics of the island joined Richard's army and so did the island's nobles who were dissatisfied with Isaac's seven years of tyrannical rule. Though Isaac and his men fought bravely, Richard's army was bigger and better equipped, ass
1 CONC uring his victory. Isaac continued to resist from the castles of Pentadactylos but after the siege of his castle of Kantara he finally surrendered. In a fit of sardonic irony, once Isaac had been captured Richard had him confined 
1 CONC with silver chains, scrupulously abiding by a previous promise that he would not place Isaac in irons should he be taken prisoner. Richard became the new ruler of Cyprus, gaining for the Crusade a major supply base that was not un
1 CONC der immediate threat from the Turks as was Tyre. Richard looted the island and massacred those trying to resist him. He and most of his army left Cyprus for the Holy Land early in June. In his absence Cyprus would be governed by R
1 CONC ichard Camville.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Arms of Richard I[edit]
1 CONT Richard's marriage
1 CONT Before leaving Cyprus, Richard married Berengaria, first-born daughter of King Sancho VI of Navarre. The wedding was held in Limassol on May 12, 1191 at the Chapel of St. George. It was attended by his sister Joan, whom Richard ha
1 CONC d brought from Sicily. It should be noted that when Richard married Berengaria he was still officially betrothed to Alys and that Richard pushed for the match, in order to obtain Navarre as a fief like Aquitaine for his father. Fu
1 CONC rther, Eleanor championed the match, as Navarre bordered on Aquitaine, thereby securing her ancestral lands' borders to the south. Richard took his new wife with him briefly on this episode of the crusade. However, they returned s
1 CONC eparately. Berengaria had almost as much difficulty in making the journey home as her husband did, and did not see England until after his death. Although after his release from German captivity, Richard showed some degree of regr
1 CONC et for his earlier conduct, he was not joined by his wife.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard had to be ordered to reunite with and show fidelity to Berengaria in the future, being told to "remember the destruction of Sodom and abstain from illicit acts." Often this has been construed as more evidence that Richard 
1 CONC was engaged in homosexual activities, despite the fact that "sodomy" can refer not only to anal intercourse but to any sordid sexual practises with men or otherwise. Some later writers, elaborating on the theory, have alleged that
1 CONC  Berengaria's own brother, the future Sancho VII, was one of Richard's early lovers. Nevertheless, when Richard died in 1199, Berengaria was greatly distressed, apparently having loved her husband very much. The picture is further
1 CONC  muddied by the fact that she had to sue the Church to be recognised as his widow. Although historians still remain divided on the issue, many fictional works and modern histories see Richard's homosexuality as a given.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Richard in Outremer
1 CONT In Cyprus, Richard had received military support from Guy of Lusignan widower of Richard's father's first cousin Sibylla of Jerusalem. Guy was still attempting to retain his title as King of Jerusalem, despite his wife's death dur
1 CONC ing the siege of Acre the previous year, and was hoping for Richard's backing, since his family were vassals of Richard in Poitou. His rival for the throne was Conrad of Montferrat, second husband of Sibylla's half-sister, Isabell
1 CONC a: Conrad, whose defence of Tyre had saved the kingdom in 1187, was supported by Philip of France, son of his first cousin Louis VII of France, and by another cousin, Duke Leopold V of Austria. Richard also allied with Humphrey IV
1 CONC  of Toron, Isabella's first husband, from whom she had been forcibly divorced in 1190. Humphrey was loyal to Guy, and spoke Arabic fluently, so Richard used him as his translator and negotiator.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Richard arrived at Acre (`Akko) in June 1191, where he and his forces carried out a successful capture of the city — at one point, while sick from scurvy, Richard himself was carried on a stretcher and picked off guards on th
1 CONC e walls with a crossbow. Eventually, Conrad of Montferrat concluded the surrender negotiations with Saladin, and raised the banners of the kings in the city. Richard quarrelled with Leopold V of Austria over both the deposition of
1 CONC  his relative, Isaac Comnenus, and Leopold's position within the Crusade. This came to a head when he ordered his men to raise the Austrian banner alongside the English and French standards. This was interpreted as rank arrogance 
1 CONC by both Richard and Philip, as Leopold was a dependent vassal of the Holy Roman Emperor. Richard's men, with or without his knowledge, tore the flag down from its pole and threw it in the moat of the Acre fortifications. Leopold, 
1 CONC stung bitterly by the act, left the Crusade immediately. Finally, Philip also left the Crusade, in poor health and after heated negotiations with Richard over the status of Cyprus (Philip demanded half the island) and the kingship
1 CONC  of Jerusalem. Richard suddenly found himself without allies.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard had kept two thousand seven hundred Muslim prisoners as hostages against Saladin fulfilling all the terms of the surrender of the lands around Acre. Philip, before leaving, had entrusted his own prisoners to Conrad, but Ri
1 CONC chard had forced him to hand them over to him. Richard feared his forces being bottled up in Acre, as he believed his campaign could not advance with the prisoners in train. In a fit of impatience and nervousness, he ordered all t
1 CONC he prisoners killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT An election forced Richard reluctantly to accept Conrad of Montferrat as King of Jerusalem, and he sold Cyprus to his own protégé, Guy of Lusignan. However, only days later, on 28 April 1192, Conrad was stabbed to death by assassi
1 CONC ns before he could be crowned. Eight days later, Richard's own nephew, Henry II of Champagne was married to the widowed Isabella, although she was carrying Conrad's child. The murder has never been conclusively solved, and Richard
1 CONC  was widely suspected of involvement.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard's tactics had ensured success at the siege of Acre and on the subsequent march south, Saladin's men being unable to harass the Crusader army into an impulsive action which might not have gone their way. However, the desert
1 CONC ion of the French king had been a major blow, from which they could not hope to recover. Realising that he had no hope of holding Jerusalem even if he took it, Richard sadly ordered a retreat. Despite being only a few miles from t
1 CONC he city, he refused, thereafter, to set eyes on it, as he had vowed to look upon it only once he had conquered the city. After the retreat from Jerusalem, there commenced a period of minor skirmishes with Saladin's forces while Ri
1 CONC chard and Saladin negotiated a settlement to the conflict, as both realized that their respective positions were growing untenable. On Richard's side, he knew that both Philip and his own brother John were starting to plot against
1 CONC  him. However, Saladin held firm on the razing of the fortifications of Ascalon (which Richard's men had rebuilt) and a few other points. Richard tried one last-ditch attempt to raise his bargaining position by attempting to invad
1 CONC e Egypt — a major source of resupply for Saladin — but failed. In the end, time ran out for Richard. He had finally realised that his return home could be postponed no longer, since both Philip and John were taking advantage of hi
1 CONC s absence to make themselves more powerful. He and Saladin finally came to a settlement of the conflict on September 2, 1192 — this included the provisions demanding the destruction of Ascalon's wall as well as an agreement allowi
1 CONC ng Christian access to and presence in Jerusalem. It also included a three-year truce.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Captivity and return
1 CONT Bad weather forced Richard's ship to put in at Corfu, the territory of the Byzantine Emperor Isaac Angelus, who was still angry at Richard for his annexation of Cyprus. Disguised as a Knight Templar, Richard sailed from Corfu with
1 CONC  four attendants in a pirate ship, which wrecked near Aquileia, forcing Richard and his party into a dangerous land route through central Europe. On his way to the territory of Henry of Saxony, his brother-in-law, Richard was capt
1 CONC ured shortly before Christmas 1192 only a few miles from the Moravian border, near Vienna, by Leopold V of Austria, who accused Richard of ordering the death of Conrad, who was his first cousin. Richard and his retainers had been 
1 CONC travelling disguised as pilgrims. Richard himself was dressed like a kitchen hand, but was identified because he was wearing a magnificent and costly ring no menial worker could afford. (Another tale claimed he was identified by h
1 CONC is insistence on eating roast chicken, a great delicacy reserved for nobility.) The Duke handed him over as a prisoner to Henry VI, Holy Roman Emperor after being held captive at Dürnstein. It was here that he wrote Ja nus hons pr
1 CONC is or Ja nuls om pres, a song in French and Occitan versions, expressing his feelings of abandonment by his people. Although the conditions of his captivity were not severe, he was frustrated by his inability to travel freely. Ric
1 CONC hard once proudly declared, "I am born of a rank which recognizes no superior but God" to the emperor. His mother, Eleanor of Aquitaine, worked tirelessly to raise the ransom of 150,000 marks demanded by the German emperor, which 
1 CONC was twice the annual income for the English Crown. Both clergy and laymen were taxed for a quarter of the value of their property, the gold and silver treasures of the churches were confiscated, and money was raised from the scuta
1 CONC ge and the carucage taxes. The emperor demanded that 100,000 marks be delivered to him before he would release the king, the same amount that had been raised by the Saladin tithe only a few years earlier. At the same time, John, R
1 CONC ichard's brother, and King Philip offered 80,000 marks for the Emperor to hold Richard prisoner until Michaelmas 1194. The emperor turned down the offer. The money to rescue the King was transferred to Germany by the emperor's amb
1 CONC assadors, but "at the king's peril" (had it been lost along the way, Richard would have been held responsible), and finally, on February 4, 1194 Richard was released. King Philip of France sent a message to John: "Look to yourself
1 CONC ; the devil is loose."
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Later years and death
1 CONT
1 CONT Tomb at FontevraudDuring his absence, John had come close to seizing the throne; Richard forgave him when they met again, treating him as a boy led astray by evil counsellors, and reportedly sending him off to catch a salmon. Bowi
1 CONC ng to political necessity, Richard even named John as his heir in place of Arthur, who was growing into an unpleasant youth, and whose mother Constance of Britanny was perhaps already open to the overtures of Philip Augustus. Inst
1 CONC ead of turning against John, Richard came into conflict with his former ally and close friend, King Philip. When Philip attacked Richard's fortress, Chateau-Gaillard, he boasted that "if its walls were iron, yet would I take it," 
1 CONC to which Richard replied, "If these walls were butter, yet would I hold them!"
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Tomb at Rouen CathedralDetermined to resist Philip's designs on contested Angevin lands such as the Vexin and Berry, Richard poured all his military expertise and vast resources into war on the French King. He constructed a grand 
1 CONC alliance of powers against Philip, including Baldwin IX of Flanders, Renaud, Count of Boulogne, and his father-in-law King Sancho VI of Navarre, who raided Philp's lands from the south. Most importantly, he managed to secure the v
1 CONC ast Welf inheritance in Saxony for his nephew, Henry the Lion's son Otto of Poitou; Otto's position became so strong that he was to be elected as Otto IV of Germany.
1 CONT
1 CONT Partly as a result of these and other intrigues, Richard won several victories over Philip, and only chance was to rob them of conclusive value. At the battle of Gisors Richard I took "Dieu et mon Droit" "God and my Right" as his 
1 CONC motto, reinforcing his earlier boast to the Emperor Henry that his rank acknowledged no superior but God. At the skirmish of Freteval Philip fled ignominiously in panic, leaving his entire archive of financial audits and documents
1 CONC  to be captured by Richard.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the end it was not the armies of Philip but a siege of the castle of Châlus-Charbrol in Limousin, France, on 26 March 1199 that would take Richard's life. Richard seems to have been facing a rebellion by the Viscount of Limoges
1 CONC  and his half-brother, the Count of Angoulême, backed by Philip of France, and was determined to destroy the Viscount's castles. Some chroniclers also claim Richard had heard of a treasure trove of golden statues at Châlus, but th
1 CONC is seems to be apocryphal. Pierre Basile was probably one of only two knights defending Châlus. Richard, who had removed some of his chainmail, was wounded in the shoulder by a crossbow bolt launched from a tower by Basile, as the
1 CONC  King laughed at the man's ingenuity in using a frying-pan as a shield. Gangrene set in and Richard asked to see his killer. He ordered that Basile be set free and awarded a sum of money. However as soon as Richard died, with his 
1 CONC 77-year-old mother Eleanor at his side, on 6 April 1199, Richard's most infamous mercenary captain Mercadier had Basile flayed alive and then hanged.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard's bowels were buried at the foot of the tower from which the shot was loosed, his heart was buried at Rouen, while the rest of his remains were buried next to his father at Fontevraud Abbey near Chinon and Saumur, France.
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Legacy
1 CONT
1 CONT This bronze equestrian statue of Richard I brandishing his sword by Carlo Marochetti stands outside the Palace of Westminster in London.Richard produced no legitimate heirs, although he is purported to have had one illegitimate so
1 CONC n. As a result, he was succeeded by his brother John as king of England. However, his French territories initially rejected John as a successor, preferring his nephew Arthur of Brittany, the son of their late brother Geoffrey, who
1 CONC se claim was technically better than John's. Significantly, the lack of any direct heirs from Richard was the first step in the dissolution of the Angevin Empire. While Kings of England continued to press claims to properties on t
1 CONC he continent, they would never again command the territories Richard I inherited.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard's legacy comprised several parts. First, he captured Cyprus, which proved immensely valuable in keeping the Frankish kingdoms in the Holy Land viable for another century. Second, his absence from the English political land
1 CONC scape meant that the highly efficient government created by his father was allowed to entrench itself, though King John would later abuse it to the breaking point. The last part of Richard's legacy was romantic and literary. No ma
1 CONC tter the facts of his reign, he left an indelible imprint on the imagination extending to the present, in large part because of his military exploits. This is reflected in Steven Runciman's final verdict of Richard I: "he was a ba
1 CONC d son, a bad husband and a bad king, but a gallant and splendid soldier."
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT Folklore and fiction
1 CONT By the 1260s, a legend had developed that, after Richard's capture, his minstrel, Blondel, travelled Europe from castle to castle, loudly singing a song known only to the two of them. Eventually, the story goes, he came to the pla
1 CONC ce where Richard was being held, and heard the song answered with the appropriate refrain, thus revealing where the king was incarcerated. The story was the subject of André Ernest Modeste Grétry's opera Richard Coeur-de-Lion (178
1 CONC 4). It seems unconnected to the real Jean 'Blondel' de Nesle, an aristocratic trouvère.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the 16th century, a few fictionalised chronicles linked Richard with the Robin Hood legends, although this did not become widespread until Walter Scott's Ivanhoe (below), and has subsequently been popularised by cinema. These l
1 CONC ater Hood stories depict Robin as upholding justice in Richard's name, against John and his officials, during Richard's imprisonment. However, in the earliest Robin Hood ballads the only king mentioned is "Edward our comely king",
1 CONC  most probably Edward II or III.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Arabic world, Richard became a bit of a bogeyman for centuries after his death. Mothers would occasionally threaten unruly children with the admonition "King Richard will get you" well into the late 19th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard has appeared frequently in fiction, as a result of the 'chivalric revival' of the Romantic era. Sir Walter Scott depicts him in Ivanhoe, in which he initially adopts the pseudonym of Le Noir Fainéant ("The Black Sluggard")
1 CONC , and in The Talisman, a highly fictionalised treatment of the Third Crusade. He is also a major character in James Goldman's play The Lion in Winter, which depicts him as homosexual. He features in Graham Shelby's The Kings of Va
1 CONC in Intent and, more centrally, in The Devil is Loose, Norah Lofts' The Lute-Player, and Jean Plaidy (Eleanor Hibbert)'s The Heart of the Lion. He is generally portrayed in a heroic role in children's fiction, such as Ronald Welch'
1 CONC s Knight Crusader.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard has been portrayed on film by:
1 CONT
1 CONT Wallace Beery, in the 1922 silent film, Robin Hood, with Douglas Fairbanks. 
1 CONT Henry Wilcoxon in Cecil B. DeMille's 1935 film "The Crusades." 
1 CONT Ian Hunter in the 1938 film The Adventures of Robin Hood, with Errol Flynn. 
1 CONT Norman Wooland in Ivanhoe (1952), starring Robert Taylor. 
1 CONT George Sanders in the 1954 film King Richard and the Crusaders (loosely based on The Talisman). 
1 CONT Hamdi Geiss in Egyptian director Youssef Chahine's 1963 film Al Nasser Salah Ad-Din 
1 CONT Anthony Hopkins in the 1968 film version of The Lion in Winter. 
1 CONT Richard Harris in Robin and Marian (1976). 
1 CONT John Rhys Davies in the episode of the BBC show Robin Of Sherwood, "The King's Fool". 
1 CONT Patrick Stewart in Robin Hood Men in Tights (1994). 
1 CONT Andrew Howard in the 2003 video version of The Lion in Winter. 
1 CONT Richard has had a cameo-role in other films. Sean Connery played him, uncredited, in the 1991 film Robin Hood: Prince of Thieves, with Connery later commenting mischievously that it was the only role of his career in which he had 
1 CONC played a homosexual. This role was parodied two years later by Patrick Stewart in Mel Brooks's comedic Robin Hood: Men in Tights. Iain Glen played Richard briefly at the end of the 2005 film Kingdom of Heaven.
1 CONT
1 CONT On television, Richard was the subject of a 1962 television series, Richard the Lionheart, in which he was played by Dermot Walsh. He has featured in adaptations of Ivanhoe and The Talisman, and versions of the Robin Hood legend. 
1 CONC The 1965 Doctor Who television serial "The Crusade" is set during Richard's conflict with Saladin; Richard is played by Julian Glover, who portrayed him again in a television film of Ivanhoe in 1982. John Rhys-Davies played Richar
1 CONC d in one episode of the 1980s television series Robin of Sherwood. Andrew Howard played Richard in a 2003 television adaptation of The Lion in Winter.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the adventure game from Sierra, Conquests of the Longbow - starring Robin Hood - Richard is also featured, held prisoner by King Leopold of Austria. As with the previously mentioned legends, Robin Hood is working in the name of
1 CONC  King Richard, and is working to raise one hundred thousand marks in ransom to release him.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the strategy game Medieval Total War there are two historical battles in which it was based on his two encounter with his rival Saladin, those were battle of Jaffa and battle of Arsuf. Also in the upcoming sequel Richard is on 
1 CONC the coverbox which suggest that the player will have the option to relive Richard military campaign.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard was portrayed as a merciless Muslim killer in a novel that follows Arn Magnusson in his Crusade Trilogy written by Swedish author Jan Guillou.
1 CONT
