0 @N6000000000769926756@ NOTE [v37t1235.ftw]
1 CONT
1 CONT Facts about this person:
1 CONT
1 CONT Fact 1September 03, 1189
1 CONT Acceded: Westminster Abbey, London, England
1 CONT
1 CONT Fact 2
1 CONT Interred: Fontevraud Abbey, France
1 CONT
1 CONT http://www.bbc.co.uk/history/historic_figures/richard_i_king.shtml
1 CONT Also called Richard the Lion-Heart and famous for his exploits in the Third Crusade (1189-92), during his ten-year reign he spent only some six months in England.
1 CONT
1 CONT The third son of Henry II, Richard possessed considerable political and military ability. However, like his brothers, he cared nothing for his family, joining them in the great rebellion against their father in 1173. In 1183 his b
1 CONC rother Henry died, leaving Richard heir to the throne. With other lands in Normandy and Anjou, Henry II wanted him to give Aquitaine to his youngest brother, John. Richard refused and, in 1189, joined forces with Philip II of Fran
1 CONC ce against his father, hounding him to a premature death in July 1189.
1 CONT
1 CONT As king, Richard wanted only to lead the crusade prompted by Saladin's capture of Jerusalem in 1187. To finance this, he sold sheriffdoms and other offices. In 1190 he departed for the Holy Land.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard arrived in June 1191; the city fell the following month. In September his victory at Arsuf gave the crusaders possession of Joppa. Although he came close, Jerusalem, the crusade's main objective, eluded him. Moreover, fier
1 CONC ce quarrels among the French, German and English contingents provided further troubles. After a year's stalemate, Richard made a truce with Saladin and started his journey home.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sailing home, bad weather drove him ashore near Venice. He was captured and imprisoned by Duke Leopold before being handed over to the German emperor Henry VI, who ransomed him for the huge sum of 150,000 marks. The raising of the
1 CONC  ransom was a remarkable achievement. In February 1194, Richard was released.
1 CONT
1 CONT He returned at once to England and was crowned for a second time, fearing that the ransom payment had compromised his independence. Yet a month later he went to Normandy, never to return. His last five years were spent in warfare 
1 CONC against Philip II, interspersed with occasional truces. While besieging the castle of Châlus he was fatally wounded in 1199.
0 @N6000000005598867941@ NOTE Richard was born at Beaumont Palace known as the Church of the White F riars.  Beaumont Palace was a massive wood and stone structure.  As a young child, he was placed in the care of a nurse named Hodierna of St . Albans.  Years l
1 CONC ater, as King, Richard rewarded Hodierna with a larg e pension.  Very likly, at this christening he was designated as the heir of Poitou and Aquitain replacing his deceased brother William.  R ichard I, the Lion-Hearted (Coeurde L
1 CONC ion), spent much of his youth in his mother's court at Poitiers.  Richard inherited both his temper an d his generousity from his father.  He however was his mothers son whe n it came to his love of show and sophistication.  He be
1 CONC came his mothe rs favorite.  He is styled as 'a 6 ft. 5 in. paladin with reddish aubu rn golden hair and piercing blue eyes'.  He failed to master English, knew how to read and write in Potevin French (Occitan - Southern Frenc h),
1 CONC  had mastered and told jokes in Latin, loved music and would conduc t the choir in his private chapel.  He strove in 'all things, to brin g honor to his mothers name'.  He was the master of the generous gestu re but less extravaga
1 CONC nt and less accessible than his younger brother G eoffrey.  As he grew older he gained a reputation for promiscuity.   I t is reported that Richard learned the art of war from a band of merce naries that at the age of twenty-one h
1 CONC e had employed to help him gover n Aquitaine.  He delighted in fighting, the use of the sword on horse and on foot, a mastery of it's skills, and a sense of how to use thos e skills to gain what he wanted.
1 CONT
1 CONT History has treated Richard as a 'selfish and cruel King'.  He was a s agacious general, a deadly wrestler and a swift runner.  He lavished t he gold of his kingdom on his crusade to Palestine.  Richard was raise d in the traditio
1 CONC ns of Aquitaine and the province was designated for h im as early as 1159. His father Henry II, early on made arrangements f or him to marry Alice (Alys) of France, when she became old enough.  I n late 1167 and 1168 Richard was w
1 CONC ith his mother Eleanor in Auqitaine where his heart remained for the rest of his life.  On 6 January 1169 , Richard,  his father and his brother Henry met with Louis VII to ens hrine the peace treay of Montmirail.  After his inves
1 CONC tment as Count o f Poitou in 1170, Richard was presented to the Lords of Poitou as thei r future overlord.  At the time he was created Duke of Aquitaine in 11 72, Richard was also invested in the abbots chair at the church of Sai 
1 CONC nt-Hilaire and granted the ring of St. Valerie, a Roman martyr.  Richa rd spent much of 1173 with the court of Louis VII, often with his brot hers.  Richard utilized the resources of the Angevin Empire to make wa r and fought a re
1 CONC bellion against his father in 1173 and 1174.  In May of 1174, Richard captured the town of Saintes.  During his rebellion a gainst his father, with Philip of Alsace, Richard moved against Pacy i n Normandie.  In November of 1173, 
1 CONC Richard made an attack on La Rochel le in Poitou, but winter brought the hostilities to an end.  Richards armed resistance to his father ended at Mont-Louis on 29 September 117 4, and he was subsequently pardoned by his father.  R
1 CONC ichard cared muc h more for the continental possessions of his mother than for England - he also cared much more for his mother than for his father. Family c onsiderations influenced much of his life: he fought along side of hi s 
1 CONC brothers Prince Henry and Geoffrey in their rebellion of 1173-74.  R ichard joined his brothers Geoffrey and Henry when they successfully s upported the new French King Phillippe in a war against Philip de Alsa ce - Count of Fland
1 CONC ers.  In 1176, Richard was involved with quieting t he troublesome Barons of Aquitaine who had appealed to the 'young King ' and the vicioius Geoffrey for assistance.  Due to a growing jealousy , the two brothers were both more th
1 CONC an willing to make war on Richard .  In 1777 he turned his attention to Gascony.  In June 1777, Richard met with his father Henry II and Louis VII at Nanacourt on the Norman frontier.  One of the primary topics discussed was his p
1 CONC roposed marria ge to Alice, the daughter of Louis VII, but Henry was able to cloud th e issue of the marriage during the negotiation.  In May of 1179, Richa rd successfully laid siege to Taillebourg in Gascony on the Charente R iv
1 CONC er.  His forces applied a scorched earh policy to the farms and vill ages of the area and the citizens were terrified.
1 CONT Early during the reign of Phillippe II of France, Richard is styled a s a romantic in the troubadour tradition of his mother Eleanor.  Richa rd's harsh rule in Aquitaine had earned him the bitter hatred of his v assels.  His broth
1 CONC ers Geoffrey and Henry joined with Bertran de Born i n alliance against him in 1182.  Soon the gulf between the brothers ha d risen to the point that Richard and young Henry would not even sit a t the same table.  In 1182, Henry I
1 CONC I demanded that Richard do nomage t o Henry 'the young King'.  Richard initially refused, but later fough t for his father against his brothers when they supported a February 1 183 revolt of Anquitarian barons in Aquitane. When Ri
1 CONC chard's older bro ther Henry d. in 1183, he became the undisputed heir to the English cr own, Normandie, Anjou, Poitou and Aquitaine.  At the time he is style d as 'handsome, strongly built, long legged, with a great reach of mus 
1 CONC cular arms'.  In June of 1183, when his brother Henry died, Aquitaine was to have reverted to John, but Richard had no intention of allowin g that to occur.  Henry II told John that it was his if he could take it.  In December of 
1 CONC 1183, Richard was ordered to turn over his Aquitai ne territories in return for lands in Anjou and Normandie, but Richar d refused.  In June of 1184, Richards Aquitane territories were attack ed by his brother John and half brothe
1 CONC r Geoffrey and their fathers ord ers.  In April of 1185, Richard did return his territory in Poitou to his mother at the insistance of his father.  In 1186, Richard was invo lved in a military expedition against Raymond of Toulous
1 CONC e.  His confli cts with his father Henry, the King grew into open hostility and he jo ined his fathers great enemy Philip II ( Augustus ) of France against his father in 1187 (ref: ‘Gesta Regis Henrici Secundi’, Roger of Howde n, 
1 CONC ed. W. Stubbs, 2 Vols., Rolls Series, 1867).  In 1187, Richards lan ds in Aquitaine were attacked by Raymond de Toulouse, Geoffrey de Lusi gnan, Aimer of Angouleme (de Tallifer) and Geoffrey of Rancogne possib ly at the instigatio
1 CONC n of Henry II.  He retaliated by imprisoning Pete r Seilun, a member of Raymond's court.  A quick thrust by Richard int o Toulouse captured 17 castles  Richard also began to have grave misgi vings about the relationship between hi
1 CONC s father Henry II and Alice (Al ys) of France.  He began to see the previously arranged marriage for h im to Alys might have a degree of political benefit.  In 1187, Phillip pe of France had marched into Berry and Richard and Henr
1 CONC y II joined fo rces to resist him.  On 23 June 1187 Henry II and Phillippe reached a truce at Chateauraux (ref 'Ralph of Diceto').  However Richard soon de serted his father again and marched to Paris and allied himself with P hil
1 CONC lippe.  In 1187, Richard was in Paris seeking the support of Philli ppe and may have met the poet minstrel Blondel.  Phillippe and Richar d became good friends at that time.
1 CONT
1 CONT In November of 1187, Richard, Phillippe of France and Henry II of Engl and all took the cross from the Archbishop of Tours, in anticipation o f a crusade.  In November 1188, Richard met with his father Henry II, his illegitamate b
1 CONC rother Geoffrey and Phillippe of France at Bonmoulin s to discuss their problems.  Richard stormed out of the meeting and H enry sent Geoffrey and William Marshall after him, to no avail.  Richa rd, with the assistancce of Philip 
1 CONC of France, took LeMans on 12 June 1 189.  Richard & his father were now "set at strief by an illusion of t heir own imaginations". (Norgate - 'John Lackland', p. 23).  Richard f elt that his father Henry had abandoned him in favor
1 CONC  of his younger br other John.  Richard is styled as having many of the Knightly virtues - but humility was notably absent from his personality.  Richard was i nformed of his fathers death by William 'the Protector' Marshall, and 
1 CONC having learned of the death, made his way to Chinon.  He accompanied h is fathers body to Fontevrault, where he was buried.  On 20 July 1189 , Richard met with Phillippe of France between Chaumont and Trie, wher e Phillippe demand
1 CONC ed that Gisors be ceded back to him, but Richard ref used the request.  Richard was invested as Duke of Normandie on 29 Jul y 1189.  At Gisors, on 31 July 1189, he informed Phillippe of France t hat his was his intention to marry 
1 CONC Alice (Alys) of France as soon as h e returned from crusade in the Holy Land.  He sailed from Barfleur on 13 August 1189 for England, and landed at Portsmouth.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard is reported to have been an artist and patron of the church an d even allegedly composed a ballad while in captivity while on his ret urn from the Third Crusade.  After his fathers death, Richard saw his illigitimate broth
1 CONC er Geoffrey and informed him that he was no longer c hancellor, as there had been no love lost between the two.  On 8 July 1189, Richard was present for his fathers funeral.  Richard received t he Kings seal from his half-brother 
1 CONC Geoffrey and he did make Geoffrey the Archbishop of York on 10 August 1189.  Richard also granted his yo unger brother John the Norman county of Mortain.  Once having landed i n England, Richard was received at Winchester on 15 Au
1 CONC gust 1189 by hi s mother and a large group of court officials.  He secured the treasur y of his father.  Richard appointed William Longchamp, recently electe d as Bishop of Ely as Chancellor to replace his half-brother Geoffrey . 
1 CONC  One of his first acts as King was to send William Marshall to Engla nd and secure the release of his mother from confinement.  He soon dec lared that his mother's jailer, Ranulf de Glanville should be thrown ' into the dungeons',
1 CONC  but his mother convinced Richard that Ranulf had b een considerate and Ranulf was spared.  The Royal Treasury that Richar d found contained some 900,000 lbs. plus more newelry.  Richard soon i nformed his mother Eleanor that his 
1 CONC choice for a wife was Princess Ber engaria of Navarre.  Richard soon established himself at the ducal pal ace at Rouen.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard spent about only six to ten months of his ten-year reign in En gland, and bled it dry to finance his crusade and wars upon the contin ent.  He possessed few of the skills of his father as a ruler and desp ite his prolonged
1 CONC  absences from England, he was greatly popular.  He m ade enormous demands upon his kingdom to finance his crusade and his c hronicler was Richard of Devizes - a monk of St. Smithins in Wincheste r.  Richard was heard to jest, "If
1 CONC  I could have found a buyer, I woul d have sold London itself" (ref: "The Chronicle of Richard Devizes of the Time of King Richard the First, ed. J.T. Appleby, 1963).  On 12 De cember 1189, Richard left England to settle affairs o
1 CONC n the continent. He sailed from Dover to Calais, where he was met by Count of Philip o f Flanders.  On 30 December 1189, Richard and Phillipe of France had i nitial discussions that centered on preparations for the crusade.  Soo n
1 CONC , Richard demanded that his younger brother John should accompany hi m on the crusade, but ultimately was convinced to leave him as regent of England during his absence.  On 1 April 1190, Richard met with Phil lippe of France at V
1 CONC ezelay to make arrangements, so that they could tr avel to the Holy Land together.  He arranged with Phillippe to postpon e their departure until July of 1190.  In Richards entourage were Will iam Longspee, Jehan I of Nesle and th
1 CONC e 'trouvers' Gace Brule and Blond el.  Richard acted upon a promise to his father to join the Third Crus ade and departed for the Holy Land in 1190 (accompanied by his partner -rival Phlilippe II of France).  Richard favored an al
1 CONC liance with Nava rre at the time and King Sancho VI was friendly.  On 24 June 1190 Rich ard said a final goodby to his mother Eleanor at Chinon and there issu ed an ordinance to govern the fleet. (ref: GestaRegis Ricardi I, s.a. 1
1 CONC 190, ed. W. Stubbs, pp. 110-111)
1 CONT
1 CONT His army assembled at Tours and there Richard was invested as a pilgri m.  On 2 July, he met with King Phillippe at Vezelay and on 4 July 119 0, he and Phillippe set forth at the head of their armies on the journ ey towards the Ho
1 CONC ly Land, initially making their way to Lyon.  There a bridge collapsed and their were numerous casualities and several dea ths  Richard and Phillippe soon separated and followed different path s to the Mediterranean.  In late 1190
1 CONC , enroute to the Holy Land, Richa rd made the decision to make the trip by water.  He moved from Lyon t o Rouen to Marseilles and was dissappointed to find that his fleet ha d not arrived from England and there waited to assemble 
1 CONC a fleet.  His fleet was to be composed of 100 vessels most of which were rented for a year at a payment of about two/thirds of their actual value.  Withou t the appearance of his fleet and losing patience, Richard hired loca l ves
1 CONC sels for his personal retinue and set sail on 7 August 1190  in h is own galley 'Piombone' with 2 large transports and 20 smaller vessel s.  He left orders for his fleet to meet him as Messina.  He made his way along the Riviera c
1 CONC oast line, past Genoa on 13 August 1190, Portto fino, Pisa on 20 August 1190, past the mouth of the Tiber river and o n towards Sicily, where he made his way to the straights of Messina. By 22 September 1190, Richard had arrived a
1 CONC t Messina which closed its gates to Richard.  Richard stormed the city and quickly won a victory .  Richard and Phillippe agreed to split the proceeds gained from the crusade equally, but while there Richard and Phillippe quarrele
1 CONC d endle ssly.  On 8 October 1190, they also agreed to a set of regulations tha t were designed to control the conduct of their troops while in Sicily .  He subsequently turned the city of Messina over to the Templers an d Hospital
1 CONC lers against the wishes of Phillippe, who had demanded half of the city.
1 CONT
1 CONT While there, his mother visited him and tried to convince him to curb the growing abuse of power by William de Longchamp who was at odds an d in a power struggle with John back in England.  Richard sent Walter of Coutances, then t
1 CONC he Archbishop of Rouen to visit England and reign in Longchamp.  When Richard and King Philip of France met at Messina a bitter dispute arose over the matter of Philip's sister Alice, then held at Winchester and scorned by Richard
1 CONC .  Richard was also having a disagreement with Tancred (illegitimate nephew of William 'le Bon' II of Sicily) over the return of the 40,000 ounces of gold dowry of his s ister Joan.  Eventually after 5 days of discussions between 
1 CONC Tancred an d Richard, Tancred relented and turned Joan and her dowry over to Rich ard.  The receipt of his sister Joan's dowry from Tancred allowed Rich ard to better finance his trip towards the Holy Land.  Richard's depar ture f
1 CONC rom Sicily till the spring of 1191.  In late March of 1191, at S icily, Phillippe released Richard from his promise to marry Alice (Aly s) of France, but Phillippe demanded a payment quitclaim of 10,000 mar ks which came out of Ta
1 CONC ncreds bounty to Richard.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard had his wooden castle "The Griffon-Killer (Mategriffon / bad n eighbor)" built in Sicily during the later months of 1190.  Richard sa iled from Messina aboard his great ship 'Trenc-la-Mer (Trenchmere)', l eading a fleet of
1 CONC  more than 203 (one source indicates 219) vessels in six lines with the tranports (busses) in the rear, on 10/11 April 1191 , led by his High Admiral Robert de Turnham, bound for Outremer.  Bere ngaria and Joan were in hte first s
1 CONC quadron of 3 ships to leave the har bor, while Richard was in the last.  The voyage did not go as planned , and hit by storms on 12 April 1191, 24 vessels were lost.  The remai ns of the fleet took refuge in Rhodes harbor on 19 Ap
1 CONC ril 1191.  Learni ng of Joan and Berengaria's location at Cyprus by 1 May 1191 and distr ess at the hands of Isaac Comnenus, Richard I descended on the Island on 6 May 1911 with a fury in a daring night attack and Isaac quickly c 
1 CONC ame to terms with him.   On 11 May 1191, Richard met with Guy de Lusig nan who urged him to make haste on his trip to the Holy Land.  Richar d set sail again aboard the 'Trenc-la-Mer'  for Acre (Acco).  They fir st arrived at Tyre
1 CONC  on 5 June 1191, but were not allowed to drop ancho r by Conrad of Montferrat and so on 8 June they landed at Acre, which he also captured with the aide of the previously built wooden castle, a rampart where his archers could rain
1 CONC  down arrows on the defenders o f Acre.  Phillippe came to the waters edge to greet Richard I at the t ime of his arrival.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard received a frenzied welcome upon his arrival at Acre, which th en had been under siege for two years.  Phillippe had delayed the fina l attack upon Acre until Richard's arrival.  His allies at Acre includ ed Phillippe of F
1 CONC rance, Leopold of Austria and Conrad of Montferrat. It quickly became apparent to Richard that Philip had little knowledg e of military matters.  Some of Richard's siege machines at Acre were attended by priests.  The machines wer
1 CONC e usually trained on the cities gates and operated day and night.  He also employed stone throwers an d sappers during the siege in addition to the siege machines he had br ought with him.  Richards advance force at Acre was led b
1 CONC y Hubert Walt er, a nephew of Ranulf de Glanville. The capture of Acre was the majo r success of the entire campaign and toook about one month.  The citie s defenders, with Saladins permission surrendered on 12 July 1191, aft er a
1 CONC s many it is reported as a combined 100,000 total casualities.  Af ter the victory, Richard and Phillippe partitioned the city and Richar d took over the Royal palace.  Richard and Phillippe promised the non- aligned barons that t
1 CONC hey would be rewarded for their service.  Phillip pe left the crusade, claiming illness and had left Hugh of Burgundy i n charge of his army in Palestine, but the funds left to support them quickly ran short and Richard was forced
1 CONC  to advance them from his own monies.  At Acre, Richards army took over 3,000 prisoners.  Richard, B erengaria and Joan moved into the ducal royal palace.  A treaty was ar ranged with Saladin that allowed for a trade of the Holy C
1 CONC ross and 150 0 Christian prisoners for an equal amount of his prisoners, but Saladi n stalled - having trouble meeting the deamnds of the truce, and enrag ed by the delay, Richard ordered that his troops move down to the wel l nea
1 CONC r Tell al'Ayadiyya and occupy the plain between that point and Te ll Kaisan. There he ordered that all the chained prisoners be brought out and all the prisoners be killed on 20 August 1191.  It became cold -blooded butchery, host
1 CONC ages were beheaded, throats were slit, bodies m utilated and bellies slit open to collect any swallowed gold coins.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard and the remanant of his army (not enthusiastically), on 22 Aug ust 1191, moved down the coastal roads south towards Jerusalem and sur vived an attack by Saladin on 7 September 1191 at Arsuf.  The army fol lowed the great T
1 CONC emplar battle flag of Beau-Seant carried on a cart o f its own pulled by four horses.  The army shawdowed by Saladin marche d through Haifa and Caesarea.  On 7 September 1191, Richard engaged Sa ladin at the Battle of Arsuf where 
1 CONC with a charge of the heavy cavalry of knights won the day.  On 9 September 1191, Richard took Jaffa (mode rn Tel Aviv) which had been razed to the ground by Saladin's retreatin g forces.  Much of the time in the next several weeks
1 CONC  was spent involv ed with construction and rebuilding in the area of Jaffa.  In preparat ion for a move against Jerusalem, Richard ordered work by all from sol dier to King, but Leopold of Austria saw himself above such labors an 
1 CONC d soon embittered at Richard, also left taking his army with him.  By December of 1191, Richard reached Beit-Nuba, twelve miles from Jerusal em, and had established his winter headquarters at Ramla.  He was howe ver to far removed
1 CONC  from his supply fleet to attempt a siege of Jerusal em.  Before Christmas of 1191, Richard had moved his headquarters to L atrun.  During the remaining days of December and in early January of 1192, Richard made an initial move o
1 CONC n Jeruslem, but had returned to Ra mla by 10 January 1192.  Richard soon led his army south towards Ascal on on the road to Egypt so as to secure his southern flank.  By 17 Ma y 1192, Richard had arrived at Darum (25 miles south o
1 CONC f Ascalon) whic h surrendered unconditionally and Richard turned it over to Henry de C hampagne and within a few months was back at Beit-Nuba.  On 23 June 11 92, Richard gained a complete tactical suprise victory at Kuwaifa on a s
1 CONC upply caravan destined to supply Saladin.  His allies had negotiate d a seprate peace with Saladin.  Later, Richard prepared to move on Je rusalem from Jaffa, but was initially halted by an illness theat predi cated a truce with S
1 CONC aladin's brother.
1 CONT
1 CONT Phillippe II, in the meantime, returned to France and schemed with Ric hard's brother John. The Crusade failed in its primary objective of li berating the Holy Land from Moslem Turks, but did have a positive resu lt- easier access
1 CONC  to the region for Christian pilgrims through a truc e with Saladin.  Realizing that the purpose of the crusade was lost, R ichard turned towards Bethany and sent Hubert Walter to Saladin to neg otiate a treaty.  Richard had perfo
1 CONC rmed admirably against Saladin, nea rly taking the Holy City twice.  A truce was finally arranged between Richard and Saladin, 'the peace of Ramla' on 2 September 1192, which l eft a small coastal strip incorporating Acre and Jaff
1 CONC a under the rule of Eleanors grandson Henry of Campagne - King of Jerusalem.  The Chris tians would be allowed to have guaranteed free access to Jerusalem an d the peace would be guranteed for a period of three years.  Richard r e
1 CONC turned to Acre and the royal apartment with Barangaria and Joan for a short time.  Acre and Jaffa were left to the Christians, and their r ight to make pilgrimage to Jerusalem assured.  After sending Berangari a, Joan, and Isaac C
1 CONC omnenus (still in silver chains) forth on their jo urney home under Stephen de Turnham, Richard spent the next 10 days o r so in secret preparations for his own departure.  He canceled all de bts that were owed him in Acre.  Richa
1 CONC rd, having received word of his brother John's treachery, decided to return home and sailed from Acre on 9 October 1192, with the intention of arriving home in England by C hristmas.
1 CONT
1 CONT He received a series of warnings that several princes and pirates wer e lying in wait for him.  On the progress he initially sailed to the w est across the Mediterranean aboard a large buss, possibly the 'Franch e-Nef' some 100 fe
1 CONC et long and 40 feet wide, on which Richards cabin wa s under the stern platform.  He sailed with no escort or fleet in atte ndance and made his way up the coast to Tre and beyond until they coul d see Beruit.  From there, they tur
1 CONC ned west into the Mediterranean, ag ain passing Limassolon, then north along Asia Minor heading for Rhodes .  They moved past Karpathos and headed for Crete.  They entered the I onia sea and made for Corfu from which he embarked o
1 CONC n 11 November 1192 .  They reached Italy's east coast and made for Brindisi, but warned o f trouble from local princes amde his way south around cape St. Maria di Leuca and through the Gulf of Toranto.  His objective was the south
1 CONC  ern coast of France,  but he found his way blocked along the southern coast of France at Marseilles.  He returned to the area of Pisa on Ita lys west coast for supplies.  At Sicily, it is possible that he was wa rned by Tancred o
1 CONC f dangers along the southern French coast.  Setting s ail east again, storms blew his vessel as far as Corfu.  The individua ls who still were with Richard included William de l'Etang, Baldwin o f Bethune, Philip of Poitou and his
1 CONC  Royal Admiral Robert de Turnham. There he hired two Romanian private vessels that took him up the Adria tic.  He tried to put in at Ragusa (todays Dubrovnik) an independant c ity-state on the Dalmatian coast, but his vessel was f
1 CONC orced ashore at the island of Lokrum by a storm.  It is reported that Richards intende d destination at this point was either Pola (Pula) or Zadar (Zara), no w Croatian ports, then under the control of Bela III.  Richard anticip a
1 CONC ted that he might be safe within Bela's domain.  He made his way nort h for the northern Italiam coast, but tempests forced him past Pula an d he was subsequently shipwrecked on the coast near Istria (Istra). H e possibly landed i
1 CONC n a swampy area of an estuary of the Tagliamento ri ver or near Friuli on 10 December 1192.  He made his way north throug h the lands of Bela III of Hungary, disquised as a pilgrim (some sourc es indicate as a wealthy merchant) na
1 CONC med Hugo (Hugh), made their way n orthwest towards Udine, followed the old Roman road Via Julia Augusta into the Julian Alps crossing at the Pontebba pass and eventually was obliged to cross into Austria and arrived in the town of
1 CONC  Gynatia (Erdb ert, or Ganina per Ralph of Coggeshall).  There on 21 December 1192, R ichard sent his German boy servant into the town to buy supplies and t he boy made show of gold coins given him by Richard as well as a ring and
1 CONC  acted pompously.  The local citizens were attracted by these actio ns and eventually Richard was recognized by Duke Leopolds loyal vasse l Count Mainard of Gortz (Gorz or Gorizia).  Gortz pursued Richard wh o finally agreed to su
1 CONC rrender, but only to Leopold V of Austria near V ienna. He was in possession of the Great Seal of England at the time o f his capture, which was stolen by the soldiers that took him into cus tody.  Later it was returned to Richard
1 CONC  who kept it with him during th e rest of his confinement.  At the time of his capture, he was less th an fifty miles from Moravia which would have offered him safe haven. He initially was imprisoned at Leopolds palace, then at Kr
1 CONC em and final ly at the castle of Durnstein by 25 December 1192, above the Danube ri ver (ref: John Gillinhham, ‘Richard the Lionheart, 1978).  While at Du rnstein, Richard is reported to have been held in the north tower.  Th ere,
1 CONC  his keeper was Hadmar II von Kuenring a trusted baron of Leopold.
1 CONT Later, Richard was placed in the hands of the Holy Roman Emperor Henr y VI., and on 6 January 1193, Richard was moved to Regensburg on the D anube, but quickly moved back to Durstein.  By 19 March 1193, Richard was seen on the roa
1 CONC d to Speyer near Ochesfurt with an imperial escort and the party included William de Longchamp.  on 14 February 1193, Hen ry VI and Leopold V met at Wurzburg to discuss the release of Richard .  They agree that Richard would pay a
1 CONC  ranson of 100,000 marks of whic h Leopold would receive hald,  that Richard's niece would be betrothe d to one of Leopolds sons,  that Isaac Comnenus and his daughter woul d be released, and that Richard would provide Henry with 
1 CONC 50 fully arme d galleys, 100 knights, and 50 crossbow-men.  It was also agree that t here would be a trail of Richard at Henry's court at Speyer on Palm Su nday 1193.  One of the conditions for this release by Leopold, was tha t R
1 CONC ichard would be forced to persuade the Pope to absolve Leopold and Henry V from the sin of capturing a returning crusader.  On 20 March 1 193, Richard met with Henry at Speyer to discuss the ransom.  Richard rejected all the terms
1 CONC  of the agreement and his trial began on 23 Marc h.  Later he was moved to the castle of Trifels near Annweiller on th e Swabian border.
1 CONT Richard was brought before the Imperial Council (Diet) at Speyer on th e Rhine on 23 March 1193.  There Richard was accused by Boniface de Mo ntferrat of being responsible for the death of his brother Conrad.  I n April of 1193, R
1 CONC ichard informed his mother Eleanor that he had concl uded 'a mutual treaty of love' with Henry VI.  Henry VI dismissed the charges against Richard with a 'Kiss of peace.  During the early part of April 1193, Richard was placed in 
1 CONC the Trifels castle west of Speyer , above the small town of Annweiler, in solitary confinement.  He was subsequently moved from Trifels to Henry's main palace at Hagenau by m id April 1193, where he was treated more as a royal gue
1 CONC st rather than a prisoner.  Richard became a great aide to Henry during his difficult ies with his princes from the Rhineland during his captivity at Hagena u.  He was permitted to hold court at Worms and Speyer and to attend t o 
1 CONC the business of his kingdom via constant contact with his mother an d councillors.  At Worms, in June 1193, Richard and Henry VI agreed t o the betrothal of Richards niece, Eleanor of Brittany to the son of L eopald V of Austria. 
1 CONC  With Richard's consent, in December 1193, his mo ther Eleanor appointed Hubert Walter as Justiciar of England.  Initial ly, Richards date of release was to be on 17 January 1194, but on tha t day, emissaries of John Lackland and 
1 CONC Phillippe of France arrived at Henry VI's court with a counter-offer and the release was put off.  He nry VI finally released Richard into the custody of his mother Eleanor , after much barganing on 4 February 1194 at Mentz (Mainz
1 CONC ), (Norgate, 'John Lackland', p. 49).  The next day, Richard, Eleanor and their par ty started their journey back to England.  On the return trip to Engla nd, Richard was accompanied by William de Longchamp.  They spent thre e day
1 CONC s at Cologne as guests of Archbishop Adolf.  They traveled on int o the lands of the Duke of Louvain and then Brussels.  On 4 March 119 4 at Antwerp, they boarded the vessel 'Trenchemer', commanded by Steph en de Turnham at the Sc
1 CONC heldt estuary for their voyage to England.  The y initially sailed to Swine and finally on 12 March 1194, landed at Sa ndwich.  After visiting Canterbury, they passed through Rochester wher e they were joined by Hubert Walter, and
1 CONC  arrived in London on 23 Marc h 1194  (ref: 'The Maligned Monarch', Alan Lloyd, p. 71 suggests that Richards entry occured on 16 March).
1 CONT The administrative machinery of Henry II insured the continuance of Ri chards royal authority, as Richard was unable to return to his realm u ntil 1194.  Richards reign saw the development of the royal messanger service known as '
1 CONC Nuncios Regis'.  After one day in London, Richard ma de his way north to Edmund St. Bury and from there set out for Notting ham castle.  On 30 March 1194, Richard presided over a meeting of the Great Council of England at Nottingh
1 CONC am Castle.  There, Richard re-inst alled William de Longchamp as Chancellor of England.  After the meetin g of the Great Council, at Nottingham Castle, Richard visited Sherwoo d forest and then Southwell where he met with King Wil
1 CONC liam of Scotlan d regarding territorial concessions and financial compensation.  Richa rd then moved on to Winchester and there held a second coronation on 1 7 April 1194 and received the crown from Hubert Walter - Archbishop o f 
1 CONC Canterbury (with eleven attending bishops), at Winchester Cathedral .  From Winchester, Richard moved with his court to the area of Portsm outh.  Upon his return to England, he crushed a coup attempt by his br other John.  On 12 M
1 CONC ay 1194, Richard and his mother Eleanor left Engla nd and set sail from Portsmouth for Barfleur.  They would never set fo ot in England again.  His fleet included about 100 vessels.  From Barf leur, he made his way toward Bayeux,,
1 CONC  then Caen and on to Lisieux wher e he was reunited with his brother John.  For the rest of Richard's re ign, he was engaged in a bitter and unceasing struggle with the Frenc h King Phillippe, who at the time was involved with a s
1 CONC iege of Richard s city Verneuil in May of 1194, which Phillippe quickly abandoned in t he face of the threat from Richard.  Richard moved on to Montmirail, T oraine and then Loches, where he joined his brother-in-law Sancho VII . 
1 CONC  In June of 1194, Richard won a decisive battle over Phillippe at th e battle of Freteval, where he came close to capturing Phillippe, afte r falling on the rear guard of Phillippes retreat.  On 23 July 1194, t he church negotiate
1 CONC d a truce (Treaty of Tillieres - July 1194) betwee n Phillippe and Richard until November of 1195.  During the last month s of 1194, Richard traveled through Argenten, Alencon, Chinon and Roue n where he enjoyed hunting with his d
1 CONC ogs and friends that included hi s half-brother Geoffrey, William de Longchamp, Baldwin of Bethune, Rob ert de Wancy, Warin FitzGerold and William de l'Estang.  Starting in 1 194 and for the next four years Richard gradually pushe
1 CONC d Phillippe bac k and slowly regained the land that Phillippe had taken in Normandie .  During this period, Pope Celestine III became increasing suspeciou s of Richard I.  In 1195, Richard issued an ordinance for the electio n in 
1 CONC each 'hundred' of four knights to act as keepers of the peace.  H e returned to his promiscuous ways, but was reunited with Berengaria a round Easter of 1195.  He briefly considered naming Arthur of Brittan y as his heir.  In July
1 CONC  1195, Richard renewed his active war on Philli ppe.  They met near Le Vaudreuil on different sides of the river Eure , but Phillippe withdrew from the confrontation.  The flareup failed t o develope into open hostilities.  Philli
1 CONC ppe soon made an open attack on Normandie and captured Dieppe  In August of 1195, Richard bowed to Phillippes demands and delivered Alice (Alys) of France to Phillippe .  By December of 1195, Richard and Phillippe again agree to a
1 CONC  genera l truce until they could meet at Louviers.
1 CONT In the spring of 1196, Richard still fearful of invasion by Phillippe , commenced building Chateau Gaillard 'Sauch Castle' on the rock of Le s Andelys on the Norman frontier.  It was designed to have overlappin g fields of fire.  
1 CONC Richard often referred to the castle as 'his daught er'.  Also in the spring of 1196, Richard named Arthur of Brittany as his heir.  On 7 January 1196, Richard and Phillippe agree to a permane nt peace at Louviers in Normandie (Tr
1 CONC eaty of Louviers).  Even though P hillippe was able to take Aumale where he repulsed Richard, Richard wa s able to regain Gaillon and much of his territory previously taken du ring his captivity, including the Vexin.  During the f
1 CONC irst three month s of 1197, Richard was personally involved with construction at Chatea u Gaillard.  By 1197, Richard and Phillippe were again at war in Norma ndie.  In June of 1197, Richard was able to conclude an alliance with t
1 CONC he counts of Flanders and Boulogne.  That same month, he captured th e castle at Dangu and then proceeded on a successful invastion of Berr y.  In 1198,  in response to a raid by Phillippe, Richard invaded Fren ch territory and la
1 CONC id siege to Phillipps castles at Courcelles and Bur riz, both of which he siezed. Richard overran the Vixen, defeated Phil lippe near Gisors and captured over 100 French knights.  In October o f 1198, Hubert Walter attempted again
1 CONC  to bring the two adversaries tog ether and they agreed to a temporary truce until 13 January 1199.  Tha t same year, he placed a carycage tax on lands which would be accesse d by a sworn and elected jury.  The taxes during Richar
1 CONC d's reign left the people of London ripe for rebellion.  On 13 January 1199, Richard met with Phillippe on the River Siene between Vernon and Les Andelys a nd agreed to a five year truce.  Richard arrived at Chinon on 7 March 1199
1 CONC .  On the evening of 26 March 1199, Richard left his dinner table intent on practicing with his crossbow.  He was involved in a siege o f Lord Achard's castle at Chalus.  He was not wearing his armour.  An opposing archer loosed a
1 CONC  shot that penetrated Richard's left shoulder .  In a few days, the arrow having been removed, the wound inflamed an d he sent for his mother Eleanor.  He bequeathed England and his land s in France to his younger brother John.  H
1 CONC e required an oath of loyal ty to his brother John from those who were with him at the end.  He al so left a large number of jewells to his nephew Otto - Emperor of the Romans.  Richard asked that he be buried at this fathers side
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard died April 6, 1199, from a wound received from an arrow while inspecting the siege works before the castle of Chalus in the Limousin .  The shaft from the arrow was awkwardly removed and gangrene sat in .  Near his death, 
1 CONC Richard finally reconciled his position with his la te father, as evidenced by Sir Richard Baker in A Chronicle of the Kin gs of England: "The remorse for his undutifulness towards his father, was living in him till he died; for a
1 CONC t his death he remembered it wit h bewailing, and desired to be buried as near him as might be, perhap s as thinking they should meet the sooner, that he might ask him forgi veness in another world." (ref: Ralph of coggeshall, Ral
1 CONC ph of Diceto) .  Richard willed his jewels to his nephew Otto - Holy Roman Emperor .  On his deathbed, he changed his mind regarding his kingdom and cont inental possessions.  Instead of Arthur, he choose his younger brothe r John
1 CONC .  This decision may have been the result of his mother Eleanor s presence at the time of his death. Richard's prowess and courage in battle earned him the nickname Coeur De Lion ("heart of the lion"), bu t the training of his mot
1 CONC her's court is revealed in a verse Richard co mposed during his German captivity:
1 CONT
1 CONT No one will tell me the cause of my sorrow Why they have made me a pri soner here. Wherefore with dolour I now make my moan; Friends had I ma ny but help have I none. Shameful it is that they leave me to ransom,T o languish here t
1 CONC wo winters long.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is reported that Richard captured the popular imagination with his crusading zeal and chivalry.  He  was nicknamed 'Coeur-de-lion'.  He w as buried 'with royal pomp' (ref: Adam of Evnsham) at Fontevrault Abbe y at his fathers f
1 CONC eet.  His heart was sent to Rouen for burial.  Hugh of Lincoln officiated at the funeral and both his Queen Berengaria an d the dowager Queen, his mother Eleanor were the chief morners.  Richa rd has also been styled as a troubado
1 CONC ur, musician and poet in his own right.  At least one source indicates that in additionp to his illegit imate son Philip, that Richard may also have had an illegitimate daugh ter.
1 CONT
1 CONT Additional resources for Richard include:
1 CONT
1 CONT 1.) David Boyle: 'The Troubadours Song: the Capture & Ransom of Richar d the Lionheart', 2005.
1 CONT Few monarchs are surrounded by as much romantic lore as is
1 CONT Richard I. Remembered by his English subjects as the
1 CONT Lion-Heart, Richard's reputation was built in a time when war
1 CONT was considered a romantic art and killing in the name of one's
1 CONT God the essence of courage.
1 CONT
1 CONT Born on 8 September 1157, the second son of Henry II and
1 CONT Eleanor of Aquitaine, Richard was raised in Poitiers, the court
1 CONT of his mother, well-known for its adherence to the rules of
1 CONT courtly love, chivalry, and knighthood. The skills Richard
1 CONT learned in these years proved invaluable for he had two strikes
1 CONT against him: he was the second born son and he was not his
1 CONT father's favorite. Henry II's first son, also Henry, was
1 CONT crowned during the old King's lifetime. The Young King Henry
1 CONT died in 1183, and Richard was poised for succession, but Henry
1 CONT favored John, his third and youngest son. Richard, pushed to
1 CONT the point of open rebellion, joined with Philip II of France in
1 CONT an attempt to destroy the Angevin empire and Henry. In July,
1 CONT 1189, with his health failing, Henry accepted a humiliating
1 CONT peace and died. On 3 September 1189, Richard was crowned in
1 CONT Westminster.
1 CONT
1 CONT The coronation marked one of the few visits Richard ever made
1 CONT to England, but it also marked one of England's worst
1 CONT massacres. The coronation ceremony was to be followed by a
1 CONT banquet. By the express decree of Richard, no Jews were to
1 CONT attend. However, Jews arrived and Ralph of Diceto, quoted in
1 CONT The Plantagenet Chronicles, described the scene:
1 CONT
1 CONT ... the courtiers laid hands on the Jews and stripped
1 CONT them and flogged them and having inflicted blows, threw them
1 CONT out of the king's court. Some they killed, others they let go
1 CONT half dead. One of those Jews was so badly injured with slashes
1 CONT and wounds that he despaired of his life,and so terrified was
1 CONT he by the fear of death that he accepted baptism of William,
1 CONT prior of the church of St. Mary of York, and was christened
1 CONT William. And in this way he avoided the danger of death, the
1 CONT hands of his persecutors. The people of London, following the
1 CONT courtier's example, began killing, robbing and burning Jews.
1 CONT Yet a few of the Jews escaped that massacre, shutting
1 CONT themselves up in the Tower of London or hiding in the houses of
1 CONT their friends.[:ITAL]
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after the coronation, Richard renewed his vow to fight in
1 CONT the Crusades with Philip II of France. (In England only long
1 CONT enough to raise money for his Crusade, Richard jokingly
1 CONT remarked that he would have sold London if only he could find a
1 CONT buyer.) Richard and Philip set out for the Holy Land in the
1 CONT summer of 1190, but the following winter became waylaid with
1 CONT transportation problems near the city Messina in Sicily. Camped
1 CONT outside the walls of the city, Richard found himself at odds
1 CONT with the King of Sicily, Tancred. Tancred had been unkind to
1 CONT Joan, Richard's sister, and widow of the previous King of
1 CONT Sicily. When fighting broke out between the Crusaders and the
1 CONT people of Messina, Richard seized Messina for ransom and
1 CONT exacted a tidy profit from Tancred. The forced encampment at
1 CONT Messina, however, strained the good relations between Philip
1 CONT and Richard. Richard had vowed to marry Alice, Philip's sister,
1 CONT and Philip thought Richard to be dragging his heels in the
1 CONT matter Richard, in reality, hesitated, some say because he
1 CONT believed Alice to have been his father's lover. Either way,
1 CONT when Richard's mother arrived in Sicily with an alternative
1 CONT bride, Berengaria of Navarre, Philip stormed off.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Richard sailed from Sicily in April 1191, he brought
1 CONT Berengaria and Joan with him. The ship carrying the two
1 CONT princesses was separated from Richard's fleet and nearly fell
1 CONT into the hands of the King of Cyprus. Richard, in a daring
1 CONT military operation, rescued the princesses and captured the
1 CONT island of Cyprus. While Richard's capture of Messina had done
1 CONT much to earn him the reputation of Lion-Heart, the Cyprus
1 CONT maneuver enshrined his reputation, for Cyprus was strategically
1 CONT important for generations of Crusaders.
1 CONT
1 CONT In June 1191, Richard arrived at the sieged city of Acre. His
1 CONT forces had the Muslims of Acre surrounded, but Richard and the
1 CONT other Christian forces were in turn surrounded by the great
1 CONT Muslim leader, Saladin. Encouraged by the arrival of the
1 CONT Richard and his army, the Christians pressed on and Acre fell.
1 CONT The victory was tainted, however, when Richard and Duke Leopold
1 CONT of Austria quarreled over the spoils of Acre and left Leopold
1 CONT feeling cheated. Richard ordered all prisoners taken at Acre
1 CONT killed and then led his army to Jaffa. Followed by Saladin, the
1 CONT Crusaders were never able to enter Jerusalem, stopping a mere
1 CONT twelve miles outside the city. When Saladin agree to a three
1 CONT years' truce, Richard began the long journey home. While on the
1 CONT journey Richard was captured by Duke Leopold of Austria and
1 CONT held for ransom for over a year. The barons of England
1 CONT bargained for his release and eventually bought Richard's
1 CONT freedom for 100,000.
1 CONT
1 CONT While Richard was in prison, his brother John joined forces
1 CONT with Richard's former friend, Philip II of France, and as a
1 CONT result lost important Angevin castles and territories. Upon his
1 CONT release, Richard visited England for a short while and busied
1 CONT himself with the task of winning back territories lost by John.
1 CONT This end had been accomplished when, in April 1199, while
1 CONT fighting a small skirmish in Limousin, Richard the Lion-Heart
1 CONT received a fatal wound and died. The age of chivalry lost one
1 CONT of its most charismatic figures, and England lost one of its
1 CONT most beloved kings. The stage was now set for King John, for
1 CONT whom the troubadour Bertrand de Born wrote, "No man may ever
1 CONT trust him, for his heart is soft and cowardly."
1 CONT
1 CONT Biographical information from The Lives of the Kings and Queens
1 CONT of England, ed. Antonia Fraser , The Oxford Illustrated History
1 CONT of the British Monarchy, ed. John Cannon and Ralph Griffiths,
1 CONT and The Oxford Book of Royal Anecdotes, ed. Elizabeth Longford.
1 CONT NOTE
1 CONT GEDCOM created by TMG...
0 @N6000000002244407455@ NOTE Richard was sometimes cruel, such as when he executed 2700 prisoners of war,
1 CONT and sometimes magnanimous, but always courageous. As a King, he left the
1 CONT affairs of the state to able administrators while he went out to do battle in
1 CONT the Third Crusades, and the middle east, etc. Heavy taxation was levied to
1 CONT support his warefare antics, but it was an arrow in an insignificiant
1 CONT skirmish in 1199 which took his life. Brother John succeeded him.
0 @N6000000008176864963@ NOTE [howard.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard I, called Coeur de Lion or Lion-Hearted (1157-99), king of England (1189-99), third son of King Henry II and Eleanor of Aquitaine, born in Oxford. When he was an infant, Richard was betrothed to a daughter of the French ki
1 CONC ng Louis VII, and in 1172 he was given the duchy of Aquitaine in France, his mother's inheritance. His early years were spent in warring against his father to protect his own interests; he emerged a brilliant soldier. In 1189 he b
1 CONC ecame king of England and shortly thereafter set out on the Third Crusade. He was accompanied by the young Philip II, king of France, son of Louis VII. The Crusade proved a failure almost from the start, mainly because of the lack
1 CONC  of harmony between the two kings. In Sicily Richard quarreled with Philip and refused to marry Philip's sister as planned. Instead he married Berengaria of Navarre on Cyprus, which he conquered in 1191. After capturing Acre from 
1 CONC the Saracens that same year, Richard executed 2700 Muslim prisoners of war. It was Richard's personal valor in the Holy Land, however, rather than his ruthlessness, that made his name famous in legend. Conflict over policy in the 
1 CONC Holy Land resulted in a break between the two, and Philip returned to France alone. Richard spent months in indecisive contests against Saladin, sultan of Egypt and Syria, before making a truce by which Jerusalem was left in Salad
1 CONC in's hands. Captured en route to England by Leopold V, duke of Austria, Richard was handed over to Holy Roman Emperor Henry IV. He was released in 1194 only after paying a heavy ransom. Richard returned to England and there made p
1 CONC eace with his brother, John, later king of England, who in his absence had been conspiring with Philip to usurp the English throne. Leaving the government of England to the care of the able administrator Hubert Walter, archbishop 
1 CONC of Canterbury, Richard went to France in 1194 to wage war against the French king. Campaigns in defense of his European lands continued for five years. Victor in most of the warfare in which he engaged, Richard was fatally wounded
1 CONC  by an arrow during an insignificant skirmish in 1199.
1 CONT As king, Richard had chosen able ministers, to whom he left most matters of administration. Under his rule, however, England suffered heavy taxation, levied to support his expeditions. Sometimes cruel, sometimes magnanimous, and a
1 CONC lways courageous, Richard was well versed in the knightly accomplishments of his age and was also a poet. He was to become the hero of many legendary tales.
1 CONT "Richard I," Microsoft (R) Encarta. Copyright (c) 1994 Microsoft Corporation. Copyright (c) 1994 Funk & Wagnall's Corporation.
0 @N6000000003300400985@ NOTE Early Years
1 CONT Richard was born at Oxford in 1157. His father was Henry II (Duke of Normandy, Count of Anjou and Duke of Aquitaine), and his mother was Eleanor of Aquitaine. At the age of fourteen Richard was given the title Duke of Aquitaine. H
1 CONC e was well taught, could write in French and speak in Latin. H e even cracked a joke in Latin at the expense of a lesser learned Archbishop of Canterbury and often fought in knight's tournaments. Eleanor decided that her duchy sho
1 CONC uld go Richard and Richard became betrothed to Alice the second daughter of King Louis VII's second wife Constance.
1 CONT Conflicts with his father
1 CONT Problems arose between Richard and his father Henry as Henry did not wan t to give any kind of power him or his other sons. In 1173 Richard's brothers Henry (the Young King) and Geoffrey went to join Richard in Paris i n an effort
1 CONC  to rise up against their father. Their mother, Eleanor was captured by her husband's troops as she tried to join her sons in Parisan d was held a prisoner until Henry's death. In May 1174, Richard seized th e town of Saintes from
1 CONC  his father but quickly lost it again to Henry II's superior forces and tactics. Henry (the Young King) and Geoffrey both returned to their father's side but Richard held out until he and Henry I I met in Paris to settle their dif
1 CONC ferences. Henry II then used Richard's new found military skills of siege warfare to put down rebellious baron s in the Aquitaine area. In 1179 Richard took the supposedly impenetrable fortress town of Taillebourg within two weeks
1 CONC  and his skills became famous.
1 CONT The Young King became jealous of Richard's new found fame and fled the French court. Henry II tried to force Richard and Geoffrey to pay homage t o their elder brother the Young King, but Richard refused to do so. Richard was bein
1 CONC g compelled to swear on some holy relic so stormed out. It was then the turn of the Young King and Geoffrey to try a subdue Richard. T o achieve this, the Young King and Geoffrey sided with the barons but the barons were more inte
1 CONC rested in attacking Henry II not Richard. Again the tables were turned and Richard joined Henry II to subdue the Henry the Young King, Geoffrey and the barons.
1 CONT Heir to the throne
1 CONT In June 1183 Henry the Young King died of dysentery and Richard became heir to the throne. As Richard was now heir to the English throne Henry II hoped Richard would hand Aquitaine over to his youngest son John, but Richard refuse
1 CONC d. John joined Geoffrey against Richard but Richard won, leaving John the title 'John of Lackland'. In 1180 King Louis VII of Franc e died and his son Philippe became king of France as Philippe II. Philippe was determined to make 
1 CONC France great again and in 1187 Richard joined forces with him. Geoffrey died in a tournament in 1186 and in 1189 Henry II died at Chinon and was buried in the choir church of Fontevrault leaving Richard the new King of England. Wi
1 CONC lliam Marshal, who served under Henry II and was favored by Richard was sent to England to prepare for Richard's arrival. Queen Eleanor was freed from prison and was to reign until Richard arrived.
1 CONT As King of England
1 CONT On the 20th of July 1189 Richard was girded with the Ducal Sword, and in August Richard returned to England. Henry II's fortune was calculated a t 100,000 marks. 24,000 marks were used instantly to buy peace with Franc e. On the 3
1 CONC rd of September 1189 Richard was crowned in Westminster and o n the 11th of December Richard began planning his Crusade by selling mans ions and castles for funding. Richard had no children and in the event o f his death, John his
1 CONC  youngest brother or Geoffrey, Henry's illegitimate son could have become king. So Richard gave Geoffrey the title of Archbishop of York thus making it impossible for him to become king. He gave John land including Derbyshire, Som
1 CONC erset, Dorset, Devon, Cornwall and the Norman county of Mortain. He also made John the Earl of Gloucester an d banished him for three years to try and prevent him trying to take the throne. Richard nominated his nephew Arthur of B
1 CONC rittany as his successor even though Arthur was only four.
1 CONT
1 CONT Crusade
1 CONT On the 4th of July 1190 Richard's Crusade began (...).
1 CONT Death
1 CONT In March of 1199 Richard besieged the castle at Chalus where some treasure had been unearthed. Believing the treasure to be his own, Richard tried to take the castle but riding too close to the walls was shot in the shoulder. The 
1 CONC castle then fell and the archer who shot Richard was brought before him. Richard forgave the archer but Richard's second in command had the archer executed. Richard died of his wounds due to the lack o f proper medical attention. 
1 CONC He had arranged that after his death his brain was to be buried in the abbey of Charroux (Poitou) and his heart in Rouen (Normandy) while the rest was to lie at the feet of his father in the abbey church of Fontevrault (Anjou). On
1 CONC  his deathbed, Richard nominated his younger brother John as the heir to the throne.
1 CONT
1 CONT RICHARD I COEUR DE LION ('THE LIONHEART') (r. 1189-1199)
1 CONT Henry's elder son, Richard I (reigned 1189-99), fulfilled his main ambition by going on crusade in 1190, leaving the ruling of England to others. After his victories over Saladin at the siege of Acre and the battles of Arsuf and J
1 CONC affa, concluded by the treaty of Jaffa (1192), Richard was returning from the Holy Land when he was captured in Austria. In early 1193, Richard was transferred to Emperor Henry VI's custody.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Richard's absence, King Philip of France failed to obtain Richard's French possessions through invasion or negotiation. In England, Richard's brother John occupied Windsor Castle and prepared an invasion of England by Flemish m
1 CONC ercenaries, accompanied by armed uprisings. Their mother, Queen Eleanor, took firm action against John by strengthening garrisons and a gain exacting oaths of allegiance to the king. John's subversive activities were ended by the 
1 CONC payment of a crushing ransom of 150,000 marks of silver to the emperor, for Richard's release in 1194. Warned by Philip's famous message 'look to yourself, the devil is loosed', John fled to the French court.
1 CONT On his return to England, Richard was recrowned at Winchester in 1194. Five years later he died in France during a minor siege against a rebellious baron. By the time of his death, Richard had recovered all his lands. H is success
1 CONC  was short-lived. In 1199 his brother John became king and Philip successfully invaded Normandy. By 1203, John had retreated to England, losing his French lands of Normandy and Anjou by 1205.
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard I, Coeur de Lion
1 CONT The Lion-hearted
1 CONT Plantagenet, Angevin Line -- Reigned: 1189-1199
1 CONT 1157-1199
1 CONT Richard I, the Lion-hearted, spent much of his youth in his mother's court at Poitiers. Richard cared much more for the continental possessions o f his mother than for England. He also cared much more for his mother than for his f
1 CONC ather, Henry II.
1 CONT Family considerations influenced much of his life: he fought along side o f his brothers Prince Henry and Geoffrey in their rebellion of 1173-1174; he fought for his father against his brothers when they supported an 11 83 revolt 
1 CONC in Aquitaine; and he joined Philip II of France against his father in 1188, defeating Henry in 1189.
1 CONT Richard spent but six months of his ten-year reign in England. He acted upon a promise to his father to join the Third Crusade and departed for the Holy Land in 1190 (accompanied by his partner-rival Philip II of France). In 1191,
1 CONC  he conquered Cyprus en route to Jerusalem and performed admirably against Saladin, nearly taking the holy city twice.
1 CONT Philip II, in the meantime, returned to France and schemed with Richard' s brother John. The Crusade failed in its primary objective of liberating the Holy Land from Moslem Turks, but did have a positive result -- easier access to
1 CONC  the region for Christian pilgrims through a truce with Salad in.
1 CONT Richard received word of John's treachery and decided to return home; h e was captured by Leopold V of Austria and imprisoned by Holy Roman Emperor Henry VI.
1 CONT The administrative machinery of Henry II insured the continuance of royal authority, as Richard was unable to return to his realm until 1194. Upon his return, he crushed a coup attempt by John and regained lands lost t o Philip II
1 CONC  during the German captivity. Richard's war with Philip continued sporadically until the French were finally defeated near Gisors in 11 98.
1 CONT Richard died April 6, 1199, from a wound received in a skirmish at the castle of Chalus in the Limousin. Near his death, Richard finally reconcile d his position with his late father, as evidenced by Sir Richard Baker in A Chronic
1 CONC le of the Kings of England:
1 CONT "The remorse for his undutifulness towards his father, was living in hi m till he died; for at his death he remembered it with bewailing, and desired to be buried as near him as might be, perhaps as thinking they should meet the s
1 CONC ooner, that he might ask him forgiveness in another world."
1 CONT Richard's prowess and courage in battle earned him the nickname Coeur D e Lion ("heart of the lion"), but the training of his mother's court is revealed in a verse Richard composed during his German captivity:
1 CONT No one will tell me the cause of my sorrow
1 CONT Why they have made me a prisoner here.
1 CONT Wherefore with dolour I now make my moan;
1 CONT Friends had I many but help have I none.
1 CONT Shameful it is that they leave me to ransom,
1 CONT To languish here two winters long.
1 CONT
1 CONT -------
1 CONT [Adapted from Brundage] The death of the Emperor crippled the Crusade. Of the army which had accompanied Barbarossa on the expedition, only a minority was to give any effective service to the Latin cause. Many of the men returned 
1 CONC to Europe directly after the Emperor's death, while many of the rest were lost to the enemy on the remainder of the journey to the Holy Land.
1 CONT The French and English monarchs, meanwhile, were still readying themselves and their armies for the expedition. The two Kings did not complete their preparations until July 1190, when they met at V6zelay to set out jointly on the 
1 CONC Crusade. Their principal armies went by sea to the East, but only after many delays. The Kings had agreed much earlier that they and their armies would meet again at Messina in Sicily before starting on the principal part of their
1 CONC  sea journey. In Sicily the Crusaders became embroiled in a series of quarrels with Tancred of Lecce, the pretender to the Sicilian throne. Peace between Tancred and the Crusading Kings was patched up only in November 1190 and the
1 CONC  Kings with their armies settled down t o spend the remainder of the winter in Sicily. They sailed from Sicily in the spring of 1191; Philip Augustus left in March, Richard in April.
1 CONT Philip Augustus and his fleet made straight for Tyre, where they arrived without incident. Richard and his fleet had a more arduous voyage. Richard's fleet stopped first at Crete, then at Rhodes, and, on what was supposed to be th
1 CONC e last lap of the journey to Palestine; the fleet ran into a storm off the Island of Cyprus. This turned out to be an opportunity for conquest.
1 CONT Shortly before sundown on the vigil of St. Mark the Evangelist [Wednesday April 24, 1191] a black cloud darkened the sky. All at once a blowing storm and high winds buffeted the turbulent waves of the sea and turned back the sailo
1 CONC rs. Even before the coming of the storm, King Richard's ship s had been dispersed by the uneven winds and were making for Cyprus. These ships were thrown about by the waves during the storm, were blown back by the wind, and were d
1 CONC ashed against some rocky crags. So many men were being thrown violently about by the wind that, although the sailors tried to prevent it, three of the King's ships were shattered by the rushing waves and some of the men in them we
1 CONC re drowned. ... Among the others who drowned there was Roger, known as Malchiel, the Keeper of the King's se al. The seal was also lost. Later, Roger's body was thrown ashore by the waves and one of the common people found the sea
1 CONC l and brought it to the army in order to sell it. The seal was thus recovered and was restored to the King.
1 CONT The natives of the place pretended that their intentions were peaceful. T hey joyfully received those who escaped to land from the shipwreck. They comforted the shipwrecked men in their misfortune and brought them t o a nearby cas
1 CONC tle to refresh themselves. When the survivors got there, however, they were deprived of all their weapons and were placed in custody. This was done, it was said, lest, if they were to go out armed, they might spy on the country or
1 CONC  even get into a fight. The Cypriot Greeks claim ed that they could not do otherwise until they had ascertained the Emperor's [Isaac Dukas Comnenus, claimant to title of "emperor of Cyprus"] wishes. Our chiefs pitied our ship-wrec
1 CONC ked men who were kept in confinement and sent them clothes and other necessary things. Stephen of Tumeham, the King's Marshal and Treasurer sent them a great quantity of necessities, but, in fact, everything sent to the prisoners 
1 CONC was confiscated by the Cypriots and the keepers at the entrance of the castle where our men were confined [...].
1 CONT When he heard the pilgrims' complaints about the stealing of their money and the injuries done to them, the Emperor promised full redress: be would return the shipwrecked men's money. He even delivered four hostages in token of hi
1 CONC s good faith. Under these conditions, the pilgrims further obtained the right of free entrance and exit from the city of Limassol. Meanwhile the Emperor ordered all the warriors of his kingdom to assemble and be gathered together 
1 CONC a very strong army. On the day after his arrival [Thursday, may 22, 1191] the Emperor (disguising his scheme with peaceful words) called upon the Queens [Richard's younger sister, Queen Joanna of Sicily, and his fiancée Joanna of 
1 CONC Navarre] to come in safety.
1 CONT He alleged that they might count on him, that they would be at liberty in every particular, that there would be no molestation of their people, and that there would be no danger to fear. They declined to come, but again, the next 
1 CONC day, the Emperor, on the pretext of doing them honor, sent welcoming gifts to them: bread, goat meat, and the best wine of the Cypriot grapes, said to be unlike that of any other nation. On the third day, a Sunday, he tried to get
1 CONC  around the Queens with blandishments and to lead them astray with his wiles. They were now in a difficult position an d shifted from one alternative to the other. They were worried lest, if t hey yielded to the Emperor's persuasi
1 CONC ons, they might be made captive, while, if they steadfastly refused to accede to him, they feared that he might do them violence....
1 CONT While they were conferring and speaking sadly with one another that Sunday, the lookouts all at once spied two ships bearing directly toward them, looking like waving spikes among the frothy tips of the curling waves. While the Qu
1 CONC eens and those with them were still arguing about this unverified news, they caught sight of still more ships following the first ones. There was no delay. The naval force was followed by a multitude of ships and they were heading
1 CONC  directly for the port. Discussing this royal fleet they were jubilant with great glee, in proportion as they bad previously been desperate and despairing. Now, indeed, after many unwelcome labors, by God's providence, King Richar
1 CONC d was brought to the Island of Cyprus. On the feast of St. John before the Latin Gate [May 6] King Richard and his whole army arrived in the port of Limassol. The King, however, remained on board ship.
1 CONT When the King learned of the hardships of the shipwrecked men, of the stealing from them, and about the other things which had meanwhile befallen them, he was deeply grieved. The next day, a Monday, he sent two knights as emissari
1 CONC es to the Emperor and peacefully asked him and his men to make voluntary satisfaction for the injuries which had been done and also to restore in full the goods stolen from the shipwrecked men.
1 CONT The Emperor was quite indignant at this command, as if the greatest injury had been done to him. He heaped harsh words upon the King's emissaries and said scornfully to them: "Tut, tut, my lords" He claimed that the English King w
1 CONC as as nothing to him and, glorying mightily in his usurped imperial excellence, he believed that whatever he wished to do was quite all right.
1 CONT When the ambassadors reported his reply to the King, he was displeased with the Emperor's arrogance and with his rude reply, as well as with the treatment of his men. The King at once cried out and ordered all his men: "Arm yourse
1 CONC lves!" They obeyed immediately. The King armed himself and set off with all his men in the skiffs of the transport ships to land in the port.
1 CONT The Emperor with many forces resisted the landing parties. All sorts of obstacles and bars and every sort of impediment that could be found in the town were placed at each of the entrances to the port to ward off the attackers. Th
1 CONC ey collected the very doors and windows, which they ripped from houses, together with jars and posts, stools and stair-steps, and long timbers which they laid down, along with bucklers and shields, old galleys as well as boats whi
1 CONC ch had been deserted and left to rot, and all kinds of utensils. What else? Every kind of portable wood or stone that could be found in Limassol was gathered by the Cypriots on the shore to keep off the landing crews.
1 CONT The Emperor, moreover, armed himself and with his people patrolled the shore. The Emperor's men were ever so nicely decked out! They were carefully armed and clad in expensive, multicolored costumes, with warlike steed s which fro
1 CONC thed and chewed at their bridles and with very beautiful mules. They came out with innumerable streamers and precious golden banners waving. They were prepared for the fight, either to hold off the attacker s for a long while or e
1 CONC lse to draw out the fight courageously. They sought to frighten away our men, who were hurrying to the attack, with terrible sounding shouts, like dogs baying. The shouting affected us like dogs and the enemy hastened to attempt t
1 CONC he impossible. They had on shore some ballistas and archers; also five galleys, sufficiently well armed and full of young men experienced in naval fighting. It seemed an unequal combat to many of our men, for they were setting out
1 CONC , rowing themselves, i n a very few fragile skiffs to occupy a port full of men. Furthermore, they were deprived of many men who were exceedingly fatigued from the continual tossing of the sea. Also, the infantry were fully weighe
1 CONC d down wit h their own weapons. The natives, on the other hand, were in their own homeland and were acting entirely of their own free will.
1 CONT When our men advanced one by one into the skiffs, the nearer ones at firs t stood up to fight the balistarii and archers who were attacking them in the boats. Our balistarii turned on them and during an attack in which the two sid
1 CONC es pelted one another with rocks many of the Cypriots were killed. The rest retreated, since they could not bear up under the weigh t of the fighting. The arrows were flying thick and fast and three or four of the men who were ret
1 CONC reating flung themselves to death in the waves in order to escape from the arrows. As they fled eagerly to the fort, their men were running into each other.
1 CONT When our men had taken their galleys and had landed their own boats, balistarii and the archers, emboldened by their first success, hurled torrent s of javelins at those whom they saw trying to escape from the beach. Without delay
1 CONC , the Cypriots, who could not bear up under the brunt of our at tack, gave up the site and retreated to firmer ground. Both our balistarii and theirs were using arrows and javelins continuously. The sky seemed clouded over by them
1 CONC  and the serenity of the day was darkened by the showers of javelins. The city boiled with a throng of men and the whole are was an occupied by a multitude of balistarii who were working persistently. Victory hung in the balance a
1 CONC nd wavered as to which party it would favor. All of our men gave the foe tit for tat, but they were making no progress, while the King deliberated for a bit over sending our brave men out of the skiff s and on to the shore.
1 CONT Then, he leaped first from his barge into the sea and bravely set upon the Cypriots. Our other men imitated his steadfast attitude. Henceforth the y accompanied the King and shot arrows at those who were resisting, in order to mak
1 CONC e the Cypriots take flight. As soon as our people rushed in, their mangled battle lines gave up. There could be seen the flying rain of spears, the Greeks, who had been overcome, fleeing. You could hear the sounds of the advancing
1 CONC  men, the groans of the fallen, the cries of those who were retreating.
1 CONT When the Greeks had retreated, our men drove them back first to the tow n and then from the town to the nearby camp on the plains. While the King was pursuing the fleeing Emperor, lie acquired a mount, or horse, wit h a little bag
1 CONC  fixed behind his saddle. He mounted at once into the saddle, which had ropes instead of straps. He rushed immediately to the Emperor and said: "My Lord Emperor, come and begin a single combat with me!" The Emperor made as if to o
1 CONC bey and then immediately fled. The King then occupied the town of Limassol. He had the Queens brought from Buza and lodge d them in a villa. There, after many adventures and discomforts at sea, they refreshed themselves quietly an
1 CONC d securely.
1 CONT The King spent that night [May 7-8] - in his tents and bad his horses brought out from the transport ships. The Emperor, however, surmised that the King had no horses with him. At nightfall, when he was two leagues away from the K
1 CONC ing, the Emperor put up for the night in his tent. The next d ay, about the ninth hour, the King advanced with his horses. He found some Greeks not far away, standing with their splendid banners in an olive g rove. The King at onc
1 CONC e pursued the fugitives. Since our horses, in fact, had been tossed about at sea for a month and had been standing all the while, many of them were upset. Our men, therefore, spared the horses and pursued the enemy rather modestly
1 CONC  until, from a vantage point, they spied the Emperor's army, which had spent the night in the next valley. The n, when the Greeks had seen them, our men ceased the pursuit and halted. The Greeks began to make noises. With clamor a
1 CONC nd tumult they flung horrid sounding insults at our men. The Emperor was roused from his sleep b y this. He mounted his horse and with his army he slowly advanced toward us, up to the adjoining hill, to see what he might do about 
1 CONC engaging the armies. . . .
1 CONT The King had with him, at this point, only about fifty knights. He, indeed, was emboldened by their fear. Letting his horse go, he charged swiftly at the enemy. He broke up the enemy's crowded battle line by charging through it. H
1 CONC e dealt now with this group, now with that one, and in short order, he dispersed them all....
1 CONT The Emperor reflected upon the courage of our men and the flight of his people. Then, when he saw that he remained alone, he spurred his horse and speedily fled to the mountains. The King struck at the banner which the Emperor bor
1 CONC e and ordered the noble and remarkable banner to be reserved for himself. Our knights followed the fleeing Greeks as closely as the y could for two miles. Then they returned peacefully to our lines, moderating their speed as they 
1 CONC withdrew. The people returned to the loot and they made off with much booty: arms, valuable silk garments, and even the Emperor's tents, together with all that was in them, including gold and silver vessels, the Emperor's bed with
1 CONC  its choice appointments, and all hi s furnishings, his special helmets, breastplates, and swords. They also took a great deal of booty in flocks and beards of oxen and cattle, goat s and sheep, noble mares and colts, fat hogs, an
1 CONC d hens. They found both choice wines and a great quantity of food and they took captive the army, which consisted of an infinite multitude of men. They took so many, indeed, that the looters were disdainful because of the great mu
1 CONC ltitude of m en.
1 CONT What more can be said? Because of the great abundance of loot, desire was satiated and one gave no regard to any gift, no matter how valuable, which might be added to one's own full load.
1 CONT When all these things had been done, the King proclaimed a decree, in a voice like a town crier. He decreed that all poor peace-loving men might come and go without hindrance from his men and that they might rejoice, since their l
1 CONC iberty was preserved. Anyone who considered the King an enemy should beware, lest he fall into the hands of him or his men. He professed that he would show himself an enemy to those who were said to be hi s enemies and that he wou
1 CONC ld be to each of them as they were to him.
1 CONT A great many men afterwards flocked to the King or to his army and the Emperor thereafter took refuge in a very strong castle called Nicosia, where he was confused and sorrowful because he could not make the progress h e wished fo
1 CONC r. . . .
1 CONT [After the rest of Richard's fleet arrived in Cyprus, the Emperor and Richard met and agreed upon peace terms. However,] the following night, the Emperor fled swiftly, trusting in the darkness of night and riding on h is best tawn
1 CONC y horse. He fled from that place at the prompting of one of h is mendacious knights, a man named Pagan of Haifa. This knight declared t hat King Richard proposed to set upon the Emperor and to throw him into chains that night. The
1 CONC  Emperor was much distressed by this and, leaving hi s tents, his very good war horses, and all his clothing, he fled early in the night to the city of Famagusta.
1 CONT The King, when he heard this, began to follow him in his galleys, declaring that the Emperor had broken his word and was a perjurer. The King left in the bands of Guido the task of leading the army by the land route to Famagusta. 
1 CONC The King himself arrived there on the third day and found it deserted by men.
1 CONT The Emperor was aware that it would not be safe for him to be besieged, since if he were shut up he would be unable to escape. He therefore hid i n out-of-the-way, wooded spots so that he could fall upon our men as the y passed by
1 CONC . King Richard, when he had come to Famagusta in his galleys, ordered the seaport to be watched very closely so that if the Emperor w ere to try to flee he could be caught.
1 CONT While they waited there for three days, the Bishop of Beauvais and Drogo, of Merlo (a famous and noble man from the domains of the King of France) came as messengers to King Richard. They urged him to sail quickly to Acre for, the
1 CONC y declared, the King of France was not going to attack the city before Richard's arrival....
1 CONT The King paid no heed to the messengers and moved his army to Nicosia. Each man brought his own necessary food, for the area was deserted. They proceeded in their spread-out battle formations, for they had learned that the Emperor
1 CONC  was going to ambush them as they passed by. The King himself went in the last formation to repel any chance sudden attack.
1 CONT All at once the Emperor and about seventy Greeks leaped suddenly out o f a hiding place. Their balistarii burled spears at our men in the rear ranks, but they could not break up our formations, which stayed together i n a discipli
1 CONC ned way. The Emperor emerged from hiding slowly, like a scout. He proceeded on an irregular course so that either our formation, when it saw him, would spontaneously break up or in order that he might shoot arrows at the King when
1 CONC  he found him. After he spied the King in the last formation, he shot two poisoned arrows at him. The King was violently outraged at this. He spurred his horse toward the Emperor in order to strike him with his lance. The Emperor 
1 CONC saw him coming and slipped away. He fled at the speediest pace to his stronghold called Kantara. There he w as extremely sorrowful and confused because he could not do as he wished
1 CONT The Emperor meditated that the fates were against him. His only daughter had been captured, a fact that weighed upon his mind, while his castle s had been occupied or had surrendered, and for a long time now he had be en supported
1 CONC  rather than loved by his alienated men. Seeing that no hope of resistance remained, be decided out of necessity, though with reluctance, to seek peace and mercy. He sent messengers to lay his case before King Richard and the King
1 CONC 's spirit was eminently inclined to compassion.
1 CONT The Emperor came down from Kantara with doleful mien and dejected countenance. He came up to the King and humbled himself at the King's feet. Kneeling, be declared that he would submit to the King's mercy in all things , that he w
1 CONC ould keep neither land nor castle for himself, but that, for the rest, the King should be his lord, so long as be did not cast him into iron chains.
1 CONT The King was moved by pity. He made the Emperor arise and sit beside him.
0 @N6000000002240804640@ NOTE Richard was sometimes cruel, such as when he executed 2700 prisoners of war,
1 CONT and sometimes magnanimous, but always courageous. As a King, he left the
1 CONT affairs of the state to able administrators while he went out to do battle in
1 CONT the Third Crusades, and the middle east, etc. Heavy taxation was levied to
1 CONT support his warefare antics, but it was an arrow in an insignificiant
1 CONT skirmish in 1199 which took his life. Brother John succeeded him.
0 @N6000000003658760853@ NOTE From Plantagenet Ancestry:
1 CONT
1 CONT RICHARD OF ENGLAND (nicknamed Coeur-de-Lion or Lionheart), Knt., Duke of Normandy, Duke of Aquitaine, Count of Poitou, 3rd but eldest surviving son, born at Oxford 8 Sept. 1157.  In 1169 he was affianced to marry Alice of France, 
1 CONC daughter of Louis VII le Jeune ... King of France, by his 2nd wife, Constance, daughter of Alfonso VII, King of Castile and León.  She was born about 1160.  In 1169 he did homage to King Louis for his mother's inheritance, the duc
1 CONC hy of Aquitaine, and, in the following year, he was acknowledged as Duke.  In 1173-4 he joined his brothers in their rebellion against their father.  In the period, 1175-77, he established his authority in Aquitaine, forcing the C
1 CONC ount of Toulouse to do him homage.  IN 1177 Kign Louis demanded the immediate marriage of Richard and his daughter, Alice, but the matter was submitted to arbitration.  On his older brother, Henry's death in 1183, Richard became h
1 CONC eir to the English crown.  His father called on him to give up the duchy of Aquitaine to his younger brother, John, which led to a fresh war.  In 1185, on his father's orders, he resigned the duchy to his mother, Queen Eleanor, wh
1 CONC o was released from her ten years of captivity.  In 1187 the French king, Philippe Auguste, led an army into Barry and besieged Richard and John at Châteauroux.  A great battle was averted only by the intervention of the nobles.  
1 CONC In 1188 Richard did homage to the French king for all his French possessions.  In 1189 war broke out again.  King Henry, his father, refused to assent to Richard's marriage to Alice, ro to acknowledge Richard as his heir.  IN July
1 CONC  1189 Henry was forced to sign a treaty yielding every point.  On Henry's death 6 July 1189, Richard succeeded as King of England, and was crowned at Westminster Abbey 3 Sept. 1189.  He immediately set about organizing an army to 
1 CONC join the French and Germans on the Third Crusade, whose aim was to recover Jerusalem, captured from the westerners by the Muslims in 1187.  Richard left England 11 Dec. 1189; secured Acre and Jaffa and defeated the Muslims in the 
1 CONC Battle of Arsuf, but his forces were not sufficiently strong to gain Jerusalem.  He had to be content with making a truce with the Islamic leader, Saladin, who much admired him.  In March 1190/1 he voided his marriage contract wit
1 CONC h Alice of France.  He married ... BÉRENGÈRE ... OF NAVARRE, daughter of Sancho VI el Sabio, King of Navarre. ... She was bron about 1163.  They had no issue.  On Richard's journey home, he was imprisoned in Germany by the Duke of
1 CONC  Austria, who he had rashly insulted in the Holy Land.  He was released in 1194 on payment of a huge ransom; returned to England 13 March 1194.  Upon his return, Richard turned his formidable military talent to wage war against th
1 CONC e French king.  In 1199, during a minor siege at Châlus in Limousin, RICHARD I, KING OF ENGLAND was fatally injured by a crossbow bolt; and died there testate 6 April 1199.  He was buried at Fontevrault Abbey. ... In 1204 his wido
1 CONC w, Bérengère, ceded all her rights to the castles of Falaise, Domfront, and Bonneville-sur-Touque to King Philippe Auguste of France, and Chateau-du-Loir to Guillaume des Roches, in exchange of the vill of le Mans and its dependen
1 CONC cies and 1,000 marks sterling.  In 1216 she founded a chapel of religious hospitaliers of Jerusalem at Thorée in Maine.  In 1230 she founded a Cistercian abbey at l'Epau, near Le Mans.  She died 23 Dec. 1230, and was buried at l'É
1 CONC pau Abbey.
