0 @N6000000002187590215@ NOTE Geoffrey II, Duke of Brittany
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT English Royalty 
1 CONT House of Plantagenet 
1 CONT
1 CONT Armorial of Plantagenet 
1 CONT Henry II 
1 CONT    William, Count of Poitiers 
1 CONT    Henry, Count of Anjou 
1 CONT    Richard I the Lionheart 
1 CONT    Geoffrey II, Duke of Brittany 
1 CONT    John 
1 CONT    Matilda, Duchess of Saxony 
1 CONT    Leonora, Queen of Castile 
1 CONT    Joan, Queen of Sicily 
1 CONT Geoffrey, Earl of Richmond, Duke of Brittany (23 September 1158 – 19 August 1186) was Duke of Brittany between 1181 and 1186, through his marriage with the heiress Constance. Geoffrey was the fourth son of King Henry II of England
1 CONC  and Duchess Eleanor of Aquitaine.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents [hide]
1 CONT 1 Family 
1 CONT 2 Life 
1 CONT 3 Death 
1 CONT 4 Fictional portrayals 
1 CONT 5 Sources 
1 CONT 6 External links 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Family
1 CONT He was a younger maternal half-brother of Marie de Champagne and Alix of France. He was a younger brother of William, Count of Poitiers, Henry the Young King, Matilda of England and Richard I of England. He was also an older broth
1 CONC er of Leonora of Aquitaine, Joan of England and John of England.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Henry arranged for Geoffrey to marry Constance, the heiress of Brittany. Geoffrey was invested with the duchy, and he and Constance were married in July 1181. Geoffrey and Constance would have three children, one born posthum
1 CONC ously:
1 CONT
1 CONT Eleanor, Fair Maid of Brittany (1184-1241) 
1 CONT Maud/Matilda of Brittany (1185- before May 1189) 
1 CONT Arthur I, Duke of Brittany (1187-1203) 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Life
1 CONT Geoffrey was fifteen years old when he joined the first revolt against his father, and was later reconciled to Henry in 1174, when he participated in the truce at Gisors (when Richard was absent) and later, when Richard reconciled
1 CONC  at a place between Tours and Amboise. Geoffrey prominently figured in the second revolt of 1183, fighting against Richard, in behalf of Henry the Young King.
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey was a good friend of Philip Augustus of France, and the two statesmen were frequently in alliance against King Henry. Geoffrey spent much time at Philip's court in Paris, and Philip made him his seneschal. There is eviden
1 CONC ce to suggest that Geoffrey was planning another rebellion with Philip's help during his final period in Paris in the summer of 1186. As a participant in so many rebellions against his father, Geoffrey acquired a reputation for tr
1 CONC eachery. Gerald of Wales said the following of him: He has more aloes than honey in him; his tongue is smoother than oil; his sweet and persuasive eloquence has enabled him to dissolve the firmest alliances and his powers of langu
1 CONC age to throw two kingdoms into confusion.
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey also was known to attack monasteries and churches in order to raise funds for his campaigns. This lack of reverence for religion earned him the displeasure of the Church and also of the majority of chroniclers who were to
1 CONC  write the definitive accounts of his life.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Death
1 CONT Geoffrey died on August 19, 1186, at the age of twenty-eight, in Paris. There are two versions of his death. The more common first version, is that he was trampled dead in a jousting tournament. At his funeral, a grief-stricken Ph
1 CONC ilip was said to have attempted jumping into the coffin. Roger of Hoveden's chronicle is the source of this version; the detail of Philip's hysterical grief is from Gerald of Wales.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the second version, in the chronicle of the French Royal clerk Rigord, Geoffrey died of sudden acute abdominal pain, which reportedly struck immediately after his speech to Philip, boasting his intention to lay Normandy to wast
1 CONC e. Possibly, this version was an invention of its chronicler; sudden illness being God's judgement of an ungrateful son plotting rebellion against his father, and for his irreligiosity. Alternatively, the tournament story may be a
1 CONC n invention, by Philip, to prevent Henry II's discovery of a plot; inventing a social reason, a tournament, for Geoffrey's being in Paris, Philip obscured their meeting's true purpose. See [1]
1 CONT
1 CONT Geoffrey was buried at Notre Dame Cathedral.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT See also: Dukes of Brittany family tree – British monarchs family tree – Other politically important horse accidents
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Fictional portrayals
1 CONT With a character closely resembling that given by Gerald of Wales above, Geoffrey appears as a major character in the James Goldman play The Lion in Winter. In the 1968 film version of the play, Geoffrey is played by John Castle a
1 CONC nd in the 2003 film version the role is portrayed by John Light.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Sources
1 CONT Everard, Judith. Charters of Duchess Constance of Brittany and her Family, 1171-1221, 1999 
1 CONT Everard, Judith. Brittany and the Angevins: Province and Empire, 1158-1203, 2000 
1 CONT Gillingham, John. The Life and Tmes of Richard I, 1973 
1 CONT Reston, James. Warriors of God: Richard the Lion-Heart and Saladin in the Third Crusade, 2001 
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] External links
1 CONT Hoveden on the The 1183 Revolt 
1 CONT The Medieval Sourcebook contains many primary sources including Hoveden and Gerald of Wales, some of which pertains to Geoffrey 
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Conan IV Duke of Brittany
1 CONT 1181–1186 Succeeded by
1 CONT Constance 
1 CONT
1 CONT -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 CONT Duke of Brittany (1166 -1186) as Georffrey II. Earl of Richmond (1181 m - 1.). Accidentally killed in a tournament. Portrayed by John Castle in the 1968 film, *Lion in Winter*. Source: RoyaList 
