0 @N6000000005599114402@ NOTE Geoffrey is styled as the most handsome of Henry and Eleanor's sons. He was betrothed to the heiress of Brittany, by his father as early a s 1166.  Geoffrey is styled as one of the most difficult of Henry II' s sons and his friend
1 CONC ship with Philip II of France became a great sour ce of difficulty for his father.  He was enthroned at Rennes Cathedra l in May 1169 with the ducal crown of Brittany.  He was almost as adep t as his older brother Richard militari
1 CONC ly.  In May of 1173, with King Louis VII, Geoffrey moved against his fathers forces in the Vexin.  I n 1182, Geoffrey joined with his older brother Henry 'the young King' in alliance with Bertran de Born (Burn) against their broth
1 CONC er Richard' s harsh rule in Aquitaine.  In February 1183, Henry - the young King, and Geoffrey laid siege to the a fortified church at Gorre, a short di stance from Limoges, where they were taken by suprise by their brothe r Richa
1 CONC rd, prisoners were taken and Richard ordered some of them drown ed  and others to have their throats slit while the rest were blinded.
1 CONT
1 CONT In June of 1183, when his older brother Henry died, Aquitaine was to h ave reverted to his brother John.  Richard had no intention of allowin g this to happen and their father Henry told John that it would be hi s if he could take
1 CONC  it.  John and Geoffrey marched on Poitou, looting a nd ravishing the area as they went.  It is said that Geoffrey spent th e remaining years of his life in aimless looting and killing.  Geoffre y entered a tournament in Paris, wa
1 CONC s thrown from his horse and was tra mpled to death.  His mother Eleanor received news of his death while s he was in confinement at Winchester.  Among those present at his funer al at Notre Dame, were his wife Constance of Brittan
1 CONC y - then pregnant , his half-sister Marie - Countess of Champagne and King Phillippe of France.
1 CONT NOTE
