0 @N6000000000374518290@ NOTE BIOGRAPHY: Princess Eleanor of England was the sixth child of Henry II ofEngland and his wife Eleanor of Aquitaine.
0 @N6000000002248195640@ NOTE Queen Eleanor of Aquataine personally escorted her daughter to Bordeux, where the evnoys of Alphonso VIII received the young Princess and led her off to a splendid marriage in Burgos.  Princess Eleanor, in addition to her bridal f
1 CONC urniture, took with her to Spain the old Authurian tales that so enthralled her mother's court.  Princess Eleanor also took with her to Castile the distinctive Angevin style of building.  at her request, the royal abbey of Las Hue
1 CONC lgas in Spain was founded in 1187.
1 CONT The nuns there were drawn from the highest of Spanish aristocracy.  The second abbess of the abbey was Princess Constance, one of Eleanor and Alphonso's daughters.  Las Huelgas was a royal mausoleum for Eleanor and Alphonso.  The 
1 CONC abbey reflects the influence of France, but one feature is purely Plantagenet--the domed vaulting with small additional compartments or ribs of the transept.  A Spanish troupador gives us a brief impression of the young Queen of C
1 CONC astile, Eleanor of England;
1 CONT
1 CONT And when the King had summoned to his court,
1 CONT Many a knight, rich baron, and jongleur,
1 CONT And the company had assembled, Then come Queen Leanore
1 CONT
1 CONT Modestly clad in a mantle of rich stuff,
1 CONT Red, with a silver border wrought with golden lions.
1 CONT She bows to the King and near him takes her seat.
1 CONT
1 CONT In her middle years, Queen Eleanor off Castile had grown into a gracious, pious, learned, wise, still-beautiful woman.  She was the patroness of prelates, grandees, and troupadors and head of one of the most civilized courts in Eu
1 CONC rope.
0 @N6000000009304996515@ NOTE #Générale#s:hg96.321
0 @N6000000000269706015@ NOTE Please find below a revised listing of the bastard children of King John.  The list has been augmented by many new references, corrections, and additions since my last posting of this listing.  The new listing includes Philip "Fiz
1 CONC  Le Rey" [i.e., Philip the king's son]) found in a 1263 Sussex fine as a possible bastard child of King John.
1 CONT
1 CONT Also, it appears there may be a link between Isabel, wife of Richard Fitz Ives, and another royal bastard, Eudes Fitz Roy.  I've learned that in 1245, Isabel's son and heir, William Fitz Ives, sued Giles de Chanceaux regarding pro
1 CONC perty in Cornwall.  This is surely the same man who was granted Eudes Fitz Roy's properties in Essex on his death without issue in 1242.  The Fitz Ives and Chanceaux link deserves further study.
1 CONT
1 CONT Comments are invited.
1 CONT
1 CONT Best always, Douglas Richardson, Salt Lake City, Utah
1 CONT
1 CONT - - - - - - - - - -
1 CONT
1 CONT REVISED LISTING OF BASTARD CHILDREN OF KING JOHN OF ENGLAND:
1 CONT
1 CONT Legitimated child of John of England, by a mistress, Clemence _____:
1 CONT
1 CONT i. JOAN OF ENGLAND, married LLYWELYN AP IORWERTH, Prince of North Wales [see WAKE 4].
1 CONT
1 CONT Illegitimate child of John of England, by a mistress, _____ de Warenne, daughter of Hamelin, 5th Earl of Surrey, by Isabel, daughter and heiress of William de Warenne, 3rd Earl of Surrey [see WARENNE 2]:
1 CONT
1 CONT i. RICHARD FITZ ROY (or DE WARENNE), Knt., Baron of Chilham, Kent, married ROSE DE DOVER [see ATHOLL 4].
1 CONT
1 CONT Illegitimate child of John of England, by a mistress, Hawise _____:
1 CONT
1 CONT i. OLIVER FITZ ROY.  He defended Wolvesey Castle for Peter des Roches, Bishop of Winchester, in 1216.  In 1217 he was granted the lands formerly held by Peter Fitz Herbert to sustain him in the king's service.  In 1218 he arrived 
1 CONC with other English knights at Damietta in the company of Papal legate, Pelayo.  He did not return.  H.R. Luard, Chronica Majora 3 (1876): 40-41.  C.P.R., 1216-1225 (1901), pp. 42-43,92.  S. Painter, Reign of King John (1949), pp. 
1 CONC 232-233.  NEHGR 119 (1965): 94-102.  J.M. Powell, Anatomy of a Crusade (1986), pg. 235.  N. Vincent, Peter des Roches (1996), pp. 71,175.
1 CONT
