1 CONT Possible illegitimate child of John of England, by an unknown mistress, _____:
1 CONT
1 CONT i. PHILIP FITZ ROY (possible child).  In 1263 he and his wife, Lavina, conveyed lands at Bignor, Pebmarsh, and Petworth, Surrey to Henry Fitz [le] Roy, perhaps their son.  L.F. Salzman, Abstract of Feet of Fines Relating to the Co
1 CONC unty of Sussex (Sussex Rec. Soc., vol. 7) (1908), pg. 45 ("Ph[illip]um Fiz Le Rey" [i.e., Philip the king's son]) (cf. W. Hudson, Three Earliest Subsidies for the County of Sussex in the Years 1296,1327,1332 (Sussex Rec. Soc., vol
1 CONC . 19) (1910), pp. 73,95 (references to "Henr[icus] fyz le Rey" on 1296 Sussex subsidy).
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is a list of eleven bastard children of King John of England which I've assembled from a variety of sources.  If anyone has any additions or corrections to this material, please let me know. The first two children li
1 CONC sted, Richard Fitz Roy (or de Warenne), Baron of Chilham, Kent, and Joan, wife of Llywelyn ap Iowerth, Prince of North Wales, have many modern descendants.
1 CONT
1 CONT Although sometimes questioned, I accept Isabel, wife of Richard Fitz Ives, Knt., as a bastard daughter of King John.  Isabel appears to have modern descendants.  Besides Isabel, I've also included a new bastard child for King John
1 CONC  not commonly found on such lists, namely Maud Fitz Roy, Abbess of Barking, whose identity is proven by the sources cited below.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sidney Painter, King John's historian, says of him: ".... cruel, lecherous, and deceitful."  Surely the number of illegitimate children listed below for King John proves the lecherous part.
1 CONT
1 CONT Best always, Douglas Richardson, Salt Lake City, Utah
1 CONT
1 CONT E-mail: royalancestry@msn.com
