1 CONT Upon his return to England, John found many of his barons in open defi ance.  The barons presented themselves to John with their greivances a t a conference in London on 6 January 1215. On 19 February 1215, John granted safe-condu
1 CONC ct to the northern barons to enable them to travel O xford for a conference with the primate of Canterbury Stephen Langton , his bishops and William 'the Protector' Marshall.  John put them of f until Easter.  John reacted camly t
1 CONC o the overt act of rebellion of t he barons in April of 1215.  Instead of taking steps to disperse the i nsurgents, John commissioned Archbishop Langton and William Marshall t o meet with them and discover their precise terms and 
1 CONC grievances.  On 3 May 1215, having failed to gain Johns surrender, the rebellious lord s formally renounced their homage and fealty to King John.  On 9/10 Ma y 1215, John issued proposals that the dispute with the barons be sett l
1 CONC ed by a committee of arbitration under the auspices of Pope Innocent .  When the rebels under Richard FitzWalter took London in May 1215, J ohn was at Freemantle, a royal hunting lodge in the woodlands of Wilts hire.  John attache
1 CONC d the Flemish troops under Robert de Bethune to tho se of William of Salisbury - his half brother.  by 31 May, John had mo ved to the area of Windsor on the Thames above London.  William of Sal isbury at the time was trying to res
1 CONC tore order in the West.  On 6 Jun e 1215, John ordered the mercenary Captain Fawkes of Breaute to reinfo rce the area of Salisbury.  On 10 June 1215, he saw his seal affixed t o the petitions of the barons and on the 15th of June 
1 CONC at Runnymede, Jo hn and rebel barons met.
