1 CONT John marched on Newark and then Doncaster where he arrived by 1 Januar y 1216.  He then marched on Pontefract in Yorkshire then held by John de Lacy who eventually submitted to John.  John then moved on to York which also submitte
1 CONC d.  John then moved towards Northallerton and beyon d that to the river Tees to Darlington.  On 8 January 1216 he moved ag ainst Durham and then towards Newcastle.  After King Alexander's force s made a stand at Berwick, on 15 Jan
1 CONC uary John forces took Berwick.  B y the end of January 1216, John had taken the Northumbrian castles of Mitford, Morpeth, Warkworth and Alnwick.  He made a raid into Scotlan d as far north as the Firth of Forth.  His northern camp
1 CONC aign had ende d brillantly.  He moved south via Lincoln, Stamford, Fortheringay and Bedford prior to reaching St. Albans by the end of February 1216.  On 12 March 1216, John siezed Roger Bigods castle at Framlingham in Susse x.  O
1 CONC n 28 March 1216, John took the castle of Robert de Vere at Heding ham in Essex.  John still was concerned about an invasion fleet from a cross the channel and worked to complete the muster of his fleet to pr event the expected inv
1 CONC asion.  In May and April of 1216, John moved bet ween the coastal stations of Sandwich, Dover, Folkestone and Romney an d established his headquarters at Canterbury.  In the third week of Ma y, a storm erupted off of the North sea
1 CONC  and John's fleet lay devastate d as a result.  John marched his assembled force from Sandwich soon af ter 22 May 1216, after learning of the landing of Louis, Prince of Fra nce.  Leaving Hubert de Burgh in command at Dover, John 
1 CONC withdrew west along the coast of Sussex, strengthening castles as he went arriving i n Winchester on 28 May 1216.  The loss of William of Salisbury, who al igned himself with Louis of France greatly staggered John's confidence .  
1 CONC By the time, Louis of France arrived at Winchester, John had withdr ew northwest to the area of Ludgershall, then to Devizes in Wiltshire .  In June and early July 1216, John was in Wiltshire and Dorset and v isited his family at 
1 CONC Corfe castle.  On 17 July 1261, John made his wa y north to the west midlands.  In July and August, he toured the Wels h Marches proceeding from Bristol to Gloucester, Tewkesbury, Hereford , Leominster and Shrewsbury.  He met with
1 CONC  Reginald de Briouse whom Joh n offered restoration of his families heritage.  John occupied Lincol n on 22 September 1216 and on 9 October 1216 arrived at Lynn.  Having marched north, John saw much of his army and train engulfed 
1 CONC while tryi ng to cross the Glen river near the Wash.  Eventually, John was moved by litter from Swineshead to Sledford and finally to Newark and the ca stle of the Bishop of Lincoln.  He died 19 October 1216.  The issue o f the Ba
1 CONC rons rebellion was still in doubt when John died." (Encycloped ia Americana, Vol. XVI, p.156).
