0 @N6000000002187746324@ NOTE Waleran IV of Limburg
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Waleran IV or Walram IV (d.1279) was the duke of Limburg from 1247 to his death. He was the son and successor of Henry IV. and Ermengarde, countess of Berg.
1 CONT
1 CONT He played a great part in the politics of the Great Interregnum in Germany. He left the Hohenstaufen fold and supported William I of Holland as king. He was sent in an embassy to Henry III of England and after William's death, sup
1 CONC ported Henry's brother Richard, Earl of Cornwall, as king. In 1272, he was party to the nobles who offerred the crown to Ottokar II of Bohemia and then Rudolph of Hapsburg.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1252, he intervened in the War of the Succession of Flanders and Hainault, at the side of Jean d'Avesnes. After 1258, it fell out with John I of Brabant, putting an end to sixty years of good relations with the dukes of Brabant
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Waleran frequently intervened in the business of the archbishop of Cologne in his constant fight with the bourgeoisie of the city.
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Henry IV Duke of Limburg
1 CONT 1247–1279 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ermesinda 
0 @N6000000002188021150@ NOTE Barnim II, Duke of Pomerania
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Barnim II (circa 1277–28 May 1295) was Duke of Pomerania.
1 CONT
1 CONT Son of Duke Barnim I and his third wife, Mechtild of Brandenburg, Barnim's father died in 1278 when he was still a child. He was titular co-ruler with younger brother Otto I and his much older half-brother Bogislaw IV, in whose ha
1 CONC nds effective power lay. Protected by his mother, Barnim had no real power until 1294 when he and Otto gained a shared in ruling Pomerania.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was killed the following year, in the Ueckermünder Heide, by an irate husband whose wife he had seduced. He left no children and Pomerania was ruled by Bogislaw and Otto after his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT   This biography of a European noble is a stub. You can help Wikipedia by expanding it. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit] Reference
1 CONT (German) Werner Buchholz (ed.), Deutsche Geschichte im Osten Europas. Pommern. Siedler Verlag, Berlin 1999, ISBN 3-88680-272-8 
